      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0005" n="art0005">
        <head>Tettas to the (fbitor.</head>
        <p>Tettas to the (fbitor.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>Tag EDITOR of "The Examiner" invites COM LDTTERS must in all cases be accompanied by the spondence on all subjects of public interest, name and address of the writer. Lsrrme should be brief and pointed. Their COMMIINICATIONS received early in the week— object must be to impart or seek information, except urgent matters—can always be given not abuse, the best attention.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0006" n="art0006">
        <head>ERNEST BROOKES.</head>
        <p>ERNEST BROOKES.</p>
        <p>Sir,—As an ardent supporter of the Warring- ton Rugby Football Club, and one who is a great admirer of our local players, I think the time has arrived when the question of a benefit to that loyal and whole-hearted player, Ernest Brookes, should be considered. He has, I under- stand, been playing with our premier team for 12 years, during which period he has gained both County and International honours. He has also played in three Northern Union Cup finals for our team. If the members would give their support, I feel sure the committee would be glad to recognise what a grand asset he has been to our olub.</p>
        <p>ADMIRER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>LATE 'CARDINAL NEWMAN AND THE BIBLE.</head>
        <p>THE LATE 'CARDINAL NEWMAN AND THE BIBLE.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Perhaps an extract from the writings of the above English Cardinal may be interesting to some of your readers. Ile says:— . .</p>
        <p>--``iVho will not say that the uncommon beauty and marvellous English (i.e., of the Authorised Version, not the Revised) is one of the strong- holds of the Protestant heresy in this country? It lives in the ear like music that can never be forgotten, like the sound of the church bells, which the convert hardly knows how he can forego. .</p>
        <p>"Its felicities often seem to he almost things rather than mere words. It is a part of the national mind, and the author of national seriousness. The memory of the dead passes into it. The potent traditions of childhood are stereotyped in its verses. The power of all the griefs and trials of man is hidden beneath its words. It is the representative of his best moments, and all that he has been, and there is about him of soft and gentle and pure and patient and good speaks to him for ever out of his English Bible. "It is his sacred Thing, which doubt has never dimmed and controversy never soiled. In the breadth and length of the land there is not a Protestant with one spark of religiousness about him, whose spiritual biography is not in his Saxon Bible."</p>
        <p>The simple candour of Newman contrasts much with that of his ambitious co-pervert Manning, a very different type of man, to whom Newman had a peculiar antipathy. In one of his letters to Manning he says: "When I am writing to you I hardly know whether I am standing upon nay head or my heels." But, alas! both of them fell under the great delusion sent by God upon those who "receive not the love of truth that they might be saved" (11. Thess. One of the essential differences between Chris- tianity and Popery is that while the former makes Christ the centre and foundation of our hope (John iii. 16), the latter makes the so-called '</p>
        <p>" Church" such—hence the fatal mistake. That system puts the Church in the place of Christ, and the Sacraments in the place of the Gospel. M. A. (CANTAB.).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>PEACE DAY. '</head>
        <p>PEACE DAY. '</p>
        <p>Sir,—l venture to suggest, through the medium, of your journal, the observance of Peace Day in all day schools in Warrington and district.</p>
        <p>May 18th has now been observed in this way for a number of years, as being the date on which the first Peace Conference of the Sover- eign State; of the world met in 1899, on the initiative of the Czar, and on the invitation of the Queen of Holland. The observance has ob- tained more generally in the United States, France, Italy, Hungary, Belgium, Sweden, and, to some extent, in England. Our suggestion should, I think, find ready acceptance with Education Committees, school managers, head teachers and their staffs, the proposed observ- ance being: 1. Entirely non-political. 2. Calculated to create a much needed wider human sympathy, and what Lord Haldane aptly terms "higher nationality." The schools of the nation are the training centres into which are gathered the citizens of the future in their formative years.</p>
        <p>3. A means of procuring for the schools generally, in, as we hope, the near future, much more generous help than has been afforded hitherto by the State. The amount spent on education in this country is absurd and alto- gether incompatible with the vast extent and tremendous importance of the work which should be done, and the results which should be achieved.</p>
        <p>_ "The Charlottenberg Object Lesson" cannot be brought within measurable distance of Eng- lish elementary schools on the existing educa- tion pittance. If we spend, as we are doing, one million Pounds' a week on naval armaments alone, the amount spent On our schools should bear, at least, some reasonable proportion to this huge SLIM It is not by armaments of any kind, naval or military, that the future is formed and deter- mined, hut in the schools of to-day, "the work- shops of humanity." It has been well and truly said by a high authority, "We should put into the schools of to-day whatever we would have in the nation of to-morrow." The programme proposel for the observance is very simple, consisting of well-known popular school songs and recitations, a hymn or two, an address (if desired), and the National Anthem. I earnestly hope the suggestion now made may be.favourably considered and followed. A. M. MITCHELL. Burtonwood Vicarage.</p>
        <p>CLARKE'S 841 PILLS can be relied noon to care in either Rex all acquired or constitutional dis- charges from thee Urinary Organs, Gravel and Pains in the back. Free from Mercury. Established up• wards of 50 years. In boxes 4s. 6cl. each, of all chemists and Patent Medicine Vendors throughout the world or sent for sixty stamps by the makers.— The Lincoln and Midland Counties Drug Co., Lin- eoln.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>TRADE UNIONISTS AND TARIFF REFORM.</head>
        <p>TRADE UNIONISTS AND TARIFF REFORM.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Your issue of April 11th contains a re- port of the Trades Council meet-ing, in which Mr. Grierley, referring to the resolutions passed by trade unionists belonging to the Tariff Reform League, is reported by you to have said "that a statement in the letter (from the Tariff Reform League) about the conference of 81 trade unions was a tissue of falsehoods. The men who were supposed to be at the conference had been seen, and they stated they knew nothing about it," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>I cannot allow you to ,publish such a state- ment as this without officially informing you that every word of it is untrue. The circulars and letters issued by the League in connection with its conference are accurate and provable in every particular, and no claim has been made which cannot be fully substantiated. It is pro- bably because Mr. Grierley is fully aware of this that he uttered his untruths in order to preju- dice his fellow-members against accepting the offer of the League to send a speaker to the Trades Council meeting.</p>
        <p>Mr. Grierley and his friends know quite well that they cannot defend the policy of Free Trade to which the Labour party is pledged, involving as it does free competition with British workers from black, yellow, and non-union labour throughout the rest of the world. That is why Mr. Grierley prefers to rely upon a falsehood and bluff rather than attempt to defend his posi- tion against a trade unionist member of the. League at one of his own Trades Council meet- ings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>E. ASHTON BAGLEY. Tariff Reform League, Manchester.</head>
        <p>E. ASHTON BAGLEY. Tariff Reform League, Manchester.</p>
        <p>Mr. Grierley, seen by "The Examiner repre- sentative, •expressed his surprise at the above letter. His remarks, he said, were based upon statements in the "Daily News," and he had seen nothing to repudiate them. With regard ' to "relying upon falsehood and bluff" to oppose Tariff Reform, he remarked that it is not needed, because the truth is quite suffi- cient. We ha4e ourselves exposed the method of the Tariff Reform League. They hold a con- ference at which some Tory Trade Unionists are present, and pass resolutions which are represented as "the real voice of Trade Union- ism." The trade unions involved vigorously repudiated the right of self-selected persons to speak for the trade Unions. ,As we said at the time, it is the same old device—like the sending of "Liberal" deputations to Ireland by Tories.—Enrroa.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>"THE CHARM OF WOMAN."</head>
        <p>"THE CHARM OF WOMAN."</p>
        <p>Sir,—Yes, it sounds very nice, and from a man's point of view I suppose it is very nice, but, not being a man, I can't say. But what about the woman's view of the points mentioned under the above heading in last week's .-Exam- iner"?</p>
        <p>Why should a woman be so patient that she puts up with bores merely because they are ' men? Is there no charm in being sincere, and if we expect to have to put up with bores, do we encourage men to become anything else? And if, at the writer says, woman is such a manager of men (which I do not believe, the art of management being purely temperamental), why does she not manage them so that they will not bore her? The writer puts woman on a pedestal and endows her with great wisdom in.many things, evidently for the purpose of concealing it from the world in general an men in particular. Surely if there is wisdom in this world it should be proclaimed from the housetops, even if it has to be acknowledged that it came from the lips of a woman. - The writer suggests that women are so tactful that they never let you see that you are merely making a fool of yourself. For so clever a person do you think that it will be very difficult for her to conceal from the ordinary male that some time during the last five years she had rtorded her vote as the recognition of her responsibility in her country's welfare? Was not the teaching "It is better to give than to receive" intended for women as well as men? and I venture to say that they have learnt the lesson equally well. If there is any woman or Man who still prefers the life of a parasite, i.e., a consumer who is not a producer, surely the world were better without them. Many women are forced against their wills to be para- sites because of the old-fashioned customs to Which so many men still cling. Like the writer of the "Old Maid's Diary," the writer of "The Charm of Woman" would do well to study the product of his own pen, since the highest praise he can give to a man is to liken him to a woman. Oh! men of little faith, how long will you refuse freedom to women? Not, mark you, the freedomrnut men think that women ought to have, b means the responsibility, good the freedom that women desire, and which ea ,ofanadll.whAinchd Ithaeyskwmoueu lduse so well forespo ity who have r • sisters received kindnesses from mothers, wives, .s and daughters—Do you feel beasts and do your womenkind wish you to feel so. 2 Do you not rather feel inspired and strengthened for your work in a w miss? ay th at you would quickly</p>
        <p>A man who feels himself a beast will behave like a beast, and if the effect of the enfranchise- ment of the women of Finland has been to cause the young men to lose their sense of chivalry, which I beg to doubt, then the women are well rid of that kind of chivalry, which rests on such a slight foundation: Chivalry is the protection of weakness by strength, is irrespective of sex, and must be founded on justice. If not, then give us justice. WOMAN SUFFRAGIST.</p>
      </div>
