      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 25, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 25, 1914.</p>
        <p>tettas to the Oita</p>
        <p>CATHOLIC LECTURES.</p>
        <p>an indebted to "Phil Squod " for his appreciation of the Protestant work which is being done in Emmanuel Reformed Episcopal Church. That our efforts for the maintenance of simple liturgical services are favourably re- ceived is evidenced by the fact that at. our Easter vestry meeting the churchwardens were able to submit a financial statement which has not been equalled for twenty years. Many Evangelical Anglicans give us monetary support from time TO time, and not a few, driven from their own parish churches through sacerdotal innovations, join us in the Sacrament of the Lord's Supper. As reference has been made to the work of my father in Spain, it may interest friends to Protestantism to know that he has laboured in many Continental countries for over 25 years, and has suffered many indignities at the hands of Roman Catholics, especially in Spain and Italy. The knowledge of what the father has suffered for the Truth's sake has made the son an aggressive l'rotestant.</p>
        <p>As to the lectures on the Controversy with Rome, from the Protestant standpoint, the idea is a good one, and I would suggest that,the;Free Church Ministers consider the advisability of arranging a ,course of lectures to be given during next winter. I proffer this suggestion in all Christian charity, witt the hope that knowledge may be spread in the interests of true Religion, and of the Catholic Church of which I am a member. In closing, may I remark that with all due respect to the editorial chair the title given to this correspondence is incomplete and mislead- ing? The title should be " The Roman Catholic Lectures." WILLIAM T. JAYE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>Emmanuel Reformed Episcopal Church, Bold-street, Warrington.</head>
        <p>Emmanuel Reformed Episcopal Church, Bold-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln their letters to you, " Anxious" and "Phil Squod" suggest a series of Protestant Evi- dence -Lectures. It is very evident that "Anxious" was impressed by the Catholic Evi- dence lectures, as' he states that he was astounded at the simplicity of some of the most vital dogmas. He must have found the Catholic religion to be far otherwise than he understood it, and being roused to the progress which that religion is making in this country, desires lec- tures on Protestantism as a counter-blast.</p>
        <p>Now, as Protestantism is absolutely contrary to Catholicity in most doctrines, it follows that both religious systems cannot be right, that both do not teach the truth; and that God being what He is, the God of Infinite Truth, He is only honoured and worshipped in an acceptable man- ner in tfiat religion which is right, whichever that may be.</p>
        <p>„ It is of the greatest importance to have the true religion, and it is the duty of every man to seek it and embrace it when he finds it, for, until he does, a man in doubt will never have peace and happiness. And a very important fact must be borne well in mind, viz., that if the Catholic Church is not the true one, that which is must give better proofs. Now, lectures intending to demonstrate the truth of Protestantism must be of the positive order if they are to be of any value. The line usually taken by non-Catholic lecturers is that of the alleged errors of Rome, and abusing the Catholic Church generally. But such lectures are of no use whatever as proving the truth of Protestantism -</p>
        <p>To be of any consequence, it will be necessary that the substance of the lectures should--:.</p>
        <p>(1) State plainly what Protestantism really is. (2) Show what it really and definitely teaches. (3) Give proofs of its having a Divine Com- mission, to teach. (4) Prove that Protestantism fulfils Christ's command to " Teach all nations," if it can be proved at all that it does teach. Such lectures would be highly interesting, and as "Anxious" truly says, "If Protestantism is to remain, something of this kind is needed." J. RICHARDSON. 86, Buttermarket-street.</p>
        <p>Sir,—lt is. quite evident that your correspon- dent, Mr. H. Ogden, has had quite enough of this religious disCussion, his dearth of facts com- pelling him to revert to the old argument, viz., "Spiritual indwellings as the Church of Christ." I understand the reverend gentleman has many duties to perform, clerical, social, . .There- fore, recognising the hard work and worry which attaches itself to these duties, I will, in this, my last, letter, let him achieve what must be his ambition, that of having the "last saY." But, sir, I contend that he lui,s not shown me— a mere layman—the folly of my Christian be- liefs, nor has he convinced me that he is com- petent enough to enter into controversy with any other than mere laymen. Now, Mr. Ogden says that the cardinal canon and foundation of his faith is "Where two or more shall be gathered together in My name . . and there to bless." Yes, granted the are gathered together in His name. But what is His name? Jesus Christ— God—the Eternal Word. "And the Word was made flesh and dwelt amongst us." "For this was I born, and for this I came into the world. that:, I should give testimony of the truth." (John viii. 37). "And I shall be with you all days, even to the consummation of the world." Not as a spirit, but as a living body. Mr. Ogden goes on to say that "it is not the corporeal , body of Christ which gives power to the spirit but that spiritual sustenance is given to men by the spiritual indwelling of Christ,"., and quotes: "What is of the flesh is flesh, what is of the spirit is spirit," He might have added :1 "it is the spirit that quickeneth; the flesh pro- ' fiteth nothing." Now what does he mean? Does he only take the words in the spiritual sense? If in the texts quoted above "the spirit" means the figurative interpretation of Christ's words, then "the flesh." being in antithesis, must mean the literal interpretation of them. But who- ever heard before of such a meaning being attached to those two words? In Scripture, at any rate, there is not a simple example of it. If, on the other hand, "the flesh" means simply "the flesh," then. "the spirit" must mean simply "the spirit." If one is to be taken literally, so must the other also, and figurative meaning vanishes altogether.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ogden also says that I have not disposed of his argument regarding Communion in the Early Church. Now, if his eyesight has not deceived him, he must surely have read in my last letter that this doctrine was certainly taught by the fathers of the Early Church At. Cyril making the doctrine quite clear. Not only St. Cyril, but the early Popes used the very same words "Hoc est corpus meuxn." Even if in the Acts of the Apostles there is no mention of the word Transubstantiation, it is quite clear to any- one who can reason that the Apostles, when administering the bread and wine, did so as the Body and Blood of their Divine Master. When St. Cyril uttered the words, What seems to be bread is not bread, but the body of Christ; and what seems to be wine is not wine, but the Blood of Christ," he did not say "This is Transub- stantiation," but the very words themselves tell us that it was, and is, Transubstantiation. So also with our Divine Lord's words, "This is My Body; this is My Blood." But perhaps Mr. Ogden does not understand the Catholic teaching regarding terms or phrases. Now, the Catholic Church, as the true Church, must formulate expressions, to make clear all that is obscure in the revelation of which she has charge. Objectively, then, the Catholic Faith means this: All that is contained in the revelation made by Jesus Christ, as it is expounded or shall be hereafter expounded by that one True Church to whose keeping lie com- mitted it. It is contained in a series of defini, tions.ranging from the existence of the Deity to Papal Infallibility. As the years roll by it may be more fully developed by further definitions, for the human intelligence is for ever revolving spiritual problems, and the potentialities of revelation are inexhaustible. Such has been the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>PHILOMATHS DINE,</head>
        <p>PHILOMATHS DINE,</p>
        <p>Suggestions for the Improvement of</p>
        <p>the Society.</p>
        <p>DR, JOSEPH'S VIEWS.</p>
        <p>Warrington Philomaths discarded discussion, and were not eager to jump to their feet and pull a fellow member's views to shreds, when they met .at the Patten Arms Hotel on Wednes- day night, for the occasion was that of the fifth annual dinner.</p>
        <p>The President, Mr. J. Walsh, presided over the subsequent proceedings, which were con- tinued until near midnight.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>PAST AND PRESENT.</head>
        <p>PAST AND PRESENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. H. TICKELL, in a humorous speech, in which he gave several extracts from Alderman Bennett's "Dream of a Warringtonian," pro- posed the toast, " The County Borough of War-</p>
        <p>rington." Dr. JOSEPH, in reply, said that Mr. Tickell in his humorous speech had mainly alluded to the past and left alone the present, with which he (Dr. Joseph) and his colleagues on the Council were mainly concerned. His knowledge of IWarrington went back for 40 years, and during the time he had the; town under his paternal care he had always found a certain number of leading citizens who had always been intent to do their best to further the interests of the town. (Hear, hear.) However, he could not help thinking that they were not making that progress which they ought to do, but he thought the main reason why they did not pro- gress was common to all municipalities. They were administrative bodies, and had no legis- lative power; indeed, their administrative power was limited, and that would continue so long as they were tied down. Municipalities were con- trolled by one common leash, under which there was no room for any individual ambition on the part of any municipality, for the least compe- tent and most lazy got oil as well and probably better than other anxious for the progress and development of their respective areas. The kind of home rule which was needed was muni- cipal home rule. (Hear, hear.) They wanted to have some influence in framing their own laws. (Hear, hear.) Surely, as in their case, after they had been incorporated since 1847, they had experience enough to know something about municipal government—even more than the President of the Local Government Board, who was appointed for, perhaps, a few-months. They should have a kind of Municipal Parlia- ment in different parts of the country, one in Lancashire and crilfe in Yorkshire, and so on, to which each municipality should send, a trusted representative to give the views of his town or' district. He threw out this suggestion, so that the society could debate the subject at one of their meetings.</p>
        <p>"THE GUESTS." .The Rev. Dr. STANLEY Mmon, proposing the toast of the guests, humorously remarked that if the Principal guest at the next annual dinner failed to put in an appearance, it could then be regarded as a habit, for that would make a trio of such misfortunes. That was probably the last occasion on which the speaker would appear there as a member. Amongst all the things that he had found most interesting in War- rington, the Philomathic Society was very, near to the first place. (Hear, hear.) He said that not to flatter the society, for if he felt inclined to criticise he would do so. His only regret was that the society had not been better attended, for a society like that ought to have a large membership. He presumed there were numbers of intellectual young men in Warrington, and they could not do better than join and attend the society's meetings. (Hear, hear.) He did commend to the members of the society that in the future they should endeavour to make their society a feeding ground for the Town Council, for nothing could be better for the Town Coun- cil than that a few memers of the society should join it. The socieo should hold joint meetings with the Literary' and Philosophical Society, and so draw them closer together. ,</p>
        <p>THE COMBINING IDEA. Mr. LOFTHOUSE, of the Literary and Philo- sophical society, in reply, said there was some- thing in the idea that the members of the two societies might be merged into one. It was de- sirable that societies like Philomaths should take up the subjects they d4l, for they lived in an age when everything and everybody has to justify its existence; it was not a time when any theory or belief could exist, because it,had always been so, and he admired the men who examined things fearlessly and faithfully. How- ever, when they had examined a thing and come to a conclusion about it, they should not think they had got the truth for all time, but a step- ping-stone to the ultimate truth. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>THE SOCIETY PROPOSED. Dr. J. S. MANsos proposed the toast of the " Philomattic Society,' and said he did not think that matters with which the society dealt were held in anything like the respect which I sport— England's greatest industry —could claim. He referred to the equipment of raced courses, football grounds, and cricket pitches, &amp;c., and to the space which those subjects held lin the Press. There was no doubt that sport was the dominant idea in the country. Suppos- ing that society dropped its objects, and next year commenced as a bowling club, they would soon have a strong membership, and have a I splendid (Hear, hear, and laugh- equiPraent ter.) He had wondered if it would always be • So, and he was inclined to be rather hopeful and . think that the indifference to intellectual. mat- ters would be different by-and-bye. He was not a kill-joy by any means, but he thought that sport was sometimes gaining an influence which they should not have. He could not see why that and kindred societies could not have proper equipment for carrying on their work. They should certainly have a suitable hall and com- mittee rooms. (Hear, hear.) But how were they to get them? If he were a Carnegie he would present a hall to the town, but that being impossible, be thought the best thing was for such societies as the Field Club, Photographic. Literary and Philosophical, their own, and kindred societies, to amalgamate for the pur- pose, and they could have joint meetings on topics of general interest, and at the end of the session wind up with a good dinner. Mr. T. E. KNowiEs, the secretary, responded, stating that the society aimed at culture.</p>
        <p>"THE PRESIDENT." Mr. D. J. DuNx proposed the toast of the President, who replied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>CATHOLIC LECTURES—continued.</head>
        <p>CATHOLIC LECTURES—continued.</p>
        <p>method of the Catholic Church, from the begin- ning, such it will be to the end. Of course, we Catholics may argue and argue; we may use the most trenchant and convincing logic; we may put the proofs of Christianity and Catholicism so clearly before those outside the Church that it would seem as if they could not help acknow- ledging their claims; but as long as in theiil souls there remain those evil dispositions which resist preventing grace, we will avail nothing. There- fore it is that prayer is far more efficacious in obtaining the True Faith than all the argument that Was ever framed. In conclusion, I would refer 11r. Ogden to the latter part of his last letter, wherein he says that I am doing my Church no service, and that I am not, convincing the Warrington public. If I can do my Church no service I can do her no harm, because she is divine. But, sir, if he wishes to convince the public, let him accept the suggestion made by the correspondent "Anxious," seconded by his late ally, "Phil Squod," to which I readily append my signa- ture. If he does agree to hold these Evidence Lectures, I also wish to remind him that a question box is necessary, and, as a suitable poster, the following would not be bad : " Baptist Evidence Lectures. Difficulties Ex- plained to Roman Catholics, B.:c. Questions Invited._ All Seats Free."</p>
        <p>J. D. Warrington, April 22nd, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>NO CONSCRIPTS.</head>
        <p>NO CONSCRIPTS.</p>
        <p>Conscription Alien to British</p>
        <p>Traditions.</p>
        <p>SERIES OF PEACE MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>Much has been said about the pernicious sys- tem of conscription being adopted in this country, and this week there have been a series of interesting talkse against such a procedure. Mr. H. E. Brown, of Manchester, has aroused considerable enthusiasm by his eloquent ad- dresses on such subjects as "Conscription" and, "Trade and Empire," on the Bank Quay Green, on Tuesday and Wednesday evenings, and on Thursday evening he was equally at home in 'dealing with the subject "War and the Workers." Last night he gave an address on "Britain's Burden of Force." On Tuesday evening Mr. Haley (treasurer of the Warrington Trades Council) presided, and, in the course of his address, Mr. Brown said that conscription was unnecessary. When the Agitation for National and Military Service was started some years ago the reason advanced for its necessity was an expected German invasion. The Germans had no more chance of successfully invading England than they had of invading the moon. We had to send 450,000 men to South •Africa, and it,took nearly three years:to conquer a territory with a population about the same as the population of Salford. Other countries would probably have had the same difficulties to contend with had they, instead of us, fought the Boers. Yet we were told that the Germans were going to land a force of 70,000 or 240,000, and destroy the Empire. Conscription was bad kit a nation, for it deprived men of liberty of con- science on a most important point—that of his right of refusing to be trained to take human life.</p>
        <p>It was alien to all British traditions, and un- popular wherever in force. It involved a pass- port system and police supervision. It would drive young men out of the country. The work- ing men of this country would find it a blood tax as well as a money tax. In Australia the Act was unpopular, over 20,000 prosecutions being recorded. Young boys of 14 had been sen- tenced to gaol with hard labour; some had been detained in barracks or forts. The boys ob- jected to serving in the Army on moral and religious grounds. Over 50,000 people had formed Freedom Leagues in Australia to try and break up the Act that was passed without any mandate from the people. There was no elec- tion. _</p>
        <p>--Mr. Brown concluded his address by appealing to patriotic Englishmen to do their duty; to perform real national service; to fight Eng- land's greatest enemies—misery, poverty, ignor- ance and wretchedness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>TRADE AND EMPIRE.</head>
        <p>TRADE AND EMPIRE.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday evening, when Mr. Faulkner presided, Mr. Brown opened his address with a reference to the jingo feeling now prevailing in the United States of America. Such a feeling might not create war, but it made war possible. We were often told by our Imperialist friends that we required a big Navy to defend our Colonies. Our very existence depended upon our Colonies, and Gerinany, who wanted her place in the sun, was looking to our Colonies for new markets. In other words, Germany wanted to capture our Colonies. Mr. Brown then said that the Germans were able to trade with our Colonies on the same terms that we were. The Colonials were prepared to buy German goods, if , the price and value were all right, in the same way that they bought ,English goods The Germans did a big trade with our Colonies People talked about ownership of Colonies, in the same way that they talked of the ownership of property. Countries like Canada and Australia were independent nations. They managed their own affairs. The Germans had built up a trade without a big Navy, and without an Empire. England was Germany's best colony; England was Germany's best customer. , A war between the two countries would be as senseless as a war between Lancashire and Yorkshire, because their interests were inter-dependent. Trade was not carried on by fighting, but by co-operation. Co- operation was the law of life, not struggle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>"WAR AND THE WORKERS."</head>
        <p>"WAR AND THE WORKERS."</p>
        <p>Mr. M. Llewellyn presided over the meeting on Thursday. - - -</p>
        <p>In the course of his address Mr. Brown said they were often told that workmen did not want wars, that they were in favour of peace. That .was .certainly correct during normal times, but let there be a war scare and they found 'a big section of the working classes ready to shout and cheer in support of war in the same way that they did before the South African War. Wax hit the workers the hardest. It meant that there was a great possibility of some near relative losing his life, of widows and orphans being created. During the time of war little talk was heard of social reform. After war there was a great trade depression, which meant unemploy- ment. The war had to be paid for, and that meant more taxation. When money was re- quired for necessary social reform they could not get it because it was required for Dread- noughts. The South African War did not bene- fit the working classes of South Africa; it cer- tainly did not benefit the working classes of this country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>WHEN GIRLS GROW WEAK.</head>
        <p>WHEN GIRLS GROW WEAK.</p>
        <p>When girls grow deathly pale, weak and miserable, then is the time for parents to take prompt steps, for delay means danger. The girl in her " teens " cannot develop into robust. happy womanhood, without abundant red, rich blood in her veins, and it is lack of new, good_ blood that is the great trouble with nine girls out, of ten. They grow anemic (bloodless), de- pressed, delicate and thin, with no appetite, no strength and no interest in life. In Dr. Williams' Pink Pills there is splendid, vigorous health, with glowing cheeks and spark- ling eyes for every unhappy, fragile girl who is struggling on to womanhood in a wretched state of health.</p>
        <p>There is no medicine that can compare with Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for giving strength and dispelling disease. These Pills have a special beneficial influence on the blood, making it rich, red and pure, so that it is able to carry on its work of refreshing the tired body, the over- worked nerves an/.l the flagging appetite. This is why thousands of women, now robust and attractive, are recommending Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, to their .suffering sex. Begin to-day to dispel your Anfemia, your headaches, your indigestion and feeling of weak- ness by starting a course of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. Sold by all dealers, or .direct from Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., 46, Holborn-viaduct, London, 2s. 9d. a box or 13s. 9d. for six boxes, post free.</p>
        <p>. _ FREE.--Send a postcard to Book Dept., 46, Holborn-viaduct, London, for a handy treatise on The Blood and Its Work, sent free.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>TONS OF HONEY.</head>
        <p>TONS OF HONEY.</p>
        <p>The Secretary of the Port Arthur (Ontario) Board of Trade sends to the illustrated journal "Canada" an interesting account of the pro- duction of honey by bees in Mr. Munro's apiary near Port Arthur.</p>
        <p>, Six and -p-t-I;if.tons of pure extracted honey have been gathered by these bees, the sale of which has enabled the farmer to pay for his 500-acre farm and buildings in five years instead of ten. The farmer estimates that 40,000,0001 b. of honey has gone to waste through non-cultiva- tion of the bee business; that over six million dollars have been lost to the Port Arthur Slate Valley districts through the non-cultivation of honey producers of tbe common flowers of tlPe field in that vicinity. "There are sermons in stones all right," said Mr. Munro, "hut there is a sermon in the work of the bees. The rose is the sweetest smelling flower, but the bees will not touch it, as it gives them no nectar. The richest honey comes from the ragweed, that noxious weed which is con- demned .and trodden under foot as worthless. The common dandelion produces most luscious honey, and the raspberry, which flourishes so abundantly here, gives honey of an ,excellent quality. Only want of following up thi indus- try prevents millions of pounds of honey being marketed from this district vtnnually, for rasp- berries grow here in profusiOn."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>GOOD CITIZENS.</head>
        <p>GOOD CITIZENS.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. E. Parker's Tribute to the Wesleyans.</p>
        <p>BEWSEY-ROAD SALE OF WORK.</p>
        <p>The annual two-days' sale of work in connec- tion with Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church was held in the Lecture Hall, Bewsey-road, on Wednesday and Thursday. The stalls ,were tastefully laid out to attract those who were to contribute to the ~t 250 which was anticipated. The Mayoress performed the opening cere- mony on Wednesday, Mr. C. E. Parker presid- ing over a large attendance. The Mayor, the Rev. W. A. Beckett (pastor), the Rev. H. Wil- liamson, Alderman Tinnion, Mr. J. Upson, and Mr. J. W. Hayward were amongst those present. _ _ _</p>
        <p>In making his statement the Rev. W. A. BEciarr said that those who knew the history of Bewsey-road were aware that they could not, from the ordinary income of the church, meet the requirements for the upkeep and; the various expenses associated with the church, and they were compelled to have a sale of work year by year. They were in a more difficult position this year than for some years past, owing to the fact that they did not hold a,sale on•behalf of their Own church last year, but Made way in the interests of Norris-street bazaar. They set out now to raise £250. In addition to that they could do with .2200 or -t3OO to put the building in a proper state of repair, but he was afraid they would have to wait for that. They had been remembered by their old friends, and sub- scriptions had been received from Capt. Savage £5O, Mr. J. W. Hayward ...t.12 128., Mrs. Andrews .210, Mr. William Bennett £5, Mr. John Ben- nett .25. (Applause.) They wanted to wipe off the debt from the trust account and provide a nucleus for the renovation scheme. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>VALUABLE CONSTITUENTS.</head>
        <p>VALUABLE CONSTITUENTS.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said that in connection with bazaars he felt himself in the' wrong place. If it were a question of 'business, politics, or pos- sibly of religion, be could feel he .was on his native heath, but, being connected with the opening of a bazaar, he did not know where he was or what to talk about. He realised it would be dangerous for him to make criticisms on such matters as drawn-thread work, hem- stitching herring-boning, &amp;c., for he might get into difficulties by doing so. He was ,very glad they had the Mayor and Mayoress associated with them. (Hear, hear.) He thought the Mayor would recognise that as far as Wesleyans and all Christian communities were concerned, they were the most valuable constituents in the borough over which he so worthily presided. (Hear, hear.) He did not think Christian com- munities gave the police or the poor rates a great deal of trouble or required special visits from the sanitary authorities. He thought in all those things the principles of the Christian faith could but make for good citizenship. (Hear, hear.) He was sure the Mayor would recognise that whatever effort he or his Council might make which would go towards the better- ment of the social, moral or physical condi- tions, he had in the Christian communities those who were always willing and ready to take their part in such work. (Applause.) While they might say from that point of view that they had a special claim on the Mayor and Mayoress, in such matters as that, he was sure the Mayor gladly acknowledged the value, apart from the higher spiritual teachings and truths for which the church stood, that from a citizen point of view Christian churches were a valu- able and useful asset to the town. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>MAYORESS'S TRIBUTE.</head>
        <p>THE MAYORESS'S TRIBUTE.</p>
        <p>The Mittoacs F3lid: I have special pleasure in coming here to-day, as in 'my early days I at- tended here , and also the Sunday school in Silver-street. Sales of Work, I think, are•neces- sary to enable the good work to be carried, on efficiently, and I hope your efforts will be abundantly rewarded. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>, . Alderman TINNION proposed a vote of thanks to the Mayor and Mayoress and the Chairman. The Mayoress, he said, was always willing to help any good work without distinction of sect. Her whole object had been to further what, in her opinion, was beneficial, to the best interests of the town. Many—of them who gave part of their time for the benefit of the town wondered how he Mayor and Mayoress managed to give the whole of their time for every good object and. at the, same time it had also been a matter of sincere pleasure and appreciation. (Ap- plause.) In regard to the Chairman he might say that there was no man in Warrington, either on the platform or in the Press, who had stood so fearlessly for what he judged to be the highest ideals and in the truest 'interests of Warrington. And no man had carried the banner of progress in that determined manner so ably as Mr. Parker had done, and no one had done it with greater admiration from his friends. (Applause.) Mr. J. W. IlivwAien seconded, and said they felt they had some proprietary interest in the Mayoress there. (Hear, hear.) He recalled the graceful ceremony in presenting to the school, where she was associated in early life, her por- trait. In Silver-street they realised that all the children would come to be honoured, and in 1914 they had a Mayoress who formerly did be- long to Silver-street. The Mayor and Mayoress had set a noble example in giving their whole time in the service of the. town. (Applause.) •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>SOME DONATIONS.</head>
        <p>SOME DONATIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. UPSON supported, and said he had re- ceived £lO from Mr. Parker, „Z 5 Se. from the Mayor, and £1 Is. from Canada. He also ex- pressed his appreciation of the services ren- dered to the town by the Mayor and Mayoress and Mr. Parker. tThe Rev. W. A. BECKETT endorsed all that had een said. The MA-row-said he, was always willing to do all he could for the benefit of the town, and par- ticularly that church. He used to attend the Sunday school there. (Applause.) •- He 014, regretted he was not born a brother of frierpoint Morgan or Carnegie, and then he could have made things " hum." (Laughter.) Mr. PARKER also replied in suitable terms.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>CENTRIFUGAL FORCE.</head>
        <p>THE CENTRIFUGAL FORCE.</p>
        <p>People Who Are Leaving the Toni).</p>
        <p>The sale of work was opened on Thursday by Mrs. Moston, of Lymm. Mr. R. H. Meldrinn presided, and he was supported by the Revs. W. A. Beckett and E. Morgan, Mrs. Shirley Robin- son, and Mrs. Moston (Penketh). The Rev. W. A. BECKETT announced'- hat the proceeds from the previous day's sale, together with subscriptions, amounted to £153. They were now well on the way to realising the J.;250. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>INTERESTING HISTORY. ' Mr. MELnaum said lie often thought of the be- ginnings and history of Bewsey-road, and of its present position. He might be wrong, but he rather thought that in Dallam-lane was about the first meeting-place of Methodists in Warring- ton, for there was a little place called Chapel- yard there, which looked to him as if it had been a Methodist Chapel. If that were right it seemed to him that Bewsey-road was the direct descendant of Methodism in Warrington, and ought to have a warm plaCe in the hearts of all those who called thernselves Methodists. (Ap- plause.) Now, the centrifugal force was at work when people were getting spread abroad something like they were on the joy wheel. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>He did not know whether they had seen people on the joy wheel. He had, and, what was more, he had been on it. (Laughter.) There was al- ways a tendency to try to get to the circum- ference, and they could not wonder that tend-</p>
        <p>(Continued in next•eolumn —4</p>
        <p>LOVERS' SUPERSTITIONS.</p>
        <p>WHEN TO MARRY.</p>
        <p>Marriage, as the ultimate aim of all true lovers, is the most important part of the busi- nes and therefore is most beset with quaint and often unreasonable beliefs. Some of these beliefs relate to the time of marriage, as is expressed in, the following lines:— Marry in Lent And you'll live to repent.</p>
        <p>May was avoided by the Romans for the purpose of celebrating matrimony as it wag under the influence of spirits who were noted as spiteful match-breakers. The Romans pinned their faith to June, especially to those days when the moon was full. In the north of England the fate of those who mayry On certain days of the week is thus duly set forth:—</p>
        <p>Monday for wealth, Tuesday for health, Wednesday the best day of all; Thursday for losses, Friday for crosses, And Saturday no luck at all.</p>
        <p>In the Middle Ages, if the bridal party, on their way to church, met a monk, a priest. a ,hare, a dog, cat, lizard, or s•:.srpent then the union would prove a miserable one, but if they met a wolf, a spider or a toad happinegi was assured.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>CURIOUS. BELIEFS.</head>
        <p>CURIOUS. BELIEFS.</p>
        <p>Sweden has a host of curious beliefs, and despite the spread of modern education, the northern peoples of Europe are essentiallv superstitious. A Finn, for instance, has etiil more belief in magic than in medicine ill cases of serious illness. When the Swedish bridegroom goes to the altar he carries, sewn in his clothes, a quantity of garlic, rosemary or other stong-smelling herbs as a protection against trolls. The young women carry bouquets of the same plants. It is unluelq for the bridegroom to stand near a closed gate or wheroteross roads meet. At the altar, if the lady can put her right foot in front of that of the man she is marrying she will govern him to her liking to the end of their days. As she goes to the church she distri- butes bread from her pocket to all the poor and needy that she meets. Every gift means misfortune averted, but alas for ''the beggar, if he be so careless as to eat it, for then will the evil that the lady has avoid:ll fall upon the one who has received the gift. When they come home, the newly married pair visit the eowhouses and stables, that their cettls may be a gradually increasing source or pro- fit during the period of their wedded life. In parts of England it is unlucky for the maid to hear her own banns pronounced, the particular misfortune that will befall her under these circumstances being the deafness of any future children. If the funeral bell be toiled for a married woman on the dn.', when the banns are read out the prospective bride will not live for more than a twelve' month. In the all important matter of dress it is advisable to avoid green. for according to 3 :.,cottlsti superstition, rnat is the favourite hue of the fairies and they woualrebilbey nieeprrttaai, in to resent its adoption by any e There seems to be no I?eend.ary authority Tor white except itq, suggestiveness of purity and happiness. The chief positive direction that has been discovered concerning the bridal robes merely says that the bride must put on Something old, something new, •Something borrowed, and something blue.</p>
        <p>And here, by way of conclusion, is a curi- ous practice that is only superstitious in its ignorance of the law of the land; if a man marries his wife whm she is drzssed in no- thing else but a sheet then he is not reF- ponsible for any debts she may contract after the ceremony.</p>
        <p>FACE ONE MASS OF PIMPLES. 3, Newton-road, Isleworth, Middlesex, Eng.— " About five years ago I went to work at 3 chemist's in the poison room. Before I had been there long, pimples began to come on my face. They itched terribly, and soon spread all over my neck, till my face and neck were one mass of pimples. They itched something awful. Then they came to a white head. I was afraid to go in any company as my face 'always looked so raw. I used to pinch the pimples and it made their' worse. They got so bad that I was ashamell to go to the barber's thinking any time he might refuse, to shave me.</p>
        <p>"After five years' suffering I saw the adver- tisement of Cuticura Soap and Ointment and I thought I would write for a sample. I they bought a tablet of Cuticura Soap and a tin of Cuticura Ointment. After a month I now have my face clear." (Signed) J. Milford, March I. 1913. Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists everywhere. A sample of each with 32-p. book post free. Address: P. Newbery and Sons, 27, Charterhouse-square, London, or Potter Drug and Chem. Corp. Boston, U.S.A.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>GOOD CJTlZENS—continued.</head>
        <p>GOOD CJTlZENS—continued.</p>
        <p>eney held good in a place like Warrington. There was a time when Bewsey-street and Bewsey-road were the aristocratic parts of the town, where people lived to enjoy themselves after making money in the town. It seemed to tim that now the money was made in Bewses- road, and people went away to get out of tile smoke.</p>
