        <p>Nft Effect of Eyestrain on. the otis System and consequent" the general health is serious, th'eeessitates the greatest care in reie. Selection of Spectacles to give Siblehf' and the lmportanco of "t•testing cannot be over- mated Confidence the,: .4ve confidence in recomm the :114g our methods which are titwesult of over 25 years prac- ill I txPerience. r. A. NEuBERT, (r.S.A.C. by Exam.) eollSulting Optician, 17 I Sankey St., ' Warrington. (AM at Southport.)</p>
        <p>AV**</p>
        <p>II•-</p>
        <p>Therefore, try and gauge your birds to meet the requirements of the market. Birds wanted for table purposes should be hatched in January and February; these are then ready for killing about the end of April and May, when prices are at the highest. The cost of rearing at this period will run at about is. to Is. 6d. each, and if sold at 12 weeks old should show a profit of is. 6d. each, whereas if kept longer their maintenance bill is mounting up and the price per lb. is coming down, as we get nearer to August, when game enters into competition.</p>
        <p>The best methods of quick fattening is to give cooked foods, that is, instead of soaking the bis- cuit meals, to put them into a cooker and cook for about three lhours, also to use milk amowsst the food instead of water. A piece of mutton fat melted into it also aids, with Sussex ground oats and pea meal, and plenty of good, sound wheat oats as a staple grain food, with the addi- tion of " Lasco " Meat Meal in powder form in the soft mash. These foods are all flesh form- ing, and quickly add to the desired weight to he obtained. There is much profit to be made in careful handling in this department, and once a reputation is gained for supplying tender, well-fleshed, juicy fowls your demand for birds will keep you fully employed.</p>
