      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>Rejoiced in Smoke.</head>
        <p>Rejoiced in Smoke.</p>
        <p>Some of the townspeople seemed to rejoice in smoke, but he did not. Those who lived ill Bewsey would have to wash their curtains more often than the people in Toronto, where once a year was sufficient. (Laughter.) Bewsey was suffering, had suffered, and would suffer frors that centrifugal force which drove people fur- ther and further away. But there was a good population round there, and a great work to be done at Bewsey-road, and all Methodists in the Warrington Circuit, and others -who were not Methodists, helped Bewsey, in honour, ia that for so long it held its position and fulfilled its mission and preached the gospel to the poor round about. (Applause.) Mrs. MosTox said she was sure it was a privi- lege M do anything to help forward the cause of Methodism, and she trusted they would have a successful day, so that they might have inspir- ation and encouragement to continue in the 500 work. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>VALUABLE OUTSIDE HELP. Mr. UPSON proposed a vote of thanks to Mr' Moston and the Chairman, and said what struci' I them' most during that sale had been the large amount of support and sympathy they had re; ceived from all parts of the circuit. He 1)3" been associated with every sale of work there, since the bazaar in Mr. HockinS' days, and OTI 'no time had they had so much help from all the parts of the circuit. Mrs. Moston, who live`, almost at the end of the circuit, had helpee them in many ways. The Chairman was circuit steward, who was ever ready to help a' causes in the circuit: (Applause.) Mr. SHERLOCK seconded and recalled his en_rril associations with Lymm and tlie late Moston. gr. The Rev. W. A. BECKETT supported, and MeLnaont suitably responded. • The total proceeds to date amount to 030.</p>
        <p>1 i</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>Eggs, Poultry, etc.</head>
        <p>Eggs, Poultry, etc.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE PUBLIC-HOUSE TRUST.</p>
        <p>Lord Newton attended the annual meeting of the Cheshire Public-house Trust Company on Monday. He said the public-house belonging to him was the first taken by the Trust. He offered it rent free, which virtually represented a gift of ,i 250 for one year. In addition to that he spent between ,t3OO and £4OO in improving the premises and reduced the rent from £257 to £lB5. The meeting approved a dividend of five pei cent., with £750 added to the reserve. Grants amounting to over £5OO were made for public objects. Viscount Ridley and Lord Ashton of Hyde were elected on the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>THOSE WHO FAIL IN CANADA.</head>
        <p>THOSE WHO FAIL IN CANADA.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. L. Gordon, D.D., whose church is the "Central Congregational" in Winnipeg, preaching iecently, used these wards: "I am not surprised that an Englishman should be home- sick in Western Canada. I would be surprised if he were not. Think of England with her cathedral towns, her classic villages, sacred monuments, beautiful, hedge-lined roads, her peaceful atmosphere and her ten thousand his- torical associations. It will he a thousand years before we have anything to compare with the beauty, the fascinating beauty, of dear Old Eng- land. Yet I know certain Englishmen and Eng- lishwomen who think and affirm that Canada is one of the finest places in the world in which 'to live. They believe in Canada because they have succeeded here (and Canada is a good country to succeed in). The emigrant who fails in Canada was a failure at home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>ST. HELENS HEALTH:</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS HEALTH:</p>
        <p>The St. Helens health report for the past fort- night shows a greatly improved condition as compared with last year, the death-rate being 17.5 against 24.5. The town is comparatively free from measles and scarlet fever, which were epidemic last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 25, 1914. FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 25, 1914. FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>COMPETITION IN, THE MILK TRADE.</p>
        <p>- _ Mr. J. Sadler, secretary of the Cheshire Milk Producer? Association, addressing a Farmers' Union meeting at Shrewsbury, said a large number of people had entered the milk-produc- ing industry in recent years expecting to make huge profits in view of the increased prices paid for milk, but they had found that those prices had not gone up in proportion to the cost of . production, which was likely to increase still more in the near future now that the farm workmen were asking for a rise in their wages. They were seriously threatened by the dis- cory by German chemists of synthetic milk, which could be used for every purpose for whith the milk from the cow could be employed. The unfair competition they had to deal with was the handling of milk by wholesale purchasers, who were able to bring down the prices of con- tracts to a point which to the individual pro- ducer was almost ruinous. One of several ways by which such Competition could be met suc- cessfully was combination among the producers, the establishment of bureaux and of factories.</p>
        <p>The meeting appointed a committee to confer with the Cheshire Association with a view to sectiring fair prices on contracts.</p>
        <p>Mr. Sadler said the railway companies had issued a schedule of charges, and this went a long way to remove the objections of farmers to the multiplication of fractions of a penny into a penny. The point as to whether the companies were entitled to any increase at all had yet to be tested, and even ff the decision of the Courts was that they were, they still had to show that they/were entitled tO the full 4 per cent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>Co-operation Needed.</head>
        <p>Co-operation Needed.</p>
        <p>Among the •reasons for lack of a legitimate margin of profit was the competition of Milk from the South of England, where rents were low and miserable rates of wages were paid. With a much lower cost of prod-action; the farmers of the South were able to undersell the milk producers of Cheshire and Shropshire, and to capture, to some extent, their markets. A more important phase of the situation, -how- ever, was the fact that the milk dealers, by means of their milk factories, were able to con- trol the trade at both ends. At the time when they were making their contracts with the farmers they were receiving a plentiful supply of milk from the factories, and were, therefore, in a very strong position. They were still fur- ther assisted by the fact that many farmers who made a practice of selling their milk tduring the winter, and making cheese during the summer, had quite innocently agreed to continue to send milk at the winter price during the month of April, not realising that they were thereby pro- viding the milk dealers with a weapon to beat down the prices of the regular milk sellers. The time had arrived when farmers must seriously consider the advisability of setting up milk factories zif their own on co-operative lines, and thus get control of the trade at their own end.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>WEEDS AS D3SEASE CAhiIIERS.</head>
        <p>WEEDS AS D3SEASE CAhiIIERS.</p>
        <p>Apart from the direct injury they inflict upon the cultivated crops, says The Times," many weeds are objectionable because Of their liabil- ity to harbour or propagate disease. The com- mon ehatlock is a noteworthy example of the disease-breeding weed. It has been proved that this plant acts as host—either as victim or as carrier—to the finger-and-toe •disease of the swede oartil turnip. The spores of this fungus have a limited existence in separation from the natural prey. They mild not survive the in- terval between recurring root crops if it were not for the harbourage afforded by weeds of the same natural order, df which ehanlock is the best-known specimen. The prevalence. of this weed adds greatly to the :difficulties of eradicat- ing this destructive pest, and it is probable that other weeds act in a similar capacity to- wards crops to which they have a natural rela- tionship. Many cultivated crops have their counterpart in the comprehensive category of weeds. Cereals certainly have, and some of their worst fungoid and insect .enemies are known to exist and breed upon them. This phase of the weed problem merits serious at- tention, for anything that shelters And feeds the enemies of farm and garden crops calls for vigorous measures of ' destruction.</p>
        <p>ARTIFICIAL MILK. In the Rsuse of Commisns, Mr. Rimciman was asked whether he was aware that it was proposed to sell in London and elsewhere •as milk a liquid •prepared froth a species .ef soya bean, having the appearance and to some ex- tent the flavour of cow's milk, but not possess- ing its nutritive or other qualities. He was further asked if the Board or the Local Cforern- ment Board would not exercise any powers they had to prevent the sale of such a product under the misleading denomination or In reply, Mr. Runciman said he was aware of the product referred to, but was not prepared to express any opinion 'as to its nutritive qualities. But if an article which was not milk was sold under that name, it would be the duty of local authorities to institute prosecutions under the Food and Drugs Act for selling an article which was not of the nature, substance, and quality of the article demanded.</p>
        <p>WHY NOT PROTECT HEDGEHOGS AND WEASELS? Everybody knows that the hedgehog eats snails and slugs, says "Farm and Home," but few people, perhaps, have realised that he is also a liberal consumer of moles and rats. Everything considered, the hedgehog, like the weasel, deserves a place in the economy of</p>
        <p>nature. A hedgehog in the garden is just as valuable as a weasel in the rickyard. The weasel and the stoat are the deadliest ,enemies that rats and mice have to encounter. Game-</p>
        <p>keepers who wage ceaseless war on the weasels are to all intents and purposes propagating one of the worst pests of agriculture in the foster- ing of rodents, like rats, mice, and rabbits. Where a weasel takes up its residence near a farm steading, there will be few rats about, and, at the risk of losing a few chickens, if not securely cooped, the farmer would do well to leave the weasel unmolested, nor seek to hound it off the premises or shoot it. Truly, a few more weasels and hedgehogs would serve to bring the pestilential vermin to some sense of the fitness of things. And, much as we may despise the bloodthirsty weasel, it may be neces- sary sometimes to put up with one evil in order to drive out another and greater One.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>STEAMER FRIVOLITIES.</head>
        <p>STEAMER FRIVOLITIES.</p>
        <p>"Aden has disappeared from our view and we are crossing the Arabian Sea on our last stage towards Bombay, and the whole attention of the passengers is concentrated on deck games. "There are games of all ages, for both sexes; games of skill, games of chance, games of . strength, games of weight, and the man, woman or child who cannot fit into one of them must be hard indeed to please. "A bridge tournament is always popular, and one is roused out of a sweet morning's sleep, a post-prandial forty winks, or an early night 1 beauty-sleep, to take a hand. "Deck quoits come next in popularity, and those who have a cabin on the spar deck, just where the quoits should land, have no peace whilst there is any daylight; nothing is more terrible than the continual 'plop' of the little red or blue ribboned rope ring; it obliges one to follow the game right through, and the laughter, and howls, and war dances, keep one fully in- formed of the varying fortunes of the com- batants.</p>
        <p>"There are several varieties of deck quoits. ,The quoits may either be thrown into chalked concentric rings on the deck, or into a bucket, or on to a spike; or there are little bean bags, which are thrown on to a slanting board marked out in squares of different values. " The tug-of-war is a favourite game with all, and is seldom left aside. Pulling the turtle is simple and amusing; a rope about three yards long is provided, with a big loop at each end. The two contestants slip the loops over the pack of their necks and under their arms; they then start back to back, and crawl on their hands and feet (their knees must not touch the deck) an opposite directions until the rope is taut be- tween them, and at the word "go" they crawl, or try to crawl, turtle fashion, each one en- deavouring to drag his opponent back over the line. "Pillow fighting,on a spar causes endless fun— mattresses are spread on the deck and a spar covered with a blanket is fixed about four feet above it. Two fighters' straddle the spar, rider fashion, and fight with pillows, the object being to make the opponent lose his balance and fall off the spar on to the mattress.</p>
        <p>_ 'There are several ways of winning; some duck and dodge the coming blow and hope that, not meeting with obstruction, it will overbalance the striker and make him the easy prey to a smart little tap; others trust to brute strength</p>
        <p>, and grip the spar with strong thighs; aiad some let their opponents batter them ruthlessly, and patiently wait until he getm fore he has recovered, Put one strong, quick carele then, be- ss clip that sends him sprawling,,, to a chorus of laughter i .—J. J. Mann, n Round the World in a Motor Car."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>BULLOCK'S ESCAPADE.</head>
        <p>A BULLOCK'S ESCAPADE.</p>
        <p>Three persons—a child, a butcher and a re- tired captain—Were tossed by a bullock which got beyond control at Yarmouth last week. The child was not much the mrcirse, but the butcher was badly hurt, and the captain was gored in the thigh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>Hardy ferns are now getting active for the new spring growth after. the period of rest. This is the best time to transplant or divide. A rauleh of decayed manure and leaves after planting will be helpful.</p>
        <p>The lesser PeriWiiskle Yirica minor, succeeds eonditions. It is quite happy bank or for carpeting gulil•loilyupenr d uleorncillehrlgtla varyingeB 81-Inn lt is a good plant for a smoky district. • , Horseradish should be grown in a corner where it has not much chance of spreading. The soil should be dug deeply working into the soil some manure. Any bit of crown root will strike. Make holes 10 inches deep with a dibber, drop a crown piece in each hole, making sure that it rests fiat on the soil. Then fill in the holes with fine soil. The crop will be ready for use by autumn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>HARDY ANNUALS FOR EDGING.</head>
        <p>HARDY ANNUALS FOR EDGING.</p>
        <p>_ There are quite a number of hardy annuals suitable for edgings to beds, as the following list shows:— Sweet alyssum, white, sweet scented, 6 inches. Alyssum little Dorrit, dwarf and compact, white. Acperula aturea setosa, blue, 10 inches. Kaulfussia •amelloides, blue, 9 inches. Kaulfussia kermesina, crimson, 7 inches: Nemophila insignis, blue, 6 inches. Nemophilm atomaria, white, spatted dark blue, 6 inches. _ _</p>
        <p>Sanoitalia procumbeus, golden yellow, 6 inches. • Saponaria calabrica, pink, 6 inches. Omphaldles linifolia, white, i 6 inches. Phacelia lcampanularia, blue, 6-8 inches. Macelia campanularia, white, 6-8 inches.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>SOWING oNio,NS.</head>
        <p>SOWING oNio,NS.</p>
        <p>Onions are practically always wanted, and should be regarded as oily of the most important kitchen garden crops. The ground should be deeply dug and enriched with good manure. As soon as the soil is right for sowing fork over the ground two or three inches deep, rake and make level. Draw the drills, 12 to 15 inches apart, sow the seed thinly, covering them, and then thoroughly tread the ground to make it firm. The moment the young plants are through, scatter soot over them, an stir up the surface with the hoe. A good crop of onions depends on frequently moving the Surface soil with the hoe; it stimulates growth, prevents the ground from cracking, and keeps down weeds. Give occasional dusting of soot during growth, and plenty of water. .</p>
        <p>THE NIGHT-SCENTED STOCK. We would remind reader A before it is too late about the charm of the night-scented stock, which is our usual English name of the plant signalised by the Latin appellatioga of Matthiola bicornis. We have heard of the Virginian stock (Malcolmia rnaritima) having been purchased in mistake for the real Simon pure, impossible as it seems to fall into such an error. Every even- ing and all night long the night-ooented stock gives out a delightful incense sort of smell, which never seems to pall upon our olfactory nerves. To those who do not know it, we would say that it should not be planted in a prominent position, as it blooms close up during the day- time and give it a woe-begone appearance. . It should be sown where it is to bloom, and the seedlings thinned out to about two inches apart every way. It is a plant for close association with the dwelling-house." The Garden."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>REPAIRING GARDEN PATHS.</head>
        <p>REPAIRING GARDEN PATHS.</p>
        <p>Where this was not done earlier, no time should be lost in seeing to this very necessary work. During March the rainfall in some dis- tricts was abnormal, so that the drains and traps were severely tested in consequence. Where gravel is used on the paths, it would be advisable to have it passed through a riddle. This will rid it of soil, and assist in destroying weeds and moss. Some will say this is a big task. So it is, but it will pay if one can find time for it. The work, of course, must be done in fine weather.—" The Garden."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>SECOND-HAND CARS.</head>
        <p>SECOND-HAND CARS.</p>
        <p>A motor car, like everything else, always gives , most satisfaction when purchased new, although on the other hand the second-hand article is often very satisfactory. It seemed almost super- fluous to point out the importance of the prospec- tive buyer of knowing that the car in question is. really the property of the vendor, but a recent warning by the Legal Department of the Royal Automobile Club indicates a hitherto unsus- pected pitfall for the unwary. A member acquired a car from an individual whom he naturally believed to be the owner, but judge of his surprise, after some months' use, to receive an intimation from one of the big London stores : that the car in question had been supplied by them to the original owner on the hire purchase system, the agreement being that the firm re- tained ownership until all the instalments had been paid. Obviously the second possessor of the car had not the slightest title to it, and if the sellers had insisted on their legal rights they could easily have demanded possession. In this particular case there would have been no chance of redress, as the original purchaser has since become a bankrupt, but fortunately the case was settled amicably and without recourse to extreme action.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>SMALL, BUT UNMANAGEABLE.</head>
        <p>SMALL, BUT UNMANAGEABLE.</p>
        <p>John Fenney, aged eleven, of 23, Lewis-street, mSotn . Helens on money and goods frVoimo hadva various sesssehdo shops, stealingwas at Wednesday ordered to receive six strokes of the birch, and was bound over for three years under the probation officer. It was stated that although the boy was only eleven years old and small for his age, he was unraanag,eable.</p>
        <p>A peal of tubular bells, erected at St. Tames' Church, Lower Darwen, in memory of the Rev. George Sumner, vicar for 27 years, was dedicated on Saturday by Archdeacon Fletcher.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>CYCLING AND MOTORING The Provinces and the Annual Show Question.</head>
        <p>CYCLING AND MOTORING The Provinces and the Annual Show Question.</p>
        <p>IMPORTANCE OF THE BRAKE.</p>
        <p>If I were to be asked " What is the most important part of a bicycle?" my answer would be "the brake," and I should be greatly sur- prised to meet with much opposition. Ever since cycles have been More than the hobby of the few, brake failures have been the most prolific cause of accidents. Unfortunately, such a state of affairs still continues, and seems , likely to d 9 so, unless cyclists at last come to the conclusion that a few minutes' trouble is better tlian risking a bd spill. The brake is usually one of the strongest parts of the mach- ine, but no amount of mechanical perfection will counteract the effect of carelessness and continued neglect. After a hard season's riding brake rubbers often refuse to grip the rims as a result of wear, but only require a minor adjust- ment, or at most renewal, to ensure safety. Again screws may have worked loose or rods become defective, yet many riders never think such de- tails worth attending to. In any case, no one should ever trust to a single brake; two are al- ways necessary, and either should be capable of bringing the machine to a standstill under the most exacting conditions. In mountainous dis- tricts, such as the Lake District and North Wales, I have often seen bicycles fitted with three brakes, which certainly guarantees a maximum of safety.</p>
        <p>Wayside Repairs. Why is it that breakdowns always happen when one is miles from anywhere and without the wherewithal to make them good? Those who have silfiered and pushed a machine several miles along a hot, dusty road are probably responsible for the Suggestion that the itinerant cycle re- pairer would supply a long-felt want. But would</p>
        <p>lie? In view of the large number of cyclists to</p>
        <p>be seen during a run standing by the roadside mending 'punctures and making adjustments it seems as if he would reap a rich harvest, but, as a matter of fact, the proposal is not a new one, and has been tried with rather disappointing results. Breakdowns occur where the repairer is not, and should he finally put in an appearance the repair is very likely completed by the aid of a passing whe-elman. It is all very well say- ing: "I wOuld give anything for some valve tubing !" or similar article; but many riders would jib at increased prices, quite forgetting that the man would be working some distance</p>
        <p>froui his shop, and would very. likely have put</p>
        <p>in a considerable amount of riding in return for a solitary job. The game really is not worth the candle, and those few repairers wha have tried it soon decide to limit their activities to their own places of business.</p>
        <p>Cycling a Children's Pastime. Cycling as, a pastime has the great advantage of being suitable for people of all ages, and thus reducing the effect of physical disability to a minimum. , Children of all ages take to it as naturally as a duck takes to water, and boys and girls of quite tender years rival their elders in skill and endurance. If anything, greater care is necessary in the selection of a child's machine than of one for an adult. First and foremost the juvenile machine, should be neither too large nor too heavy. Personally, I am inclined to the opinion that it is better if a child is not allowed to cycle until it is of a reasonable age, such an age, in fact, which permits the use of an adult's machine of small size. My reason for this con- clusion is that those who buy juvenile cycles,: excellent though those may be, allow the chil- dren to fide them when a higher cycle is really necessary, being naturally unwilling- to replace a machine which is in perfect running Order. An experienced rider should see to the adjustments of the youngster's machine, and thus remove the danger of riding either too high or too low. Motor Shows. There are evidences that before very long the question of holdipg annual shows elsewhere than in London will occupy a prominent place in motoring politics. For many years Manchester has had a most successful show, worthy in every way of the great cotton city, and she naturally ' wishes to retain it for her own sake, but, on the other hand, Yorkshire, through the mouth of one of her leading spokesmen, has intimated that if Manchester is granted another show, York- shire will demand a similar privilege. There is more than a little justice in the claim; the pro- vincial show is already an accomplished fact, and who is to say that such and such a town is to be favoured above all others? Liverpool, Man- chester, Leeds and Sheffield all have strong claims, and why should not Newcastle, with its Populous hinterland and Birmingham, the in- dustrial capital of the Midlands, hold an annual show if such an event is likely to be successful? There is but little difference in the purchasing powers of the several areas, and their several claims are difficult if not impossible to refute.</p>
        <p>R</p>
        <p>The Disadvantages of Level Crossings. Level crossings are still numerous in many parts of the United Kingdom, but give satisfac- tion to no one except the pedestrian, who natur- ally prefers level ground to slopes. Of course, the system was all very well in the early days of railway construction, when trains were few and road traffic light and leisurely, but with the advent of the cycle and motor car the conditions changed and the level crossing became a rather dangerous nuisance.. But until the, time comes when such an obvious anomaly is entirely swept away, there is no reason why inconvenience should not be reduced to a minimum. A case in point was recently investigated by the Auto- mobile Association, where complaints had been made against a Sussex gatekeeper, who was in the habit of only opening two gates out of four, an action which demanded a very careful manoeuvring for cars to cross the line. In jus- tice to the Railway Company it is only fair to say that as soon tiA they were made aware of the state of affairs the careless employe was repri- manded and the crossing tended with more con- sideration for users.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>CYCLISTS' NOTEBOOK. Suggestions Regarding Some Warrington Roads. BY SILVER-STREET.</head>
        <p>THE CYCLISTS' NOTEBOOK. Suggestions Regarding Some Warrington Roads. BY SILVER-STREET.</p>
        <p>It is three weeks now since I promised to take up again the subject of Warrington roads—not in a complaining, but in a suggestive spirit. The tramways of Knutsford-road and the Cause- way are furnished for long distances with cen- tral standards. Could the committee cause the four or five feet of roadway in these stfetches to be laid with tar macadam? It would then be a safe and favourite track for the hundreds of cyclists—a track unmolested by lurries, motors, or auto-cycles. Many cyclists use it as it is, but its condition prevents many from using the only part of the road that might be our very own. A curious oversight seems to be the cause of hundreds of yards of roadway in the Knutsford- road being entirely unused and unusable. I refer to the width between the tramway setts and the gutter stones, which is supposed to be covered with road metal. For the whole winter and for every wet day no Cyclist dare touch it for its greasiness. Thus the width of roadway from the kerb to the near tram rail is divided into three patches—gutter, macadam and setts— and .as each is too narrow of itself, they are all useless. Surely the Tramway Committee are not so hard up that they cannot complete the paving alongside the tram rail. It ought to he taken in hand at once before the summer traffic becomes so heavy. REPLI ES.</p>
        <p>J.F., Knutsford.--T-h- e--e-as-iest way to Angle-</p>
        <p>sey is not the nicest. It would lie all along the coast from Chester to the Suspension Bridge at Bangor, and would take you through chemical fumes, iron and lead works, brick and clay fields, and below the mighty stone quarries of Penmaenmawr. For a pleasant change, go through Middlewich and Winsford to Tarpor- ley, 21 miles. Thence by Spurstow, over the Peckforton Hills to Broxton and Farndon, to Wrexham, 21. Ruabon to Llangollen and Coy- wen, 22. Over by Cerrig-y-Druidion and Pentre Voelas to Bettws, 22; capel Curig, Pen-y-gwryd and Llanberis to Carnarvon. Thence by ferry to the island, or along the charming road by the Strait to the Suspension Bridge. Bangor is un- interesting compared with Carnarvon, and this route takes you past' the very foot of Snowdon and through the Llanberis Pass. Also at Bettws the celebrated Swallow Falls and the Miner's Bridge ate by the roadside, whilst the Fairy Glen is only a short walk away:- At Whitsuntide Wales is at its best, and these spots will then be absolutely unrivalled, in the southern half of this island for beauty. T.S.E.—Yes, if your inner tube has stretched it is not a very hard thing to reduce it by 11 inches. It must be cut in two and rejoined, taking in any length required. The professional repairer uses a mandril, but the amateur can make a perfectly satisfactory' job with the case that contained an upright incandescent mantle. One end to be joined is poked through it and doubled back over the outside of the cardboard, which is exactly the right size for Our purpose. Then the other end is slid over till within lf inches from the other out end. Both ends must now be thoroughly cleaned with a paraffin rag, and solutioned two or three times. Then, before the solution becomes tacky, the one end slides over the other and all is. left for a couple of hours to set. When the tube has been drawn into its natural position, the mantle case is slit up with scissors to get it off the re-united tube.</p>
        <p>alb 411/. • • Aso • ~ • , __hop;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>W.W.C.C. TO FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>W.W.C.C. TO FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>EEN cyclists naturally prefer those cycle tyres which</p>
        <p>yield them the most profitable return:</p>
        <p>It thould have been to Helsby, but from one cause and another we only reached Frodsham at tea-time. The route was through Moore and ' Norton to escape the motors and the .dust and a worthy exchange was the result, for these by- roads were our own. Twenty-nine hungry cyclists are not easily catered for, and we did not know a place at Helsby, where we could all. sit down. But at the Golden Lion, in the mid- dle of Frodsham, Mrs. Forster treated us ad- mirably, and ample damage was done to what she provided. After a walk up to Overton Church the cycles were resumed and a first-rate round was completed via Five Crosses, Hatch- mere and Crowton. CLUB RUNS FOR TO-DAY. Wesleyan C.C., Whitegate, 2.30. Sankey Bridges Wesley Guild, Liverpool, 2.0. Lamp Time.—To-night, 8.25; next Saturday, 8.35.</p>
      </div>
