      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 25, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 25, 1914.</p>
        <p>SHOULD WARRINGTON BE BOOMED?</p>
        <p>A SCHEME TO ATTRACT WORKSj DOES THE WELL-BEING OF A TOWN</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>TO 'fIIE TO‘l N.</head>
        <p>TO 'fIIE TO‘l N.</p>
        <p>Deputation's Mixed Reception.</p>
        <p>Locally, we have heard much of the advan- tages of the borough as an industrial centre, but , now there is a suggestion to laud the attractions of the town all over the country, and, possibly, on the Continent, in order to induce manufac- turers to set up work's-- ;Jere to their benefit. At a meeting in the Town Hall on Monday of the Warrington Chamber of Commerce, Coun- cillor Pemberton, chairman of the Towns Com- mittee, appeared as the deputation. Captain Crosfield presided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>APOSTLE.</head>
        <p>THE APOSTLE.</p>
        <p>Councillor Pemberton said the Corporation were anxious to advertise the attractions of the borough. The general scheme was that they had received tenders from the various newspapers, and they thought the "Manchester Guardian" the most advantageous. _ _</p>
        <p>- They proposed taking a full-page and paying one-third of the cost for adyertising Warrington generally, and sub-letting fle other spaces to the manufacturers in the town. They also thought that individual manufacturers in, the borough might keep their names prominently identified with Warrington in their advertise- ments. They might also go further afield and advertise in the Continental and American papers, because there were other manufacturers I coming .into the country. 1</p>
        <p>They could not legally spend the money for this purpose out of the rates, and they had only a small sum which they proposed to spend from "The Walker Gift Fund."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>SOME DISCUSSION.</head>
        <p>SOME DISCUSSION.</p>
        <p>There was then some discussion as to the ad- I vertising scheme, and the Chairman said many I of them did not view it very clearly. They were in favour of the idea of helping on the trade and industry of the town, but they did not see how they, as manufacturers, were going to get a return for the money they spent in advertising in order to attract other people to the town. Councillor Pemberton said they would be ad- vertising their trades to the public generally, and if they got manufacturers to come in the town they would get a reduction in rates. ("Question.") That was the great point. They had everything they wanted in Warrington now. They had gone to the initial expense in regard to water, electricity and gas supply, and many items of that sort, and if they had additional manufacturers the amount received would be to a great extent profits, and therefore the rates would be reduced. No one liked paying rates they could avoid.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>"MUNICIPAL INDUSTRIES."</head>
        <p>"MUNICIPAL INDUSTRIES."</p>
        <p>Lieut.-Colonel Fairclough : Attract people here with a view to making the municipal industries pay better than they are doing now? Councillor Pemberton: That would be one object, but the great object would be a reduction in rates.</p>
        <p>Lieut.-Colonel Fairclough : One follows on the other.</p>
        <p>Councillor Pemberton: It would to a great extent, but not necessarily so. If you had more works on the Causeway you would have no extra sewerage work, road-making or lighting to do. It would be very profitable if the land by Fletcher, Russell's were built upon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>BURDEN ON THE RATES.</head>
        <p>BURDEN ON THE RATES.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. E. Parker said that if they had more people in the borough with the extra works, the charges on the rates would increase pretty well proportionately. It would depend on the charac- ter of the trades that were brought, and on the character of the masters. ,He would like to .see the trades isolated to see what burden they were on the rates.</p>
        <p>Councillor Pemberton: I agree there would be extras, but tire cost would be out of all pro- -portion to he extra profit.</p>
        <p>Mr. Parker: I wish I could see that. I think it depends on the quality of the trades and not the quantity.</p>
        <p>Councillor Pemberton: The profit on the piece of land I have mentioned would be enormous.</p>
        <p>In answer to the Chairman, Councillor Pem- berton said they proposed inserting the adver- tisement three times—Saturday, Tuesday and Thursday. Many of the landowners, continued Councillor Pemberton, had agreed to meet the committee in every way they could with regard to the price of the land. Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co. named an approximate Price for the land on the Causeway which he thought was very reasonable indeed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>LANDOWNERS TO BE INTERVIEWED.</head>
        <p>LANDOWNERS TO BE INTERVIEWED.</p>
        <p>In answer to Mr. Parker, Councillor Pember- ton said they were having an interview with the landowners shortly.</p>
        <p>. - The Chairman: i don't know that we can give any definite reply to the councillor, except to say in general terms that we appreciate the efforts of the Town Council to look after the welfare and development of the town. How would it be to circularise the members of the Chamber, stating the proposition before us, and asking for a reply, and then take the net result? Mr. 0. Lofthouse : What would you ask them? The Chairman: Whether they are willing to subscribe to the cost of this advertising. Aldertaan Bennett: We have done this thing twice over ten or fifteen years ago. Mr. Lofthouse said there were many firms in Warrington to whom advertising in the " Man- chester Guardian" would not be any use. His place, for one. Councillor Pemberton contended that by ad- vertising it might fetch those in the town a deal of trade.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>WHOLESALE MANNER.</head>
        <p>WHOLESALE MANNER.</p>
        <p>Mr. Seeker said that to take a page in any one paper would not have the desired effect for a long time. Advertising should be done in a wholesale manner. The Chairman: I agree with Mr. Seeker that you have to spend a lot of money in advertis- ing. A little bit spent is money thrown away. You must either do it on a big scale or not at all. I have found that so in our trade. Councillor Pemberton: We are only appeal- ing to a limited number. of people, and the " Manchester Guardian" covers all the ground in the North of England. We could go on the! Continent and America as well. Mr. F. W. Monks said it would not be the slightest use to him in his business. Can Coun- cillor Pemberton tell us where the blast fur: ! naces are that, are mentioned in the book pub- lished on the town? Councillor Pemberton: Does it say in Monks:War- rington r. ipz Vnz nsaboriri[o:gnt:i rington or the neighbourhood? on Ittidivseratnisienrgr.64. augh. ter.)</p>
        <p>Lieut.-Colonel Fairclough : They have put down in the hook "the river is tidal to Howley Lock and free from toll." That is a gross mis- tale.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said they welcomed the efforts being made, but they, did not want offensive trades. Of course. the Corporation had power with regard to such. Councillor Pemberton then withdriw.</p>
        <p>The ballot of members of the Textile Union shows a five to one majority for Labour repre- 'sentation, and it has been decided to put the fund into existence as from the quarter begin- ning April Ist.</p>
        <p>While playing near his home at Asker Farm, Matlock, on Saturday, Willie Smith, the three- year-old son of the farmer, fell into a trough of water and was drowned before his mother could. rescue him. .44</p>
        <p>.1</p>
        <p>WNW</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>INCREASE WITH ITS SIZE?</head>
        <p>INCREASE WITH ITS SIZE?</p>
        <p>An Illusory Theory.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "Ti!a Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—The above question i4c iSuggested by the effort at present being made kir the Town Coun- cil to increase the population Warrington. To effect this it is proposed to malPti,4raewn the; various considerations which would ll:Amended manufacturers to place their works:Het*, the members of the Borough CouncilWaffiiiiire that this would be an advantage? if§ con- tended that by increasing the rateable value through the contributions of newcomers, the rates generally would be reduced and the present inhabitants benefit. That the rateable value would be increased I grant, but that this would tend to reduce the present rates I greatly. doubt. The theory involved seems to be that the larger the town the lower will be the rates. If this principle were sound, and of general applica- tion, we should find the larger towns the lowest rated. As far as my knowledge goes, this does not hold good. The amount of the rates is no doubt affected by many causes, hence it is diffi- cult to make accurate deductions, and trace the effect of any particular cause. But if the theory that as a town increases in size its rates diminish be correct, we should find on the whole (although possibly with considerable variations) the larger towns the lowest rated. I may be wrong, but I do not think such a conclusion is borne out by facts; indeed, it seems to he rather the other etsh ei s 1 ac rzerl ythaek it on wt no tt hh ee theory of reduction of costs by business amal- gamation which has proved so illusory in the past. If such amalgamations have proved finan- cially successful this has been due to the estab- lishment of a monopoly, whole or partial, seldorn if ever due to cost reduction. But from what source is the proposed town economy likely to arise? The education rate would proportionately be no less, new schools would have to be built for the additional Children. All the other factors —street-making, poor rate, police rate, sewer rate, and salaries—would increase proportion- ately with the population. This, at any rate, is the general experience of town expansion, and what reason is there to think that the experi- ence of Warrington would differ from the general rule?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>Homes that are a Burden to the Community.</head>
        <p>Homes that are a Burden to the Community.</p>
        <p>- - It seems tome quality is a far more important factor than quantity, and this whether you judge of the personal unit, or the industry unit. There are some firms in the borough which, from the interest they take in their workers and their treatment of the same, and also from the character of the workers themselves, are much ' more desirable from the town's point of view r than others. The workers in some industries I scarcely burden the poor or the police rate at all, and they make little or no call upon public hell) or charitable effort. On the other hand, there are industries (not necessarily the worst paid), a section Of whose workers are responsible for the main part of the rates mentioned, and who create nearly all the Social difficulties. Sup- pose you have a man earning, say, 355. a week, who spends 10s. to 15s. a week on drink, by no means uncommon in some trades. Such a one may help the Imperial Exchequer, but is no use to the Town Chancellor. He lives in a poor, low rated house, and the condition of his home and aS a rule, corresponds with his manner' ,of life. The police, the Medical Officer, and the Sanitary Authorities have to regulate it; the school officer has to visit it to drive the children to school, and the School Attendance Committee has to consider applications for exemptions for every child before such attain working age. Town nurses have to look after such homes in I eases of illness, and the mother brings her bowl for public soup immediately the effect of a coal strike is felt locally. Such homes are a burden upon tho community, and such Men are asso- I ciated with particular industries and particular firms much more than many realise. To in- crease the number of such workers and such firms, instead of diminishing the burden of the present ratepayers would increase it, while the I increase of number of the workers and firms like the first-named would be a distinct benefit to , the borough. Hence I hold that the well-being of a town, from whatever standpoint you view it, is dependent more on the quality than on the quantity of any addition. And since you cannot at present regulate quality it is scarcely worth while spending money on trying to secure quantity.</p>
        <p>Those Who Benefit.</p>
        <p>The only class of individuals who are sure to benefit from an increased population are the holders of land. I have no objection to these receiving an advantage, but I take it that this is not the object the Corporation have in view in their efforts to advertise the town. Before spend- ing money upon advertising, councillors ought to make it clear that an increased population is desirable, and likely to be of advantage to the finances of the town in regard to the expenditure as well as the income, or rather in the relation between the two.</p>
        <p>CHARLES E. PARKER. Penketh, April 22nd, 1914.</p>
        <p>Old and New Cooks.</p>
        <p>--- In the days of our grandparents cooks were mostly uneducated people, whose principal capacity was that of enduring excessive heat. They stood before roaring fires and watched the roasting of huge joints and the boiling of bevy, indigestible puddings. In this enlightened Cen- tury all sensible people realise the importance of good food properly cooked. One does not hear so much about "valiant meat-eaters." They have learnt that Nature's finest food is not flesh- meat, but grape sugar. Furthermore, they have found out the best way of enjoying and getting the full benefit of that wonderful substance, which is .by eating liberally of Currants, the little fruit which is practically all grape sugar, and, therefore, all mire nutriment. Here is a recipe prepared by a man of European fame. .Try it to-day.</p>
        <p>CURRANT PATTIES.</p>
        <p>4 ozs. Currants, 1 oz. cornflour, 1 lemon, 4 ozs. castor sugar, oz. butter, short-crust paste. Method: Line some patty pans with thin short- crust paste, providing paste covers for each. Mix cornflour with a gill of cold water, stir in grated rind and juice of lemon, Currants and sugar, cook all together for five minutes, stir- ring all the time, and add the butter; mix well, fill the paste-lined patty pans with the prepara- tion, wet the edges of the paste, put on the covers, brush over with sweet milk, and bake 23 Minutes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>ST. HELENS HOUSING SCHEME.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS HOUSING SCHEME.</p>
        <p>There was a somewhat remarkable develop- ment at the meeting of the St. Ilelens Health Committee in regard to the question of housing. The Council had passed a resolution that tene- ment houses should be erected at a cost of „t 15,000 but Mr. Forshaw now moved that this resolution be varied, and that cottages be built in place of tenements. Mr. Hamblett seconded, but the resolution was defeated. Dr. Jackson:then moved that the Council be recommended to rescind their resolution with regard to the erection of tenements. Mr. Abbott seconded, and this was carried without any opposition. The Town Clerk: Does the resolution include that we must not borrow any money? Mr. Abbott: It is no good borrowing money' if we don't want it. - _ . .</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Alderman Bates), in accept- ing the resolution, remarked: We should get through a lot of business at this rate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>CHILDREN'S CAMP.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CAMP.</p>
        <p>Five Hundred Poor Boys and Girls Have Seaside Holiday.</p>
        <p>SUBSCRIBERS NEEDED.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the etlppertei6 Of thc Warrington Children's Summer Chnip was hold on Monday evening at the Ohl Atudemy, when what was described eta the blost satisfactory financial statement tree etesented. Mr. J. H. Sibethtrit presitleth 'SW he was supported by CoçiHor W. H. J. Jenkins (honorary secretary) twa fr. J. A. Ransome (honorary treasurer). The TREASURER said he was pleased to give a very satisfactory report this year—the most sat- isfactory that he had been able to give. The amount received from visitors at camp was £66 45., against -6124 12s. the previous year. For some reason or other the number of visitors had been extraordinarily small, only 57 attending the camp in addition to the, children during the whole year, which was by far the lowest they had had for the last seven years at least. They commenced the year with a debit balance of £35 3s. 6d., and finished with a debit balance of -65 4s. 7d., which was exceedingly satisfactory, and the lowest debit balance they had had. Tak- ing the gross average cost per child at camp, including everything, it amounted to Bs. 3d. a week this year, against 7s. 9d. for the preceding year and Bs. 3d. the year before. The net average cost per child, including provisions and railway fareamo amounted the to fourss kassofatglai caretaker'seinsttherevious p salary, year's 4s. sd. They could safely say that the cost of a child per week was from ss. to ss. 6(1., including everything. Thanks were accorded to Mr. H. B. Houghton for auditing the books. The accounts were agreed to. Mr. J. MOORE MURRAY Mentioned the difficulty that the committee were faced with by children not turning up for the medical exaniination: It was difficult to get others to fill in their places.</p>
        <p>THE CAUSE OF SUCCESS. The CHAIRMAN referred to the pleasure he had in presiding, and said that in other institutions it was customary, when they did well, to con- gratulate themselves, and when they did badly to look for the cause. Regarding the Children's ' Summer Camp, they simply had to look for the cause why they did so well, and they could easily find two reasons. The first was that they tried to improve the physique of the children, and the second was that these people, who had the children's welfare at heart, approved of their scheme, which was a guarantee that the best was being done. (Hear, hear.) LAST SUMMER'S CAMP. , The Swami/1r gave his report, which stated that" the past season has been, from every point of view, a fairly successful one. The weather was exceptionally fine and the children who went to camp experienced a most enjoyable and healthy time. They returned greatly benefited by their holiday. The number of children who Participated in the enjoyable holiday- at the camp were 2C. i girls and 239 boys, making a total of 504. In al' !Won to this number many weakly and sickly c "dren were sent to camp during l convalescen(e. The visitors, who have appre- elated the m y comforts which the camp with its surround, s afford, have been slightly below those of pm , JllB years, and I am sure the people of WarringLoa are not aware of the many advan- It must be appreciated that little, tages to be derived from a holiday, at the camp. tary profit is derived from this source. The committee again tender their warmest thanks to the voluntary workers, namely: Girls' fortnight 7-Mrs. J. S. Hill (superintendent), and the 'Misses Mary Emson, Roberts, A. Grounds, M. Morley, 4f any mone- M. Pennington, Lingard, Mary Abbot, D. Wain- wright and Lucy Birchall; boys' fortnight Messrs. W. H. Houghton (superintendent), E. England, E. J. Mee, W. Turner, A. Appleton, J. G. Barratt, T. Oxley, B. Farrar, L. Jones, and Inspector Wainwright. The committee also desire to record their warm appreciation of the splendid services pf Councillor Dr. McKee, for examining and pasing the children; the Direc- tor of Education and his staff, for their great assistance in the selection and passing of the children, and also in marshalling and seeing them safe off on their journey to Prestatyn; and also to Lieutenant-Colonel B. Fairclaugli, for the use of the Drill Hall. The whole of the camp buildings are in good repair, but they will re- quire re-painting during the coming season." He believed that this work was to be under- taken by firms, free Of cost. "New subscribers are desired," concluded the report, "and the committee would be grateful if friends of the movement would give their help in this direction, with a view to increasing the permanent income of the camp and enabling the committee to expand its efforts." ' THE OFFICIALS. The following officials were re-elected President: Lady Greenall. Vice-presidents : His Worship the Mayor (Councillor Peter Peacock), Mrs. Ziba Armitage, Messrs. G. F. Armitage, G. W. Armitage, J. F. Armitage, S. R. Armitage, W. T. Bennett, G. R. HCros a idfiienlgd: J. .NC rioisufiteclidil, A.A. JmKa.Ee Markel, J. Charlton Parr, William Long, and Miss Phyllis A. Lee. Committee: Mrs. d. A. Ewart, Mrs. E. J. Hall, Mrs. J. S. Hill, Mrs. Firth Lindley, Mrs. J. Long land, Mrs. J. A. •Ransome, Miss Mary Emson, Messrs.. J. H. Smethurst, E. G. S. Finney' J. Moore Murray, Edward Wallington, W. 11. Stansfield, and George Walker. Votes of thanks were accorded to the Chair- man, Secretary, and Treasurer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>SEVtiiPjjs' PROGRESS.</head>
        <p>SEVtiiPjjs' PROGRESS.</p>
        <p>The fundamental errors which lie at the basis of the Protectionist movement in this country are not very deep down in the soil, and no pro- dig" amount of excavating is required to unearth them. Reduced to their simplest and lowest terms they are to the effect that Great Britain is in a state of industrial decline, her manufactures are, decadent, her.trade is leaving her. The truth or falsity of such ideas admits of easy Proof, especially when the evidence is collected over a period of seven decades. The task of collecting this evidence has been more, than effectually performed by Lord Brassey in • his latest • contribution to the controversy-, British Free Traders are not theorists, neither do they concern themselves with abstract prin- ciples. On the contrary, they are so much alive to the actual facts regarding Free Trade that, as Lord Brassey points out, " if trade could be shown to flourish under Protection, 6-ea"), while it con- tinued steadily to decline under Free Trade, a change in our fiscal system would not be long delayed. The Free Trade position depends upon a just appreciation of the facts.",• These facts Lord Brassey has set out in detail, arid their cumulative effect is overwhelming. His. examination into the Protectionist antici- pation of the effects of a tariff on ges, em- ployent, and food; demonstrates the falsity of wa mevery Protectionist prediction; his scrutiny ef the varying and ever changing tests which Tariff Reformers have at intervals brought forward is equally thorough, and their • failure as tests mercilessly exPosed. Lord Brassey has also something to say on the question' of retaliation favourite w and on British social. and economic conditions, showing how blunt are the edges of two of the it in 1914. ealions of Tariff Reform as we know</p>
        <p>, One of the most conclusive chapters in the brochure is devoted to a discussion of the ques- tion of the industrial efficiency of this country. Great Britain may be seen in the markets of the world ranged by the.side of Germany and the United States, her . two most formidable competitors, with the result that so far" from . living on the sufferance of more prosperous neighbours; as ,the prophets of Tariff Reform declared ten- years' ago, thi; country finds her- self in the forefront of prosperity. As Lord Brassey has it, "Wit]) a population not parable. in numbers with the great .nations*of Continental Europe, confined within the nar- row area of small islands, not surpassingly rich in natural- resources, we have made our coml. try in a large degree the workshop of the world and its financial centre." ' The general effect which is left on the reader's mind may very well be summed up in Lord Brassey's concluding 'words. ." Free Trade has in these circumstances ceased to be an- advan- tage; it has become a necessity!! . ,</p>
        <p>*Seventy .Years of Progress under Free Trade. . By the Earl Brassey, D.C.L., London. The Free Trade t.elon, le.</p>
        <p>-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>i HE LAND: FOURTH ARTICLE.</head>
        <p>i HE LAND: FOURTH ARTICLE.</p>
        <p>Rating injustices in Town ad Country.</p>
        <p>RE-ADJUSIING hURDENS.</p>
        <p>BY CHARLES RODEN BUXTON, Honorary Secretary of the Land Inquiry</p>
        <p>Committee.</p>
        <p>Part IV. of the Urban Report deals with the quertion of Rating, both in town and country. Existing grievances and injustices may be sum- marised thus:—</p>
        <p>First, the system penalises improvements and hampers industry in general. The enterpris- ing trader and the farmer who improves his land find their assessment raised.</p>
        <p>Secondly, vacant land pays nothing, and land which is not put to its proper use by owners does not bear its fair share of local taxation.</p>
        <p>Thirdly, the burden of local taxation is too great. Lastly, the system of assessment often operates unfairly and creates considerable injustices as between different occupiers in the same area and as between one district and another.</p>
        <p>The fundamental evil of the present system is that all rates are levied on the annual value of the land and buildings taken together. Annual value is interpreted to mean the value which a tenant would give, not for the property as put to its best use, but for the property as at present used, however unsuitable that use may be, and however inadequate from the point of view of the community. As a result rates are levied upon buildings and improvements, and thus con- stitute a burden upon industry and production, not unlike the old protective duties which were swept away by the great Free Trade agitation. Another result is the exemption from rates enjoyed by vacant land, however valuable, and the partial exemption enjoyed by undeveloped and under-developed land. Many instances are given of land which possesses a high value, owing to the expenditure of the community, to the growth of population or to its situation or inherent qualities, but the owners of which con- tribute scarcely anything towards the rates. The community loses a valuable source of revenue, and an encouragement is given to owners to with- hold land from use for an unduly long period, and thus to rob the community of many possi- bilities of industrial and social development.</p>
        <p>SMALL HOLDINGS HANDICAPPED. In rural districts the present system involves the small holding paying unduly as compared with the large farm. The proposal to levy rates on the real value of the land itself instead of on a more or less arbitrary value attributed to the land and buildings together, has a double advan- tay. First, it' removes the cumbrous restric- tions on industry, and, secondly, it secures a fair contribution from those who are at present escaping with an unduly light burden, and thus brings land more rapidly into the market. A new rate upon the owner of site value cannot, in the overwhelming majority of cases, be shifted on to the occupier of the land. The reason is, of course, that such a rate in no way diminishes the supply of land, and in no way increases the demand for it, so that the conditions which pre- vail when a tax is imposed on a commodity pro- duced by labour are, in this case, entirely absent. Various arguments against the rating of site values are here examined and controverted. The conclusion does not follow that all rates Should be transferred to sites. It is shown th, such a course would, in practice, involve consid- erable hardship in individual cases, and would neither be just nor politic. A practical scheme, however, has been worked out. It is recommenfled that a penny rate should be imposed on capital site value (Is. Bd. upon the annual equivalent of the 'capital site value). The produce of this rate would go to meet existing expenditure. The Local Authority should Have the right,: if it chooses, to transfer a further portion. In many districts this could safely be done without imposing an excessive burden on any ratepayer. Lastly, all the future expenditure of the Local Authority over and above the .amount which it is expending at the time when the scheme is started, must be levied by a rate upon site values. It is proposed that the rate should be levied upon occupiers until the time when their con- tracts of tenancy are determinable, but should thereafter be paid by owners. Special provisions are made which would avoid the imposition of any increased burden on agricultural land, taken as a whole, although the existing burden would be lighter upon the highly developed land of small holders and other intensive cultivators, and heavier upon land which was inadequately used, or used mainly for sport.</p>
        <p>STRONG CASE FOR RATE REDUCTION: Great interest will attach to the section which deals with the relation between the rates and the taxes. The case for a reduction of rates is very strong. The present high rates, which have in- creased with astounding rapidity in the last few years, are not only a burden upon the rate- payer, but a direct obstacle to all improvements in local administration. Though in many dis- tricts there are the reductions in local expendi- ture for Poor Law purposes owing to the Old Age Pensions Act and the Insurance Act, new duties are being imposed upon loud authorities and no adequate resources are available wherewith to ' meet them. Figures are given to show that the rateable values of different distriCts per head of population vary enormously, and that while a, wealthy district has a source of revenue beyond its means, a poor district can only pay its ex- Penses by means of almost intolerable sacri- fices, and in many cases cannot maintain its standard of government aka level which satisfies the conscience of the community. Further, the present system does not secure anything like the necessary degree of central control over local administration. The Report recommends that a further grant from the Exchequer of not less than five millions should be made, and that it •shOuld be distributed in a manner which will take into account the necessities of the poorer districts. It further recommends that all grants from the Exchequer to local authorities should be made strictly conditional upon the perform- ance of their duties in matters such as housing, sanitation, small holdings and the like, and should be withdrawable in whole or in part if their duties were not performed to the satis- faction of the central authority,</p>
        <p>SOURCE OF GOVERNMENT ASSISTANCE. If such an increased grant is to be made, from what source is it to come?</p>
        <p>Although a tax on site values can be advocated by similar arguments to those adduced in favour of a rate on land values, the committee do not think it would be desirable to impose such a national tax in addition to the proposed rate on site values.</p>
        <p>As a weapon in the fiscal armoury a' site tax is fully justified, and if the rating of land values were not adopted it should certainly be imposed. But the question of the simultaneous application of both rate and tax is a different one. The effect of the two proposals taken together would be to impose a very heavy burden on considerable classes of ratepayers. The grant in aid should be raised by other means. There remains the important question of assessment and valuation. The committee re- commend that for the purpose of rates levied upon sites the national valuatiomshould be used as a basis. Further, the Method of assessment should be altered in respect of the rates which are still to be levied upon the composite here- ditament. Instead of the existing system or assessment by Overseers supervised by the Union Assessment Committee, the Report recommends that the Administrative County should be the unit for assessment purposes. Moreover, there should be a re-valuation at least once in every five years.</p>
        <p>Finally, Assessment Appeals to Special and Quarter Sessions shoUld be abolished, and an appeal to the Judicial,Land Commissioners sub- stituted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>ARE WE ALL MAD?</head>
        <p>ARE WE ALL MAD?</p>
        <p>TOM GALLON.</p>
        <p>In reality I suppose it may be generally conceded that we are all mad on one point or another; which is, after all, only another way of saying that each individual one of us refuses to travel always on the clearly de- fined lines of pure reason. That in itself is fortunate, because we should live in rather a monotonous grey sort of world, if none of us ever went off the beaten track.</p>
        <p>Like Hamlet, most of us are only mad when the wind is in a particular quarter—or when some other thing affects us adversely. Dr. Samuel Johnson, who had his weak- nesses, liko smaller men, could not walk along the Fleet Street of his day without touching each post as he walked—even going back, we are told, if inadvertently he missed one; while his friends found it necessary, if they desired to get from him the utterance of any ponderous opinion on men and manners, to sandwich themselves in a way between him and the posts. And we all know how many men there have been who had a rooted anti- pathy to this, that, or the other apparently trivial thing, or a decided aversion to set- ting out linen any enterprise upon what was once regarded as the second holiest day of the week—to wit, Friday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>FROM SUPERSTITION TO CRAZE.</head>
        <p>FROM SUPERSTITION TO CRAZE.</p>
        <p>It is hard to suggest how such prejudices or superstitions first arise in the mind; whether; as some assert, they are born with us, and only develop with our years; or whether they are the outcome of some origi- nal pose or fancy which we have not had the strength of mind to combat. In a sense, we can persuade ourselves to believe anything; can tell a lie times enough to induce our- selves to believe, after, a while, that it is the truth; why should we not therefore bring ourselves to believe that certain childish fancies we once had are actually true, and must be regarded as true? The mild super- stition of the man who laughingly "taps wood" when he boasts may develop into a craze, until that 'man may refuse to do any- thing or to promise anything without thus calling on the fairies for assistance. Personally, I know I have a prejudice against any person—male or female—who has a loud or discordant voice. I cannot get over that prejudice; I may be assured again and again that that person so afflicted is really a very good fellow indeed, and one eminently to be trusted; that his voice is merely the loud voice of the naturally hearty and genial man. Metaphorical:y. I take my- elf by the throat, and shake myself, and say that this is so; and then turn away to some less worthy person, perhaps, with a quiet and gentle fashion of speech.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>BLACK CAT.</head>
        <p>A BLACK CAT.</p>
        <p>I heard of a man once (and I know that there are many like him) who had a horror of cats—especially black ones. He was a big man, who should under the ordinary condi- tions have been afraid of nothing; and I be- he had travelled a great deal, and had had various adventures. But if IV.k happened to be sitting in a room, engiged in casual conversation, and al,black cat strolled in., in the friendliest fashion. ho would grit') the iees of his chair, and his eyes would begin to bulge, and he would presently make o..ti excuse to qet away. And this curious pre- iudiee of his followed him, by the very irony lof. Fate, to his death, and made that death horrible.</p>
        <p>The story was only guessed at by tho.e who knew the man, and who were able te put one piece of evidence against anotker and make out what had happened; but it must have run something like this. The man went on a certain 'voyage, and on a dark night the; vessel on which he was was wrecks, and all bands were lost. All, that is, .r-ve one—the man of whom lam speaking. He must have made a rough raft in some hap- havard fashion, sufficient to support him, and have got on board some hastily secured pro- visions and a little water; from that point of view he was corararatively fortunate. But we may imagine that, with the coming of daylight, he saw crawl out from among the wreckage of -which his raft was built the thing he dreaded most on earth (or on the sca, for the matter of that)—a black cat.</p>
        <p>You have to imagine the situation; to nut yourself in the man's place.. All about him the whole ocean—and floating upon it that tiny square of boards, with himself and the black eat for company. We can imagine him dodging the thing during, the day, and the cat getting nearer, whenever it saw a chance, for the sake of company; and we can see the black night settle down upon him, with the knowledge firmly fixed in a mind getting unhinged with the horror of the situation and of the cat, that that animal would seek him out in the darkness, and snuggle up to him.</p>
        <p>I believe that they found him some days later—raving mad, and with the cat at the further end of the raft, looking at him, and probably wondering what was the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>STRANGE PREJUDICES.</head>
        <p>STRANGE PREJUDICES.</p>
        <p>It would be quite impossible to chronicle all the strange prejudices that people possess. I know a lady who in all general matters is one of the sanest of her sex; she has no fads nor fancies—in fact she is just a wholesome clean nice woman, blessed with more than the average amount of courage. Yet it hap- Psued some time ago that I was with her at an entertainment, where the room was certainly very hot, but where there was no- thing particplarly discomforting in the entertainment itself or in the surroundings. Glancing at her for a moment, I saw that she was white, and her face drawn and ha-ggard: I spoke to her, and merely got a imirmuy in reply—" You must get me out of this; I'm going to faint." As she was not by any nyins of the fainting order, I was rather surprised; however. I got her out, and then. manlike, attributed her fainting to the heat. But of all wonderful things, it had simply been the scent of a flower in a man's coat next to her! assured me had almays tnhate who , 1 flitooo w.heeaxrdps—l aawinn‘Yrsiintl iv: i Ts- tlwan, in fact; yet that particular flower schs, silcirnnla;illtiterl evgmno'esoeltritnimaft riwoii inoneagrr de t oei txabi scrtotlwyrue. u upon occurred, her.and with every appearance of loathing and o whatlo effectthoicnc con- tempt, seen him pass an utter straw": ' clothing: street stranger in e tempt, because the man had on a brown col• oured suit, although of very excellent cut Tie could not account for his dislike to that tint, any more than my friend could account for her strong aversion to that particular flower: yet there you have it. Examine your own character carefully, and see how manic little kinks there are in yourself before you condemn.</p>
        <p>I suppose in the old days—and even in these days, for that matter—religious intol- erance of one sort or another was only a form of prejudice of the more bitter kind. If von objected to a man's religion, or to his belief in however small or large a degree, you go+ hold of him or her, as the case might be, an-3 tried to persuadr him by torturings and maimings, and even by burning; and youT unfortunate victim, equally prejudiced from his or her point of view, allowed you more or less cheerfully to carry on your persuasive efforts, and prided himself or herself on the fact that those efforts were fruitless. AN ANTIPATHY TO TOBACCO.</p>
        <p>Sometimes it happens that that particular prejudice or email madness lays hold on a nunwith such a grip that it is not to ht! shaken off; and then it is no matter fol. laughter. There is a story told of a man— amiable and kindly and respected—who had a rooted antipathy to tobacco. At first he onty Ispoke of the matter earnestly, as he spoke of all things—suggesting that thir friend or that should give up a pernicious habit; then he took to writing to the papers about it; and then the thing obsessed him to such an extent that it became an overmaster- ing passion. Ile was absolutely sane, except on that point; but the mere mention of to- bacco to him turned him into a lunatic. Well, strangely enough, he went one night into a tobacconist's shop. He did not smoke himself; cunningly he got the man who owned the place to talk to him—professed an interest in the premises generally—and pres- ently came away, leaving behind him the impression of a very charming; personality, if an eccentric one. But that night the nlace was burnt to the ground, and the unfortun-</p>
        <p>(OQ/tanned in scut column.)</p>
      </div>
