      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>no WAKES WEEK THIS YEAR.</head>
        <p>no WAKES WEEK THIS YEAR.</p>
        <p>A Town's Meeting Appoint a Committee to Consider the Matter for Next Year. I</p>
        <p>SCENE BETWEEN LABOUR MAN AND COUNCILLOR.</p>
        <p>h Warrington is to have a Wakes Week it will not be instituted this year, for on Tuesday, Mt the adjourned town's meeting convened by the Mayor to consider this question, it WaS de- cided that the year was too far aAvanced to alter the present arrangements. There is yet ku.,e for the promoters of the scheme, for a cons- Mittee is to be formed composed of representa- tives of the Trades and Labour Council and the Ckaniber of Commerce, whose duty it will be to Bee if it is Possible .to make arrangements for —elt year. 41 el.- was almost a unanimity of opinion • i'lat the principle of the whole town stopping a week was a practical proposal, but, as was at the previous meeting, the question reiadved itself into: Which week shall it be? , There- are 'numerous difficulties, some seem- ' • 11113' (insurmountable, to be faced, which, as (Oiiitedlor Roberts pointed out, Would perhaps • raise themselves if Warrington was a town Ingle industry. • n • ENERAL PRINCIPLE ADOPTED. fur Mayor, who presided, said that, judging hag: the replies which had been received Rl' 4 n'er to the circular sent out, it appeared that A'4vtieular week's. holiday was not favoured. kin vesolutMn was moved by the Rev. W. Reid 10°t:t,Ing the general principle of a Wakes Week 11.1. `iiiB year and selecting the week following (liking Day. C. Dukes (secretary Trades and Labour seconded. He said he was instructed ji`,o"'s Council to ask that Wakes Week should -b5 -.4de Walking Day,' or failing that, it should tk Week following Walking Day, as ens- 1n Arr. Reid's proposition. • "NOT PRACTICAL." iiil(,ll* Ur. Roberts: Moved, an amendment that arrangements be not interfered with, iis be did' not think it practicable to ar- till: nnon a week to suit all the manufa- • Re 1-„iLB,til the town. He thought the public 1' were very well satisfied with the pres- -41:1, s " 4 lidays. He appeared for the Tradesmen's i"i00, who had had, a referendum on the tict,,a'ect. They had sent out circulars to the sell in the town, but there had only been rid response, and there was a small major- -11 favour of a Wakes Week. Speaking as an tetrttlidesman, he believed that the town was the' well placed with regard to holidays. All itadltarlting men had their Saturday afternoons Tim the tradesmen and their assistants every liaa-i_NlaY afternoon. They observed all the holidays and others had a week or a t°lll*,-vnight's holiday as well. He was afraid if nte'se,sl),ega_ to alter the arrangements, for the end " year at any rate, they would have no TOt difficulty. Is was seconded.</p>
        <p>AP•nERMAN BENNETT'S VIEWS. I,et,'4lcnan Bennett said he thought it would *kelli€3' to agree to the amendment, because he in favour-of the principle of a Wakes ilathough he thought that the actual fix- tltt " particular week was impracticable. :Ifelitt'lt'd%d not imply they should go to the cf Mr. Roberts's amendment. -They had c 4leto„.ll'Bed the matter at the Chamber of ern- -114,5-,the previous day, and they could not "1 any particular week. J. Jarman said Abe whole idea would lit:" re be abandoned ifothe meeting could not e to e certain week. +l, P. Maaiun said it would be accepted by woetZ°lle that the efficiency of workmen and liec"_,B Were increased by a holiday, and he be- toAle'l it would be better -if the people in the 4.: Could have their holidays together. The I„tilze for the holiday was the week follow- ' Ir4B vvalking Day, which with a number of firms ato, st°aktaking time. The only real .argunient *44148! that week was that it might interfere the annual training of the Territorials. y to :tY their employers would allow them to go allin in August.</p>
        <p>At COULD NOT CLOSE A WEEK. setitera' Lofthouse said that the firm be repre- fiiuk eollld not agree to closing their works sz,ll for a week at any time. They quite to the principle that each of their work- • 11 should have a week's holiday, but lie, per- -41%4'14. lied seen no reason why all the work- -111 should go off together. Ilrotherton (Messrs. Pearson and ilwles) said if the proposed week.was agreed ti(slti'le!.t firm would have to blose for a fortnight. he was sure that was not desired.</p>
        <p>"OUTjr OF THE QUESTION." " E. Parker Said that at the Chamber , 'llN°l‘.lll. 'tierce the previous day there was prac- ;unanimous feeling by those present hie thing was impossible. Speaking for • te tiso eithlllg trade, it was quite out of the ques- • Tr they could not possibly i close down or 14%1,41 as the work reqftcred daily attention. • Consulted his own men, and they were 1Y unanimous against it. They pre- -14,e4, as was the custom at his place, that the o.4sclrrange mutually with .the firm when they RO for their holidays. ai.ll Layton (Messrs. Crosiield's) said that Neith,-1/1 was•not opposed to the principle of a holiday, because they were doing that but the nien - agreed themselves at they preferred to go. The time which '▪ 44 6114 their trade the best would be Whit- t-ne Week. No other time would suit them.</p>
        <p>¼</p>
        <p>r;in which case/they.wonlcl.resnine ,work on Tries- ' day, the sth August, the day after Bank Holi- day. The objections to this holiday being made earlier are</p>
        <p>1. Owing to the fact that the August Bank Holiday is a general holiday for the country, and also that the Territorials take their annual training during that week, the attendance at the schools would be very bad, and would entail considerable loss of grants. 2. The children would be in the schools during the hottest and most tiring part of the year. 3. It would be a great educational disad- vantage to the children of the Secondary and Grammar Schools to, close the schools any earlier than at preaent, as there would be an increasing number of children who would be required to spend part of their holiday's in sitting for the most important .examinations of their careers. 4. Some parents have children attending the Secondary and Grammar Schools lind others attending the elementary schools, and as the holidays are arranged at present they are enabled to have a fortnight in which they can all take their holidays. If, how- ever, the elementary school holidays are made earlier, it will be impossible in these . cases for the families to have their holidays together; and • 5. The Lent term would be considerably shortened, whilst the Christmas term would be unduly lengthened. No change could be made this year, as the holidays have been .announced and the school curricula have been based on the longer period. Again, many of the staff may have made their arrangements for their holidays.</p>
        <p>WEEK FOR EVERY MAN. Councillor Roberts, continuim.;, said that everyone was in favour of the principle of •a week's • holiday, and, more, if possible, 'being given to every working man in the town, but the fixing of any. particular week was thoroughly impossible, due to the multiplicity and varied character of the industries. If they were a town of a single industry,. probably no- difficulty would arise. Pukes: All the statements go to prove the necessity of some arrangement being made. We i seem to be in a state of chaos. .Nothing seems to be arranged systematically; everything seems to rest on individual convenience. There was a more serious difficulty to be met, which goes to support the necessity of a Wakes Week. For example, let them take a forge worker, who had w daughter in the cotton industry and a son in the chemical trade, and under the pres- ent arrangement each member. of that family had to take a holiday wiled the rest ,of .the family were at work. Those cases could be multiplied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>TERRITORIAL DIFFICULTY.</head>
        <p>TERRITORIAL DIFFICULTY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dukes was referring to the Territorial difficulty, when Lieut.-Colonel 13. Fairclough asked if Mr. Dukes was in order.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Dukes: It is in order. Who is respon- sible for bringing it here? I protest it ought not to be so. If a man is to do Service to his country he had to work just as much as he does in the factory.</p>
        <p>. Continuing, Mr. Dukes said that they should consider the question of a holiday from the standpoint of health recuperation. Alderman Tinnion said the idea of the holi- day this year would be bound to result in abso- lute failure, and the.thing would be killed not on its merits, hut because it would not have a chance. They were nearly at the end of April, and the holidays would soon be here.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>NOT THIS YEAR.</head>
        <p>NOT THIS YEAR.</p>
        <p>Mr. Felix Magian said that after Alderman Tinnion's remarks, perhaps it would suit the ease if the propositions Was amended so as to exclude this year, and suggest that the holidays commence next year.</p>
        <p>The amended proposition was defeated; an amendment that a committee be formed to con- sider the arrangements for next year being car- ried by 16 votes to 8. Mr. Lofthouse again declared that he was opposed to a Wakes Week. Following further discussion relative to the composition of the committee, Mr. Dukes said: We have conveniently put the wet bag on it, and it will have ,to come from the undesirable source. I think we ought to inspire the workers with a desire to have a week's holiday, and if it is not convenient, adopt other measures.</p>
        <p>A LIVELY BREEZE. Councillor Roberts: I object to the tone of that speech. We are not going to be threatened by Mr. Dukes, and we are not enemies to the workers in any degree. • Mr. Dukes: I did not suggest you were. Councillor Roberts :. ..Yon suggest we have gone out of our way to kill the movement. Mr. Dukes I suggested that half the men who voted against it are walking out of the room the moment it has been defeated.</p>
        <p>Councillor Roberts: You know perfectly well that every man in the room with the exception of one has pledged himself to the principle of a Wakes holiday.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dukes: That does not get us any nearer. Councillor Roberts : Certainly it does. . Mr. Dukes: I question whether it does. Councillor Roberts: That •is a matter of opinion. Some of us were working in the inter- ests of working men in Warrington before you were born, and will probably be doing so after you have gone. Mr. Dukes: That also is a matter of opinion. COUNCILLOR ROBERTS'S PROTeST.</p>
        <p>Councillor Roberts: I protest against the manner and purport of your remarks. They are not worthy of you or the people whom you represent. Mr.. Dukes: I wish to .say, seeing you have made it a personal matter— Cintncillor Roberts: You made it personal. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Dukes: Personal to whom? charge you. _ . _</p>
        <p>-I did not</p>
        <p>Councillor Roberts: ,Personal to every man who has any feeling in his heart for the People who .a not so 'well off as himself.</p>
        <p>MR. DUKES'S QUERY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pukes: What right have you to answer for anybody else? Councillor Roberts: I am answering for my- self.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dukes: I Wish to protest against the man- ner in which this subject has been treated. Councillor Roberts: Because you have not got your own way all is wrong.. Mr. Dukes: Are you ruling this meeting? Councillor Roberts: No, but I will 'speak against such remarks. Alderman Tinniou, at the conclusion of fur- ther discussion, stated that in the long run the larger firms would determine the question of the holiday. The Mayor proposed that the coMmittee be representative of the Trades and Labour Coun- cil and the Chamber of Commerce, and that they discuss the question of a Wakes Week for • next year. -Mr. Parker: The Trades and Labour Council do not represent the whole of the workers, neither does the Chamber of Commerce repre- sent the whole of the employers.. . The Mayor: That will_ equalise matters. (Loud laughter.) The proposition of the Mayor was ultimately agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>POSITION OF SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>POSITION OF SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>117..1i0-Ij-e-;ts said that at the last meet- -11 regard to the school holidays, he gave 'Which he had since found he was not in giving. Since the last meeting the 444 -var of Education and the school managers taptte:lasidered the whole question, and had rtte,-..d the following statement for 11,m c: Public elementary school holidays ern- " the third Friday in July and terrain- e ii the (41)41 the third Monday in August. The Muni- -I.lSeeendary .School holidays commence on 111;It'litcl Friday in July. The Bootelel• Gram- holidays commence n the last Nil Nay in July. The Oxford ..dFriday f University r"o' • JakriTons are held from the third I\l. `0 tile fourth Saturday of July. The Itok-Illl' Universities Examinatior tsrthe held _ " 4414,0 last Monday in June to seed :,a/ in July. The local Territorials go a_ oway. +lll,,,ueie annual training the first week S."t• cThe present arrangement for the hob l ~ The public ,elementary schools is the re eo,,pipromise, and, whilst it does not the Retliii of the inconvenience with regard.tre- 10r i4R 'lac • Secondly • and Grammar Schools, e"t• 3 4).* f„ - the proposal to have a general bo rt- -17 the full week following upon the ntar Walk- is th 4tril 43't carried out, it will be necessary to, re Ivi_nge the' elemey scbool holidays, or. e 11.: schools will clae on Wednesday before liftt,t7ing Lay, re-open ou the Monday week th' 4114 elose again at the end of ihat week holidays, and result in con- rebhe le miner ,'N4lc i 88 of grant. If the pr he proposed Wakes 'eatB.,"€'d as the week following t Friday i° the Ist July it will follow that the ,eFit datef on which the holidays can corn- " 9: 'it the Jlt: j 9ementary. schools will be on 4110, and they will finish mi. the 29th 1:1:441 oant is a week before Bank Holiday, and 11„-N Ise R"ain for the Bank Holiday. The ."ticlay;l/4e o'n which the elementary school could start would be the 2nd July, •</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>'';zaru</p>
