      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>– – THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 25, 1914.</head>
        <p>- - THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 25, 1914.</p>
        <p>THE EMPLOYERS' VIEW.</p>
        <p>Subject Discussed at the Chamber</p>
        <p>of Commerce.</p>
        <p>The resolution passed in regard to Wakes Week came before the Chamber of Commerce on Monday, when Captain Crosfield presided. The Chairman said the Council of the Cham- ber had discussed the matter and the general feeling was that whilst they welcomed the idea that, workmen( should have a week's holiday some time during the year, they did not wel- come the idea that the whole works should close down at one time, and that it should be left to individual works to arrange their whole holi- days. Lieut-Colonel Fairclough held the view that if the week were fixed it should coincide with the Territorials going to camp, and he (the speaker) heartily supported him in that view. So far as Messrs. Crosfield's were concerned, the only two weeks they could shut down were August Bank Holiday week and Whit-week. They would prefer the former. Mr. F. W. Monks said the first week in August was a crowded week, and it was a most incon- venient time.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: That is a difficulty. Mr. J. A. Ransome : They are trying to over- come it.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: A lot round Manchester go Whit-week.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. G. Roberts: Only Manchester.</p>
        <p>TO GO "WILLY-NILLY."</p>
        <p>Lieut-Colonel Fairclough said the men at their works met and they looked at it from the point of view that whenever they asked for a holiday they got it as near the time, they asked for as possible. If a Wakes Week were chosen people had to go away " willy-nilly." It looked as if it were an attempt on the part of. one portion of the community to interfere with the liberty of the subject, and that was one thing he did not like.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett thought the suggestion a sound one, as he had said before. Mr. Monks said that if the works were closed they would have a chance of doing repairs. Lieut.-Colonel Fairclough : It is all right for those ma position to close their works.</p>
        <p>Mr. Monks: One man found that with the Wakes Week being introduced, his production went up. lie got more out of the 51 weeks than out of the 52.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: That is very encouraging. Mr. Ransome: You find that with piece work, the week before the Wakes Week id a high pay week. It is surprising how much more they can do that week than in an ordinary week. Mr. Monks: That is so.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>BETTER AFTER A HOLIDAY.</head>
        <p>BETTER AFTER A HOLIDAY.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett: After the holiday the men work better. They are healthier. Mr. Monks: That is this man's experience. They have worked better. He said that whilst he entered in the idea with some hesitation he would be sorry now to give it up. _ _</p>
        <p>_ Alderman Bennett: I am very glad to hear that.</p>
        <p>The following resolution was ultimately car- ried: "We approve of the principle that every workman should have a week's holiday, but we think it impracticable to close down all the works for one particular week."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>THREE INQUESTS.</head>
        <p>THREE INQUESTS.</p>
        <p>A Conjurer's Assistant Who Took Poison.</p>
        <p>A CHILD'S TRAGIC FATE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Coroner Brighouse held three inquests at Warrington on Wednesday on the bodies of Thomas Atkinson -(63), hoop-iron bundler, 22, Catherine-street; Alfred Chetter, 21 years, son of Albert Chetter, 12,• Milner-street; and Wil- liam John Barker Ashcroft (31), entertainer, 267, Knutsford-road.</p>
        <p>In the case of Atkinson, Mr. Jenkins (in- structed by Messrs. Henry Greenell and Co.) appeared for Bewsey Forge, where the deceased had been employed. The widow said that two months ago her hus- band came home from work and complained that he had stepped on a piece of hooping, which had sprung up and struck him on the shin, making a bruise. The bruise was bandaged, and four weeks ago a little abscess formed, and her husband went under the care of Dr. Adams. Her husband did not report the matter to the works people. On the 14th inst. he was ordered to bed by D. Adams; suffering from influenza. The leg became very swollen and pneumonia set in, and he died on Sunday morning. Dr. Adams said it was impossible to trace the blood poisoning, to the injury. He thought pneumonia - irtis a contributory to 'lowering the vitality sufficient to allow the septic process to develop. He could not say the wound was the actual cause, but he thought it was the probable cause of poisoning. Death was due to blood poisoning and pneumonia. A verdict in accordance with the medical evi- dence was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>RUN OVER BY CORPORATION CART.</head>
        <p>RUN OVER BY CORPORATION CART.</p>
        <p>The little boy Chetter had been standing near the Fire Station at Messrs. Crosfield's Works, with another child, and as they went across the road the boy Chetter was knocked down and run over by a Corporation tumbler driven by Charles Logan, 6, Stanley-street. The boy was taken into Messrs. Crosfield's ambulance room, where he died almost immediately.</p>
        <p>_ Charles ',Cogan said one of the wheels passed over the child. He had driven horses all his life, and did not know the child was run over until he heard people shouting. .</p>
        <p>„ Evidence to the effect that the driver wag not to blame was given by Sydney Packer, labourer, Plumpton-street, and Charles Dykes, labourer, 342; Knutsford-roitil.</p>
        <p>The. jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death," and stated that no blame whatever at- tached to the driver Logan.</p>
        <p>FOUND DEAD IN A FIELD.</p>
        <p>At the third inquest,. on John Ashcroft, it was stated by William Griffiths, traffic inspec- tor. 'L. and N,.-W. Railway,, that be saw the body of the deceased in field at 5.55 that morn- ing. He went to the dehd 'man and found near by a white bottle, a green bottle and a glass. Rupert Slater, conjurer, 267, .linutsford-road, said the deceased was his assistant, and he met him in, 1906 in Southampton. They came to Warrington 18 months ago. Deceased had taken morphia, and used to inject it in his limbs, but lately had started drinking it. The Coroner: It is worse than drink, and that is bad enough.</p>
        <p>• witness continued that he had pointed out the results of taking the drug, but deceased had said he was worried, and had taken the drug more freely during the past month. Witness saw deceased alive at noon the previous day, and he then seemed in his usual health. Another witness deposed to seeing deceased at noon in Sankey-street, when he said "Hello 1" P.C. Chrimes said that amongst the things found on deceased were a metal watch, with a crucifix and morphia Injector on his watch. chain. He also found the following letter (which the Coroner had difficulty in decipher- ing)•:— " Dear Mr. • Brown,—Forgive me, and God bless you. I told you the tale I did to keep you fro 4 worrying, thinking I could get something or some work. God Almighty look after you, and I do sincerely hope and trust that .my peophi., will look after I. God bless Rupert. You know uncle's address . S--'s busi- ness was not true. See me buried a Catholic."</p>
        <p>Detective-Sergeant Holland said that there was prussic acid in the green bottle and whisky had been in the other bottle. The jury returned a verdict to the effect that Ashcroft died from prussic acid poisoning, but there was not sufficient evidence io show the state of his mind.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>ADULT SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>THE ADULT SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Plinston and the Work of the</p>
        <p>Education Committee.</p>
        <p>MULTIFOLD DUTIES.</p>
        <p>At the Sankey Bridges Men's meeting on Sun- day afternoon the Current Events Class was brought to a close for the season by an address .by Photon on " The Work of *Local, Education Authority." He pocrited'otit the great changes which had been brought about in the administration of education affairs by the Bal- four Act of 1902, dealing more particularly with an area like Warrington. Mr. Plinston then described the constitution of the local Educa- tion Authority and the way in which the work of the various committees was carried out. The speaker then dealt in a very lucid and interest- ing manner, with some of the problems which the various committees had to deal with, such as medical inspection, dental clinics, feeding of children, exemption, "blind alley" employ- ment. Dealing with the question of medical inspection, Mr. Plinston said that there was a good deal of prejudice and opposition against it. but as the parents were beginning to realise its value the opposition was decreasing. With regard to school exemption the speakel said this was a most difficult problem, and the ratepayers generally did not realise what trouble and care the responsible committee, gave to the question. Mr. Plinston said that circumstances differ so much—on the one hand there was the desire to do the best for the child, on the other there was the position of the parents; in many cases absolutely in need of the small weekly sum the child could earn. It was downright cruel that a smart boy, for instance, should be put to running errands, often ending as an unskilled labourer. Cases had to be considered:very care- fully on their individual circumstances, and often it was not easy to decide what was best for the child. Dealing with the feeding of school children, Mr. Plinston said it was proved beyond doubt that it wa.s not only useless, but cruel, to try to teach a child that was in a chronic state of hunger.</p>
        <p>There can be no doubt that the winter's work orthis class has been beneficial in giving to the members a higher conception of true citizenship, and a better and clearer,conception of public affairs and the work of the various public bodies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>BONSEY MEN'S.</head>
        <p>BONSEY MEN'S.</p>
        <p>One of the members on Sunday morning read a paper on "Worship." Worship was described ,as " the act of rising to a personal, experimental consciousness of the real presence of God." Reli- nmeeeetciellg of the soul with God. If ases in the world, religion, in. ntitin, is doomed. We might still ightthia:avntefireiissxletaehisrneiteaebout the God whom,man once knew intimately, and we 'night still continue to work for human betterment, but there could be living religion only so long as the soul of man is capable of experiencing the fresh bubbling of the living water within, and could know for himself that a heart of eternal love beats in the central deeps of the universe. To give p the cultivation of worship meant i u n the long run the loss of the central thing in religion. We need to constantly affirm that such a relationship to God is the essence of religion, that the greatest of all truths is that the Divine Spirit comes into indepen- dent and direct contact with every human spirit, and that in proportion to the obedience of that soul it will be led to see ever more clearly the truth of God in Christ and be filled with spiritual power for service. Thus the human spirit becomes dependent for its highest results upon. ,communion with God. Character would then have some principle for its foundation. Worship will help men to a consciousness of a baptism of the spirit and life.</p>
        <p>ACADEMY-STREET MEN'S. "The Fatherhood of God and Brotherhood of Man" was the subject of an address by Mr. W, Evans on Sunday morninz,„ Man is responsible entirely for the misery and suffeiing he is sub- jected to, and God is not to blame. Selfishness and greed—the opposite of God's law—cramps and curses not only the material, but the spiritual life of mankind. A truly religious per- son has the happiness and comfort of others at heart, and endeavours to secure brighter lives for those around him, for it is not due altogether to ourselves that w 6 are not also in the "gutter"; heredity and environment have played their part. Man, however, can rise' above his " original" surroundings, and there are innum- erable opportunities to give a helping hand and a spirit of encouragement in the struggle.* True Christians will mingle with the world, fight shoulder to shoulder with their fellow-men and be true comrades, sharing their pleasures as well as their work in the different spheres of life.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>LONGFORD.</head>
        <p>LONGFORD.</p>
        <p>An address was given on Sunday at the above school by Mr. Hampson., Basing his remarks On the lesson for the day, namely, " Life,6, Basis," he dealt with his subject very admir- ably, and was listened to . with interest. A. short debate followed, in which several members of the class. took part, this ,bringing to a close a most-interesting Sunday morning.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>CYCLING CLUB.</head>
        <p>CYCLING CLUB.</p>
        <p>The Adult School C.C. mustered 19 on Satur- day and visited Pickmere, via Arley Park, Puncture trouble reduced the leisure at the lake, hut several members availed themselves of an hour's row, while one of, the party could not resist mechanical propulsion, to wit, motor launch. The puncture element reasserted itself at dusk, whereby the return journey, via Antro- bus, was unavoidably prolonged. However, every member enjoyed this favourite little spin, and a good attendance was , promised for Rostherne to-day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>SHIP CANAL INCIDENT.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL INCIDENT.</p>
        <p>Owners' Action in Court.</p>
        <p>The sequel to an incident on the Ship Canal at Warrington was heard in Liverpool on Thurs- day. The owners of the steamer Ussa, of Liver- pool, sued the owners of the steamer Sanwarine, of the port of Manchester, to recover damages in respect of the carrying away of a mooring chock on the former vessel. The plaintiffs' case, which was conducted by Mr. A. T. Miller (instructed by Messrs. Bate, sons, Warr, and Wimshurst) was that shortly after ten o'clock on the morning of j„he 18th last the Lissa was lying moored in the Man- chester Ship Canal at what is known as the Warrington lay-by. She was heading to the aseastward,dischargingjaictiht w cargo of timber. The San- warine,proceeding in the direction h port side to the wharf, and w of Eastham Locks, passed the Ussa at what was 'alleged to be an excessive speed—viz., from six to eight knots—and it was contended that the Ussa and her moorings were thereby endangered, for the Ussa began to range at her moorings, l and the aft mooring chock on her port bow carried away, causing the damage which formed l.the subject of ,the present action. Negligence was alleged on the part of those in charge of the , Sanwarine, and a considerable body of evidence was called in support of the plaintiffs' case. Hyslop prsMaanxd nwelilh who had been briefed by Messrs. R et for the defendants, denied that they or their servants had been , guilty of any negligence in the navigation of the been iSanwarine, caused t o roe ra w that the s damageasoccasioned alleged to have in the way suggested. He called evidence to show that ' on the date named the Sanwarine was proceed- ing down the Ship Canal, towards Eastharer Locks, in charge of a duly licensed canal pilot, and passed the Warrington lay-by, where the Ussa was lying moored. At this time the speed of the Sanwarine was only about three or four , knots .an hour, which, counsel contended, was a perfectly safe speed, and as she passed the Ussa, which was lying moored With her stern toward the canal, and just about clear of the Canal, those on .board the Sanwarine observed that the mooring ropes of the Ussa were slack. The Sanwarine passed the Ussa quite safely. , In' the result, the Court found that the Ussa was properly moored and that the damage was occasioned by the Sanwarine proceeding at too great a speed tinder the circumstances. There would conseciuently, said his Honour, be judg- ment for plaintiffs. - _ -</p>
        <p>- Mr. Miller said they would agree to take judg- ment for .t 37 on his Honour's finding, and so save further expense.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED] A COMEDY OF THE SALVAGE COURT. •</head>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED] A COMEDY OF THE SALVAGE COURT. •</p>
        <p>The case before the Court relates to sal- vage, and it is unusually interesting. A derelict, a timber-laden barque, his been abandoned in the North Sea, and has been, salved and taken in tow by a little tramp steamboat from the Thames. When the tow has; begun it is discovered that the derelict has not been wholly abandoned, for the mas- ter unexpectedly appears from belowAand not only demands that the tow-rope shalr be cast off, but also refuses to employ the steamboat, declaring that he and -s,t.ivitt• other men who have remained with ,the ship. can take ,her safely into port. The steamboat master vows that be will not relinquish what he has got, and he takes his prey safely into London River, which is her destination. Now the Court has to decide between the parties, ship against ship, master against master. THE IMPERTURBABLE SKIPPER. The steamboat skipper is in the box, under rigid cross-examination. He is imperturb- ably cheerful, and glances confidently at times from the presiding judge to the Trinity Masters who are hearing the case with his lordship. "You seem to find the case amusing," ob- serves counsel at last, baffled in his efforts to discredit or disconcert the witness.</p>
        <p>`U do," the skippca. answers, "and for many reasons. If you'd seen the way that Old Man came on deck from somewhere near the ballast and heard the yells he gave when he saw us walking off with his old barque, why, you would see the humour of it, too." Pct.:laps," says the counsel caustically, "you will favour us with your reasons for taking such a light view of a very serious matter." "Well, sir, that isn't as easy as it looks. You can't always bring to the front Y'hat you have at the back of your mind. But just take your•fancy out to that part of the North gale of wind blowing, a nasty sea running, Sea where we picked up the old barque. A this old ship looking as forlorn as an old maid that's got left, without a sign of life about her, and her bits of canvas blown to shred. We make a rope fast and just get her nicely underway for' London River when a chap jumps up from below and so to speak shouts "Stop thief!" " But solely," counsel protests, "you do not '..in that box deliberately suggest that having taken possession of a ship which after all, is not abandoned, and is not a derelict, you have any right whatever to hold on and force your servces upon the unfortunate master ?" "There are times," rejoins the witness, "when men at sea have to be saved in spite of themselves. And this was one a them." "And why so ?" asks counsel. "Why. so ?" repeats the witness, "because. if you must know, he was no more capable of salving that barque than, say, yourself. He. was blind to the world—and that's why he never knew what we were doing till he felt the old windjammer punching into it 011 her way to London River. Why, sir, if a ship,ever talked and begged for mercy, it was that old hooker that we saved, and so far haven't got even a thank you for it." "And," replies counsel, "I shall be sur- prised if the acknowledgement of your services goes beyond that recognition. you may stan.d down, sir." The fray has now begun in real earnest, and the fight is waged throughout the day with warmth on both ski:se. The case is re- markable and in the hearing of it there is a marked ahsince of that boredom which is characteristic of the majority of civil cases in our courts of law.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>MATE'S EVIDENCE.</head>
        <p>THE MATE'S EVIDENCE.</p>
        <p>On behalf of the plaintiffs let the mate be called. He enters the box—for the first time in his life—and leans over the edge of it very much as he might lean over the edge of the bridge during his watch at sea. He is a young man of slight build, lithe of aspect, resolute and resourceful of manner. His ex- amination-in-chief goes off with flying colours, and in the innocence of his heart he considers that his side has won. The gentle- man in the wig and gown has been so very gracious and helpful. 7But the mate is disa- bused when the hostile barrister rises and gives him a far wren handling. than he has accorded even to the master—for this ,reason, the master has been cool and collected, while the mate has been trapped more than once into somewhat damaging admissions. "-I have a suspicion,' declares counsel, " that something is being kent back." "Really," observes his lorelip, still mild- ly, "I have not noticed anything to justify such a suggestion." " That," remarks the master, in an aud- ible whisper, "is what I call a proper sort of judge.' A moment later, when the mate has resumed his seat beside him, he whispers again, but the purport of what he says is, known only to the pair of them. They bnve, evidently some secret which they are intense- ly anxious to keep to themselves. They look anxiously towards the box when their next witness enters, and still more ner- , yousily, vklhen . the cross-examination begins, for this is the chie.f engineer, and it is ap- parent that in order to sustain himself the chief has been taking evaporative refresh- ment. He is renowned in certain ports bor- dering on the Narrow Seas for the *buoyancy and freedom of his speech in such circum- stances. To their vast relief the chief has profited by the example of the other witnesses, and maintains a discretion which is positivoly owlish. "I suggest," snaps counsel, "that this ease is something in the character of a friendly conspiracy—that perhaps tramping has 'not been very profitable, and that this is in the nature of a scheme to make money out of the misfortunes of your fellow men.'' "In whicfi oasq,'' • replies the engineer, "It seems to me that there isn't Much to choose between the sea and the land. But that is not the truth—this baroue was to all intents and purposes adandoned, and if we had not taken her in tow she would have been completely lost." "Se von are basing your claim to some extent On humanity ?" "No, on something rather stronger than that,_personally I am basing any bores on salvage. I can assure You, sir, that the barque dragged so much that you might have thought she was going to pull the very vitals out of us. And as for the coal we burnt—Why, thero wasn't a shovelful left when we 'dropped anchor. And it's the best Welsh!" Even the master blushes, for it is a stand- ing grievance of the steward that no living man can keen the tramp's saloon, which is aft, free of filth from the f-nnel when she 's steaming, because of the rubbish with which the boilers are fed. THE JUDGMENT.</p>
        <p>All the speeches have been heard, all the witnesses have been disposed of, and there is nothing left hut to listen to the observations of the judge and wonder what the finding of the Court will be. The m.a.ster, the mate, and the engineer lean forward eagerly and the master at any rate has no fear of the result. He despises the law, and pins his faith to common-sense. The judgment is delivered at last. It is still given mildly and placidly, as if it were of no more moment to the judge and the Court than a second egg for breakfast. It is that unless a ship has been entirely aban- doned the salvors are compelled to give lip the charge of her to the master, who can de- cline to employ the would-be salvors further.</p>
        <p>The case is thus hopelessly against the plaintiffs. The defeated master, mate and engineer depart from the court, to all appearances bearing their ' downfall bravely; but ou reaching their own steamboat, which is lying at one of the wharves bard by Tower Bridge, they lock themselves in the saloon which is frequently defiled by Welsh oal. Prom a locker in his own state-room the skipper produces a bag, which is obviously related to their closely guarded secret. "Fill UT). boys " he says in a low voice. "and let's wish luck to the division or tile bit of money we were lucky enough to lay hands on. It's a mercy we've got surne- thing for our labour—which is more than we should have done if we'd trusted in that mean old skunk of a law-court.- This case has taught me one big lesson, and that is, don't nut your trust in ths law. That iudr74.</p>
        <p>(Continued in next column.)</p>
        <p>Q.—K in dI y explain, if God is trying to convert the world and apparently failing, why does He not exert His former power (Psalm xxxiii. 9), " He spake and it was done; He commanded and it stood fast," &amp;c. (TRUTHSEEKER.)</p>
        <p>A.—ln harmony with the thought expressed I in the text you quote (Psalm xxxiii. 4), God never tries to do anything! That which He purposes is always fulfilled; so if God, had I determined that the world should be converted I during this Gospel Age, He would have suc- ceeded. The majority of Christians believe that God desires to save all men, but His power is limited by that of the Church. This is an im- proper limitation of the Divine power, for the Scriptures state clearly that "His arm is not shortened that it cannot save." They declare that His word "Shall accomplish that which He pleases." Our Lord said: "I pray not for the world" (John xvii. 9), and again: "I thank thee, Father, Lord of Heaven and earth, that thou haat hid these things from the wise (worldly) and prudent, and has revealed them unto babes" (Matt. xi. 25). These Scriptures, as well as the facts of the matter demonstrate that God is not now seeking to save the NVor'ld indeed, we learn that the unconverted popula- tion has more than doubled within the past cen- tury, so that under present conditions their salvation never could be accomplished. It is clearly a work of the future to be undertaken by our Lord when He returns in kingdom, power and glory. The present purpose of God is stated in Acts xv. 14-18, to be the selection of a class to be associated with the Lord in that work of blessing the world of mankind with a knowledge of God and judging them in righteousness.</p>
        <p>what sense did Abraham look for a heavenly country (Hebrews xi. 16)? (P.D.W.) A.—We are to understand that the heavenly country for which Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob and all the faithful of the fleshly house of Israel waited as pilgrims and strangers is after all to be earthly, in the sense of being on the earth; but that it will be heavenly in the sense that its government, regulations, laws, &amp;c., will be heavenly, and not " earthly, sensual, devil- ish." The inheritance promised the Jews was Canaan, in Asia (Gen. ail. 1-5), and St. Stephen informs us (Acts vii. 5) that they are still wait- ing to receive it from God's hands.</p>
        <p>Q.—Some claim that the Apostle Paul was a married man, and that he married an Ethiopian, and the thorn in the flesh was his domestic troubles. Was Paul married at any time before pr after he became a Christian? (N.M.) A.—Neither sacred history nor tradition offers any answer to your question—we presume because the information would be of no value to</p>
        <p>the Christian. The grounds for deciding as to whether one shall marry or not are entirely per- sonal. The Apostle, or anyone else, could not be taken as an example in this. He informs us, however, that they that marry do well, but they that marry not do better, having the greater opportunities of giving their services and affec- tions to the Lord (I. Cor. vii. 3/3).</p>
        <p>Q.—What object did Jesus have in making two visits to the earth? (V.W.)</p>
        <p>A.—A full examination of God's revealed plans will give a 'broad view of the chief object of both the first and the second advents; and we should remember that both events stand related as parts of one plan. The specific work of the first advent was to redeem men; and that of the second is to restore, and bless and liberate the redeemed. Having given His life a ransom for all, our Saviour ascended to present that sacri- fice to the Father, a reconciliation for man's iniquity. He tarries and permits the " prince of the world" to continue the rule of evil, until after the selection of the Bride. The Lamb's Wife, to be accounted worthy of such honour, must overcome the influences of the present evil world. Then the work of giving to the world'of mankind the great blessings secured to them by His sacrifice will be due to commence, and He will come forth to bless all the families of the earth. True, the restoring and blessing could have commenced at once, when the ransom price was secured by the Redeemer, and then the com- ing of Messiah would have been but one event, the reign .and blessing beginning at once, as the Apostles at first expected (Acts i. 6). But God laad provided "some better thing for us"— the Christian Church (Heb. xi. 40); hence it is to our interest that the reign of Christ is separated from the sufferings of the Head by these eighteen centuries.</p>
        <p>Q.—Do the present conditions—social, poli- tical, and ecclesiastical—suggest that prophecy is being fulfilled with regard to the "fulness of the Gentiles"? • (G.E.8.)</p>
        <p>A.—lsrael as a nation did not accept Jesus as the Messiah, only a few did this (John i. 11, 12). Consequently their lack of faith blinded them, and they as a nation will remain in this condi- tion qntil a number from the " Gentiles " has been selecte to make up the full number origin- ally decided upon by God. When this has been accomplished, then Israel will recover their mental sight—be saved from their blindness, and a New Covenant will be made with them "when their sins are taken away" (Rom. xi. 25-27). The present conditions in the world suggest that we are nearing the end of the Gentile times, as the Lord said that this would be indicated by distress and perplexity upon the earth (organ- ised society), a mass of ungovernable people, whose actions and restlessness are compared to the turbulent waves of the sea. The powers of heaven (rulers) would not be so strong and influ- ential, but would be shaken by adverse winds of strife, and then they shall discern that the Son of Man is appearing to take His great power and reign (Luke xxi. 25-27).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>SHORTHAND PRACTICE IN PRAYER,</head>
        <p>SHORTHAND PRACTICE IN PRAYER,</p>
        <p>BOOKS.</p>
        <p>The Knutsford Parish Church officials have long endured, annoyance and loss through. the mutilation of prayer and hymn books. At the vestry meeting further complaints were made that the books were defaced in a variety of ways, budding shorthand writers being among the culprits. In' some instances the writing was of " unparliamentary" character, and officials had to destroy portions of many of the books. It was stated that if the defilers could be traced prosecution would follow.</p>
        <p>hasn't mayea tne game—and as for those Trinity Masters " "I'll say it for you," interjects the engin- eer. which he does with very great effect. "I nipped up this bag of money," adds the skipper, "when I went below in the barque just before I saw the Old Man vane from somewhere near the ballast. You heard him say in the witness-box that he thought the ratters-out • had gone off with the bag? Well, it would be criel to disturb his con- viction—especially aa the money belonged to that old Hebrew who got away in the boat. There's enough when it's shared to help to keep us all straight. And so here's to no more salvager It doesn't pay! It's a lot more profitable to hell, Yourself-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>DOCTORS GAVE HER UP.</head>
        <p>DOCTORS GAVE HER UP.</p>
        <p>She Was Finally Saved by Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable Compound—Her Story Interesting.</p>
