      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Thurth</head>
        <p>Thurth</p>
        <p>- PRIMITIVE METHODIST SUNIJtY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY.</p>
        <p>The anniversary of the Legh-street Primitie Methodist Sabbath School took place on Sunday. Two sermons were preached by the Rev. R. Hall, of Bolton. At the morning service the text chosen was Mark xiv. 9. Christ's estimate of true service, Mr. Hall said, was oftentimes opposed to the general estimate, and if we wouid serve Him in our own way—be ourselves—even though the world pronounced against it, if cur service, was in sincerity and guided ly uiity of motive, it would receive etern.d recognltion, and bring joy to our own hearts. At the evening service the text was "Bring forth the best robe, and put it on him." The preacher declared with emphasis that no robe was equal to the robe of Christianity. Ile would not say there were no good men outside the pale of -the Churches, and its agencies, but it could not be denied the noblest characters were found to be members of the Christian Church. Then the preacher contrasted the various robes and drew lessons from the wearing of. them. The Christian robe became better and more beautiful and strengthened those who wore it in their efforts for good. The athletes were often watched in their efforts, and men said Alt! but you should have seen them in theii. palmy days." They are losing now; the Chris- tian's palmy days were the succeeding days, not the past, but the future. The preacher also said Christ was willing to give •tins hest robe in ex- change, even if need be, for a robe that was of the worst—a robe that would be eternal in lieu of a robe of time and decay.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>MUSICAL SERVICE.</head>
        <p>MUSICAL SERVICE.</p>
        <p>In the afternoon Mr. John Taylor presided at the service, the chief feature of which was an Exercise Service, entitled " Lessons from names," given by Mrs. Hoyle and the scholars. Twelve of the scholars fixed names in a frame and gave lessons from the meaning of the follow- jag: Hilda, Margaret, Dorothy, Rhoda, Olive. Mary, Richard, Walter, Robert, Alfred, William and Joseph. The placing of the names was interspersed with suitable hymns from the Sab- bath School hymn book, rendered by ail the scholars. The twelve parts were taken by Gladys Joyce, Dorothy Griffiths, Lillian Minis. worth, Annie Edwards, Minnie Hodgson, Francei: Brett, Charles Lander, David Fraser, Harry Trepass, Alfred Oultram, Norman Snape and Joseph Kitchen. Elsie Ashworth sang a hymn as a solo. The scholars had been trained by the Superintendent (Mr. B. T. Parkes), assisted by Miss Elsie Spruce, and very credit Ably rendered the service.</p>
        <p>The children of the school and the choir ren- dered special hymns at each service, and a choral march, " Warriors of the Lord." The children had been trained by Mr. A. Spruce, who ccm- ducted, the organist being Mr. F. Banner. Tho financial result created a record. A special ar- poal had been issued to the members of the • church and congregation, .and they responded in a liberal, manner, and the collections realised .C5O 4s. id. During the week previous to the anniversary a platform was erected and given te the school for use in future years.</p>
