        <p>~ .. The nation has been compelled to witness some strange devices on the part of the Unionist party of late, but ~- when Mr. BONAR LAW, the nominal head ,: of that party, accuses the PRIME MINISTER of having made false state- ments in the House, we are about reach- ing the dregs of political controversial ~' methods. What Mr. LAW really means : is that Mr. ASQUITH will not condescend ~ to admit that what Mr. LAW alleges is '' true, and when one is in the straits ! in which Mr. LAW finds himself we have to be charitable and make allowances for the workings and misleadings of passion. To a certain point Mr_ LAW might be forgiven, but he has reached the point where all decent-minded men will rule him out of their minds as impossible. To do him justice from another point of view, it has to be remembered that he is the spokesman of a party almost bereft of reason - because they have had to stand aside to make room for better men for eight years, and are apparently condemned to stand aside for at least another eight. For, whatever the shortcomings of the Liberal party may be, the country is not likely to change that party for the Unionists. The memory of the days immediately preceding 1906 is too fresh as yet for that. When a party is bundled • into Opposition so unceremoniously as the Tories were at the end 111- of their long and tedious reign, there is , generally a reason for it. The present .t..' Government have been demonstrating what that reason was ever since they, came into power. The Unionists had ' become stale and helpless—reactionary methods have nothing to live upon. The Progressives got to work, did some- thing, and are planning fresh ventures to make up for the barrenness of those two sad decades that followed 1886. Even after a rest of eight years the Tories have no programme to offer to the country, and their " leader " is reduced to calling men liars whose shoes he is not fit to lace. 11 is vulgar, but it is not politics, and the country will doubtless recognise that fact with some emphasis when the next election comes round. The country sees that there is only one- party competent to rule until such time as the other has produced a few leaders with a soul that will rise superior to the gutter. If the Conservative party wish to try to live and thrive upon mendacity they are welcome to make the attempt, but ,?, . their brazen-faced effort to make it 1 , 1: appear that it is the other side - that is doing the lying must be resented. Happily the alle- gations can be ,easily refuted. We are living in days of positive topsy- turvydom. An illegal " Army " makes its appearance, with politicians at its head, and the KING'S Government is accused of provocation if the slightest move is made to checkmate that. " Army "; the Government take precau- tionary measures, and those who were cowards yesterday, ,who dared hardly breathe, are to-day unbearable bullies; Unionists intrigue with the KING'S Army officers, and " ollicial "statements are issued in the name of a 'provisional Government, while the KING'S Govern- ment is accused of treachery. Now,. when the Kirin's first Minister gives a plain, honest statement of the case he is told he is a liar. . It is time such ' buffoonery came to an end, and Mr. • ASQUITH'S' generosity in giving the Opposition two days next week to in- quire into his honesty and truthfulness , ought to settle the matter. 'We say I ought to, but past experience teaches 1 us to expect the Opposition to cOme to 1 , the charge again and again. They are • not seeking the truth, but mud-throw- ing. They are simply unteachable. ] GENERAL RAGET'S statement of what I the Government intended .and what he ! t ,Ir</p>
        <p>actually did with reference to ought to lay the plot canard. Mr. BONAR LAWand his friends will doubt- less persist; but. for men who are not utterly blinded by political jaundice and are not entirely overwhelmed by</p>
        <p>„, F„ • ,tiv„,:,..</p>
