      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <p>the calls of party expediency, the White I Paper will be amply convincing.l GENERAL PAGET had instructions to pro- vide for the guarding of certain places against risk of attack. Ile thought such a movement would precipitate a crisis, and requested that sufficient troops should be at hand should the situation develop. He never authorisied the put- ting of questions to subordinate officers. What GENERAL PAGET did was perfectly right and in order. The true perspec- tive of the events which the heated imaginations of the Opposition have distorted into a plot is afforded by a letter from Col. Sir FREDERIC CARDEW to the "Morning Post." He writes :</p>
        <p>"As an old soldier I would say this, that if Sir ARTHUR PAGET, the General Officer Commanding the Troops in Ire- land, had not made certain dispositions ifor the protection of military armaments and stores which were exposed to attack, and taken military precautions for the movement of troops in the event of an outbreak on the tart of the Ulster Volunteers, he would not have been fit for his position."</p>
        <p>What the Opposition wish is to set up this position: Orangemen are to be allowed to take the field; the KING'S Army is not to be used against these Orangemen; "Ulster," with an "army" at its back, is to defy the Government and its measures; and so Liberalism is to be vetoed as in the old days of the House of Lords' supremacy. It is a nice arrangement, but Democracy has travelled too far now to be dragooned by this second edition of Mr. LAW'S "new methods."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>TIIE TORIES AND TIIE FARM LABOURER.</head>
        <p>TIIE TORIES AND TIIE FARM LABOURER.</p>
        <p>Lord SALISBURY on Tuesday brought forward a motion which he fondly hoped would take the wind out of the sails of the Liberal land campaign. He was quite prepared to admit that agri- cultural labourers ought to be paid better. But he deprecated such horrid things as trade unionism on gentle- men's estates, or a compulsory mini- mum wage. Why should not those nice and kind people, rural landlords, meet those worthy but rather stupid people, farm labourers, and talk over the wage question? Of course, it never occurred to Lord SALISBURY that, just as labourers who have applied for allot- ments have been marked men, labour- ers who suggested that the average wage in a district was low might also hear of it afterwards. Lord Lucns, for the Government, gave some cold and keen statistics showing that low wages are depopulating the country-side; and Lord CREWE ridiculed the suggestion that the land taxes have hampered the building of cottages. The Lords had quite an interesting discussion on the subject, which revealed the grave anxiety of the Tory party at Mr. LLOYD GEORGE'S land campaign, and their realisation that it has raised questions which cannot be put aside, however much they would desire them to be shelved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>CINEMA CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>CINEMA CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>Medical Officer's Views on a Vexed</p>
        <p>Subject.</p>
        <p>NEW REGULATION WANTED.</p>
        <p>The Medical Officer for Warrington Schools, Dr. G. W. Joseph, in his annual report, issued yesterday, deals with the subject of school chil- dren attending cinema exhibitions. He deals at length with the subject, and inter alia says: The main points to be considered are the possibility of the spread of infectious and con- tagious diseases; the effect on the vision of school children; the effect of certain sanitary de- fects in the premises themselves on the general health of the school child; the effect of late hours.</p>
        <p>--- -- Over and over again it is found that children who are excluded from school suffering from severe impetigo and other contagious skin diseases attend these picture shows and there mix with healthy children. This is undoubtedly wrong, as the only reason in most instances for their exclusion from school is to prevent the spread of the disease. When we consider the infectious diseases, how- ever, the condition of things is still worse. Children found at school to be suffering from "sore throats" or suspected by the teacher to be suffering from any infectious ailment, are at once excluded. Under existing conditions there is nothing to prevent these children at- tending places of entertainment and there dis- seminating the disease, which it was hoped to prevent by closing the school.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>DEFECTS IN VISION.</head>
        <p>DEFECTS IN VISION.</p>
        <p>In Warrington there is no doubt that defects in vision are becoming more frequent in chil- dren during school age, and to a certain extent cinematograph exhibitions may be one of the causes. If no ill-effects are to be produced on members of the audience, every effort 'must be made to have the places of entertainment satis- factory from a sanitary point of view. The majority of picture halls are inadequately pro- vided with means of ventilation, especially dur- ing performances. Sufficient sanitary accom- modation must be provided and this should be so situated as to be separated by an interven- ing ventilated space from the body of the ball.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>NEW REGULATIONS WANTED.</head>
        <p>NEW REGULATIONS WANTED.</p>
        <p>With regard t the effect of late hours on school children, combined with the accessory physical excitement, it is noteworthy that in many instances teachers report that they can Pick out from their pupils those who regu- larly attend picture shows at night by their listlessness in school in the mornings. A regulation of the Watch Committee in November, 1912, "That the proprietors of all Premises in the borough licensed for public per- formances be informed that in the opinion of this committee it is undesirable that children Of tender years should be admitted to any even- ing performances unless such children are in charge of an adult person," refers to children under eight years of age, and advantage would be gained by extending this to include all chil- dren of school age. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
