      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 25, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 25, 1914.</p>
        <p>THE PASSING WEEK.</p>
        <p>PEACE AT FLETCHER, RUSSELL'S.</p>
        <p>We are more than pleased to say that the protracted strike at the works of Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co. has come to a satis- factory end, and that the men return to work upon terms that are agreeable to both sides. In this column last week we urged upon the col-17 teStlints the wisdom of making a real common- seMe effort to settle the dispute. Such an effort was made on Wednesday and Thursday, with the result that we now announce. The next thing to be done is for the parties to the struggle to forget past causes for soreness and to pull together for permanent peace. A thousand men out of work and an establish- ment standing idle for eight consecutive weeks is a state of affairs that ought not to exist with- out the gravest of reasons.</p>
        <p>REIAR OF FORMER WARRINGTON 1 BRIDGES. The demolition of Warrington Bridge has brought to light some unexpected relics of the past. Chief among them, and decidedly the most interesting, is the base of ono of the piers of the stone bridge built by Lord Derby in 1499. The pier is the one nearest to the Cheshire side. After four centuries of concealment in Mersey mud it has once more been uncovered to the light of day. In many respects this piece of masonry has value and interest. It reveals the shape of the large piers, and the project- ing cut-water, which the old bridge builders never omitted from their design. It gives us I also the approximate width of the structure over which Henry the Seventh rode upon the opening day. The antiquarian will find upon the stones the ancient mason's marks, consist- ing of diamonds, crosses and dashes, the sym- bols by which each man's work could be iden- tified. •</p>
        <p>We understand that part of the pier is to be removed and re-erected in one of our parks. We offer the suggestion that the stones should be so laid as to show the size of the pier with its two projecting cut-waters. So laid in the form of a low wall, the old stones would admir- ably serve to enclose a flower bed, and in this form would tell their story to future genera- tions.</p>
        <p>Yet another stone pier has been uncovered, which appears to have supported the wooden bridge which for a short period spanned the river. Miscellaneous timbers, some of them of massive osak, blackened by long contact with mud and water, are judged to have formed the main timbers' of the wooden bridge. Even more interesting are remnants of an ancient oaken fender, which the 15th century builder sunk into the stream to keep back the mud and water.</p>
        <p>We hope it may be possible to identify these fragments, and if some of our local anti- quarians can reconstruct them—if only on paper —there are many who will be grateful.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>MR. A. J. JOLLEY.</head>
        <p>MR. A. J. JOLLEY.</p>
        <p>Our congratulations are extended to Mr. A. J. Jolley on his appointment to the position of chairman of the Board of Guardians. Mr. Jolley is usually looked upon as a "hard man," but he keeps one standard before him, and that is efficiency. He will make a good chairman, and sentiment will have little power over his comings and goings.</p>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CAMP. Though the Children's Summer Camp is thriving, as is shown by the accounts sub- mitted' to the annual meeting this week, there is need for further help lithe usefulness of the institution that now gives a seaside holiday to 500 of Warrington's poorest children is to be maintained. Those who have enough for their Wants and a little to Spare could not do better than inquire into the work that is being done by Councillor W. H. J. Jenkins and the friends Who are associated with him.</p>
        <p>SHALL WE Boom? ' Would it be wise to boom Warrington in order to attract works to make the place larger? That is a question discussed at length in another part of this issue. There are more sides than one to the problem, and it is clearly a case in which we should look before we leap.</p>
        <p>S</p>
        <p>CHOIR BOYS. There are choir boys and choir boys, and curious are the facts that come to light respect- ing them when the virtues—and the contrary—. of those connected with the parish are revealed at the Easter vestry meetings. The "Carlisle Journal" reports the Vicar of St. John's, Car- lisle, to have said, in moving the adoption of last year's accounts :</p>
        <p>About .£9 was saved through not paying the choir boys, and the result had been most satisfactory. It is a vague kind of statement. We' are not told whether the boys were satisfied with the stoppage of payment, or whether they struck work, and the congregation were satisfied with the absence of their singing. We are left in the dark on these points, though the report appears to point to the fact that all the Vicar meant was that the saving had had a satisfac- tory effect upon the parochial balance sheet. . " Punch " says the "note of satisfaction in the choir boys" voices is said to be very touch- ing," indicating that some people look upon the reference as having a bearing upon the self-denying qualities of the devoted choristers. That there may be the other side to such a question was proved at a vestry meeting attended by the writer some years ago. The Elector, proposing a vote of thanks, said how pleased the congregation were with the work of the choir, which was entirely voluntary. An old and fearless parishioner, such as most parishes possess, to the regret of the powers that be, said he had "no objection" to seconding, especially as they had discontinued the singing of anthems. Startled by this sudden turn and anxious to secure peace at the point where he evidently knew there was danger, the good man, in his sweetest accents, said, "Yes, we don't have many anthems now." "No, thank God," retorted the old parishioner, and the Vicar, thinking enough had been said; put the vote to the meeting, closed the proceed- ings, and asked the reporters to use their dis- i'ction, which they did.</p>
        <p>THE WELSH BILL.</p>
        <p>The two days' debate in the COITITHOTIS on the Welsh Church Bill produced several fine speeches, Sir JOHN SIMON and the PRIME MINISTER especially im- pressing the House by the high level which their argument ustained. Mr. BALFOUR'S Contributioni was far and away the cleverest effort on the Opposi- tion side. The second reading was carried by the substantial majority of 81. The second reading majorities for this • measure in three successive sessions have been:</p>
        <p>1912 81 majority. 1913 ... ". 99 • , 1914 81 ,</p>
        <p>PREMIER. MOTOR CYCLES. Light-weight model, ..£36.—Sole agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge- street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>From Everywhere.</head>
        <p>From Everywhere.</p>
        <p>It's small consolation for a man to realise that he isn't as bad as he might be.</p>
        <p>Where ignorance is bliss, 'tis folly to ask whys.</p>
        <p>Most people are borrowing trouble when they borrow money.</p>
        <p>The man who thinks he's the only pebble on the beach is in danger of being submerged.</p>
        <p>After the average couple are married it doesn't take long to wear the plate off the spoon- ing.</p>
        <p>I Although there are three scruples in a dram, the more drams one takes the fewer scruples one has.</p>
        <p>Contrary to the old tradition, the world is full of people who dance and don't pay the piper.</p>
        <p>It is hard to conceive that women were ever really happy before the looking-glass was in- vented.</p>
        <p>" Having None. I am an income tax. called—" "I am an artist." "Oh, I beg your pardon" (withdraws).—" The Tatler." inspector, sir- I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Alternative.</head>
        <p>The Alternative.</p>
        <p>-- An atheist asked the Bishop if he believed that Jonah was swallowed by the whale. " When I go to heaven I will ask Jonah," said his lordship. But supposing," the other per- sisted, "lie is not there." "Then you will have to ask him."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>His Reason.</head>
        <p>His Reason.</p>
        <p>"I am glad to see, anyhow, that you sympa- thise with the under dog in this barbarous fight."</p>
        <p>_ " Sympathise with 1m? Gosh, mister, all the money I've got ia up on that dawg 1"</p>
        <p>'Two Fools. Jenkins: Guess I must be a fool. I was born on the first day of April. Muchwed : Shake; old fellow. I consider my- self a fool also. Jenkins: And were you born on that day, too? Muchwed : No; married.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>For Wil</head>
        <p>For Wil</p>
        <p>"George raid the lady, "I don't believe you have smot .1 one of those cigars I gave you on your birtl:('ay." "That's right, my dear," replied her hus- band; "I'm going to keep them until 'Willie wants to learn to smoke."</p>
        <p>Honest.</p>
        <p>- Farmer: Here is a letter from city folks an- swerin' our ad., Mirandy. They want ter know if there's a bath in. the house. What'll I tell Pem? His Wife: Tell 'era the truth. Tell 'em if they need a bath, they'd better take it afore they come.</p>
        <p>The Oxide and Ox Hide. "Thomas," said the professor to a pupil in the junior class in chemistry, "mention an oxide." "Leather," replied Thomas. "What is leather an oxide of F" asked the pro- fessor. • "An oxide of beef," answered the bright Youngster. , ,</p>
        <p>What It Was. ' Yes," said little Binks, " hliss Paynter is a handsome woman, but sometimes when I look at her she seems to me like a woman who has a terrible secret." " She has," said Whibley. • "I was sure of it," said little Binks. "Have you any idea what it is!'" "Yes," said Whibley. "She's 48 years old."</p>
        <p>Thought it Cried.</p>
        <p>_ A woman passenger on a transatlantic liner bothered the officers and captain unceasingly about whales. A hundred times a day she asked to be called if one was sighted. "But, madam," expostulated the captain finally, "why are you so anxious about this whale question?" "Because," she replied., "all my life I've wanted to see a whale blubber."</p>
        <p>One Way put. Mr. Plumpleigh, at and 50, was paying a visit to the country, and, arriving at the de- serted station after dark, he was at a loss to find the exit. .</p>
        <p>After he had roamed around for several min- utes a ragged urchin peeped through the rail- ings. The boy appeared to be vastly amused at the visitor's solid dimensions. "Can you show me the way out of this con- founded station" thundered Plumpleigh. The lad eyed him from top to toe. " Have you tried sideways?" be asked.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>I A CONVICT'S MIGHT HAVE BEENS.</head>
        <p>I A CONVICT'S MIGHT HAVE BEENS.</p>
        <p>A convict, who was in the habit of giving vent to his feelings in poetry, 'recently relieved him- self of the following reflections, which he labelled</p>
        <p>I might hav "Drawbacks." - I might have been a writer Like these joinalistio fellers, Classed with the "six best sellers," And folks would want MY autograft; My' pieter they'd know well; e written lots of books I might have been a writer, but— I don't know how tub spell!</p>
        <p>I might have been an actor strutting in the limelight's glare; A starrin' in romantic roles leadiu' ladies fair; I'd foil the villain ev'ry act— B'gee, I'd be a screech! I might have been a actor, but— I stutters in xnuh speech!</p>
        <p>A'risin' in my ' I might have been a airman Winn in' fame and prizes, too; airy-plane Into the dizzy blue, Wit' all the crowds a-cheerin° As I circled 'round on high; I might have been a airman, but— I sorter hates to die! I might have been a jockey Perched upon a famous steed; A-ridin' in the Handicap You'd see me in the lead; They'd hand me flornl libss-shoes Per my vict'ry, I'll be bound; I might have been a jockey, but— I weighs three hundred pouhd!</p>
        <p>I might have been a rich man With an income pretty steep; I'd smoke ten-dollar stogies And would think 'em pretty cheap; I'd go to Yurrup twieet a year To spend some of milli kale; I might have been a rich man, but— Wuz pinched and put in jail!</p>
        <p>L'ENVOI. Life's full o' many drawbacks, Which is why I make complaint;. I might have been most everything, But—darn it all AIN'T!</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Amateur Shakespearean Plagers.</head>
        <p>Amateur Shakespearean Plagers.</p>
        <p>COMMEMORATION PERFORMANCES OF TWO COMEDIES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>"Much Ado" and "Merry Wives."</head>
        <p>"Much Ado" and "Merry Wives."</p>
        <p>Thanks to the sustained enthusiasm of Mr.l Charles D. Law, and the band of workers he has attracted to his side, Warrington is now able year by year to have its commemoration in the week when the anniversary of the birth of our greatest poet-playwright is being observed in the town which claims him as her son, and, a few other places. The first commemoration at Stratford-on-Avon took place in 1769, under the direction of the great Garrick himself, and was marked by many extravagancies peculiar to the times; but now the observance has taken Iregular form, and its influence is slowly spread- ing over England and the world. Warrington is already well in the van of these workers to per- petuate the memory and familiarise the works of the man who stands pre-eminent as the repre- sentative of British genius and British char- acter. As the local players have survived four commemorations, it may safely be said that they are now standing on solid ground, and, we hope, a permanent basis.</p>
        <p>This year they have passed from tragedy to comedy, and have presented on alternate nights, to large and appreciative audiences, at the Royal Court Theatre, "Much Ado About Noth- ing" and "The Merry Wives of Windsor." Placed in gorgeous settings and aided by all that the arts of production of which Mr. Law is master, the comedies, especially "The Merry Wives of Windsor," have been presented in a manner that is bound to contribute materially towards ensuring the stability of the society. It is worthy of a passing note to record the fact that "Much Ado About Nothing" was the play with which the Stratford Memorial Theatre was opened in 1877.</p>
        <p>Mr. Law has found a loyal and painstaking honorary secretary a'nd business manager in Mr. William Mason, headmaster of Beamont School, and other members of the committee are: Rev. Dr. Mellor (chairman), Mr. H. M. Ashton (hon. treasurer), Mr. A. C. Hodgson (assistant lion. secretary), Mr. A. M. Loader and Mr. Walter Ashton.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Lewis Rowson and G. C. Clarke were assistant stage managers.</p>
        <p>ally, and punished Falstaff for his presumption with the finesse of consummate artistes. To our mind, however, Miss Bessie Forshaw was the most successful—after Mr. Boys, of course—in presenting her allotted part to the public. As the go-between and message-bearer for the wives and Falstaff, she could not have been sur- passed, and her encounters of frivolity with the great man were among the most enjoyable pas- sages of the week's entertainment. A more vivacious Mistress Quickly has seldom been seen. Mr. Alfred Grounds made his debut with the Shakespearean Players as Shallow, and he and his cousin, Slender (Mr. Brian T. Dickson) main- tained with ability another essential side to the comedy, though Mr. Dickson slightly over-acted the " dandy."j The Sweet Anne Page of Miss Marion Ellison was fine, but somewhat too sub- dued, and the Fenton (her successful lover) of Mr. J. Lawton lacked lover-like ardour to make it convincing.</p>
        <p>It should be mentioned that the quite appro- I priate music of the Fairies' song in the conclud- ing scene was composed by Mr. Tom Boothroyd, the able conductor of the Royal Court Theatre: Orchestra.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>"THE MERRY WIVES OF WINDSOR."</head>
        <p>"THE MERRY WIVES OF WINDSOR."</p>
        <p>Dramatis Persome.</p>
        <p>Sir John Falstaff Mr. Ryder Boys. Fenton, a young gentleman Mr. J. Lawton. Shallow, a country justice... Air. Alfred Grounds. Slender, cousin to Shallow.. Mr. Brian T. Dickson. Ford ) Two gentlemen ç Mr.L.L.Langford. Page Idwelling in Windsor 1 Mr. Charles Law Sir Hugh Evans, a Welsh parson</p>
        <p>Mr. W. S. Edwards Doctor Caius, a French Physician _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. H. M. Ashton. I Host of the Garter Inn... Mr. Leonard C. Daintith. Bard olph Followers of Mr. B. Caldwell. I Pistol Mr. W. P. Ashton. J Falstaff { Nym Mr. A. C. Hodgson. Robin, page to Falstaff Mrs. Charles Law. Simple, servant to Slender Mr. C. Nurse. Rugby, servant to Dr. Caius...Mr. W. S. Furness. Mistress Ford Miss Gladys Adams. Mistress Page Miss Winifred Corney. Anne Page, her daughter... Miss Marion Ellison. I Mistress Quickly, servant to Doctor Caius ; Miss Bessie Forshaw. William Page Master Gordon Law. ; John Mr. H. Beaman. Robert Mr. W. F. Foster. William } Ford's servants Mr. C. Nurse. James Mr. W. S. Furness. • Fairies :—Misses Ethel Bladen, Winifred Bates, Lena Blackburn, Elsie Barnett, Elsie 'Evans, Daisy Goulden, Lilian Harrison, Eliza- beth Houghton, Eileen Healing, Gertrude Jones, Bessie Jolley, Doris Leigh, Marie Perdue, Pattie Payne, Nellie Payne, Adelaide Price, Ethel Rafter, Winifred Smith, Annie Wright and Ethel Wright. `• The Merry Wives of Windsor" is, for many reasons, one of the most popular of Shakespeare's comedies. In the first place it is his one play of contemporary manners, and has a priceless value as a sketch, as only Shakespeare could draw it, of the middle-class English life of his day. He exhausted worlds and then imagined new in a way that has held an admiring world in wonder ever since; but here he gives us his first-hand I experience of the men and women who actually lived and acted their several parts while he was acting his. It is even said that the play was written by command of Queen Elizabeth, which may easily be true. The scandals connected with the Court of Elizabeth would make it quite conceivable that her Majesty would be espe- cially attracted by the doings of Falstaff, the evil genius of Prince Henry in " Henry 1V.," ' and his dissolute hangers-on, Bardolph, Pistol and Nym. She enjoyed the doings of the "oily rascal," Falstaff, whose barefaced immorality ' was so cloaked by shrewdness, humour and un- consciousness of, rather than a callous disposi- tion towards, guilt, that the rogue is lost sight of even in an unscrupulous adventurer who preys upon the weaknesses of other men. The character of Falstaff is indicated in the words of Prince Henry himself, who, imagining Falstaff dead on Shrewsbury Field, when his cowardice had led him to sham death, exclaimed "I could hays better spared a better man." We could all do so.</p>
        <p>The play has a personal side for Shakespeare himself. We know very little of the author, but, as Green says, the tradition that Elizabeth was so pleased with Falstaff in "Henry IV." that she ordered the poet to show her Falstaff in love—an order which produced " The Merry Wives of Windsor "—whether true or false, shows his repute as a playwright. To show such a. man in love in such times as those of Eliza-, heth was to show him in elicit love. The great character would have been spoiled for all time had he been suddenly endowed with a pure passion, and Elizabeth would scarcely have been satisfied. But Falstaff's attack on the honour of the wives of- Ford and Page is dealt with by a master-hand, and, as ever with Shakespeare, we witness a display of the essence of humour and the inevitable discomfiture of vileness and treachery.</p>
        <p>It must be admitted that a body of amateur Players were more than ordinarily ambitious in attempting to present •a work upon which such important issues hang, but it has to be recorded that not only did they greatly dare, but they did so with signal success. It is a matter for regret that the society went outside their own ranks to find a Falstaff. Mr. Ryder Boys, who was engaged for the part, is a professional with a fine reputation, and it was a risky expedient, considering the arrangement on its less impor- tant side, to mix so much proved talent with so ' much that was necessarily experimental. It was fortunate indeed that even Mr. Ryder Boys could not unduly outshine the amateur support by which he has this week been surrounded. He lived the part he played, and gave to us the mountain of corruption and the incarnation of humour and falsehood that 'Shakespeare in- tended. But the principals all played up to him with remarkable spirit—some almost attaining his own high level, and others approximating to it more or less nearly. Mr. Leslie Langford, as the suspecting Ford, egged on unconsciously by Falstaff as Othello was urged on maliciously by lago, gave a fine interpretation of a man who is eaten up by the green-eyed monster jealousy, and, Mr. Law, as the easy-minded Page, who trusts his wife, even when Falstaffs are about, were highly meritorious performances. Mistress Ford and Mistress Page, the merry wives, lin the persons of Miss Gladys Adams and Miss Winifred Corney, played with fire quite natur-,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>"MUCH ADO ABOUT NOTHING."</head>
        <p>"MUCH ADO ABOUT NOTHING."</p>
        <p>Dramatis Persomr.</p>
        <p>Don Pedro, Prince of Arragon.. Mr. W. H. Brown. Don John, his brother Mr. Douglas Travis. Claudio„ a young lord of Florence... Mr. J. Lowe. Benedick, a young lord of Padua...Mr. Chas. Law. Leonato, Governor of Messina..Mr. L. C. Daintith. Antonio, his brother Mr. A. C. Hodgson. Balthazar, a musician attendant Mr. J. JD. Pedro... Drape Draper. Conrad° followers of ç Mr. A. Duncan Heys. Borachio I Don John 1, Mr. Walter P. Ashton. Friar Francis Mr. P. C. Newbold. Dogberry, a constable Mr. H. M. Ashton. Verges, a Headborough Ur. Gordon Starkey. Ist Watchman Mr. FL Beaman. 2nd Watchman Mr. W. F. Foster. Oatcake Mr. R. Pemberton. { Seacoal f Watchmen Mr. B. Madeley. A Messenger Mr. C. W. Vernon. A Sexton Mr. M. 'Aukland. Hero, daughter to Leonato...Miss Milly Truslove. Beatrice, niece to Leonato...Miss Gladys Adams. Margaret gentlewomen 4 Miss Ella Royston. Ursula I attending Hero Miss M. Hutton. Ladies, Gentlemen, Musicians, Watchmen, Halberdiers, Monks, &amp;c.:—Mrs. Charles Law, the Misses Aida Clarke, Mamie Blackburn, Marion Ellison, D. Fern, Edith Forshaw, Con- stance Hall, Dorothy Hall, Muriel Houghton, Ida Haddock, Ida Jenks, Evelyn Newall, Dora Roberts, Pattie Woodcock, Mabel Walker, Jessie Woodland; Messrs. H. itrchibald, G. G. Ains- worth, W. J. Ashworth, M. Aukland, E. Brown, T. R. Bullock, E. N. Burn, G. H. Clarke, W. Curwen, B. Caldwell, E. Dronfield, W. S. Fur- ness, G. Fielder, A. G. Flute, A. Gardner, J. A. Greenwood, A. D. Heys, J. E. Hannan, W. S. Hart, J. Hepherd, jun:, F. Jackson, A. Leigh, J. Lawton, A. B. Lythgoe, B. Madeley, P. Nock- all, C. Nurse, A. Pearson, R. Pemberton, T. Phair, T. I'rendergast, W. K. Readett, C. A. Rose, 11. L. Shone, T. H. Savage, W. Spencer, C. W. Vernon, H. H. Wilkins, T. Worthington, J. Wainwright.</p>
        <p>The performances of "Much Ado About Noth- ing" call for modified criticism, and a fuller measure of praise; inasmuch as they embody the efforts of entirely amateur players. Inci- dentally they found employment, as "The Merry Wives of Windsor" did not do, for that stage-crowd of which Mr. Charles Law, the pro- ducer, is so inordinately enamoured as to be in danger of forgetting that one of its effects is sometimes, as happened in this instance, to spoil the play by confusing its strongly-drawn lines. There is, in the texture of "Much Ado About Nothing," a thread of threatened tragedy which keeps one in enthralled expectancy during the unfolding of the story, and, for emphasising this note of seriousness, the Warrington Shakesperean Players were happy in being able to put forward Mr. John Lowe. For it is scarcely too much to say that, in the part of Claudio, he centred upon himself a large share of one's interest in the of naturalness which is perhaps the best of all recommendations, and, remembering his manly Miss Milly Truslove and the intense way in which he throws himself play. bearing, M r h. L s ec-lee a' s clear acting n g thoughtful abouthtful i te enunciation, that icahtar:n into his work, one ceases to wonder at the secret uAsite'c'hHaermmin"g, but it was scarcely sary to a true-to-life presentment of her part in the ciilsne eap success.aeymotional way one imagines is neces- sary trying church scene. Yet, though at this point Hero scarcely played up to her Claudia, there was nevertheless much to conarnend.in Miss Truslove's sweet, rather than deeply studied impersonation. In this play one would, in ad- vance, think of Miss Gladys Adams and Mr. Charles Law as quite ideal personages for the they were appropriate to the part. was clearly at her best alike in parts of Beatrice and Benedick. As Beatrice, t Miss Adams's alternations of mood from grave to gay, and from lively te severe, were as hDeunritce charmingsirwuvh inwiloimashssenhtyetle vivacious mood, as in her more tender is, and while she was at all times singularly free from artificiality of manner, so much can hardly be said of Mr. Law, whose Beneilick became at times a little over-powering in its forced gaiety. The detracting influence characterising the assumption of the part of Benedick was, indeed, a too obvious exaggera- tion of its features all through. Whether the mood involved some good-natured raillery, the serious championing of an injured lady, or the introspection of the belated lover, there was too often about Mr. Law's acting an over-determin- ation to make these matters clear, that over-ran both meaning and purpose. But to say that undue demonstrativeness is Mr. Law's besetting fault is not, by any means, to underrate his proved high abilities as an actor, nor to set him down as a negligible quantity in this play. Rather he was not sufficiently negligible in the sense of being self-restrained, for, alike in speech, laughter and 'gesture, his acting would have gained in convincing power in the inverse proportion to the suppression of some of his strong mannerisms. The Leonato of Mr. L. C. Daintith was an appropriately dignified, and, in many respects adequate, portrayal of a fine part. Therein it differed from the assumption of the character of Don. Pedro by Mr. W. H. Brown, in which the distinctiqn of rank, as the Prince of Arragon, was largely conspicuous by its absence. Nor was the part of Don John taken by Mr. D. Travis that of the thorough-going villain it ought to have been. When will the amateur actor learn that the first essential in the elimination of self-consciousness is the deter- mination to be in real earnest? It is pleasant, in this connection, to pass on to high commend- ation of Mr. H. M. Ashton, whose Dogberry will, we venture to think, be remembered as one of his happiest impersonations. His haranguing of the rank and file of the "watch," and, sub- sequently, his administration of justice in the Court scene, were inimitable specimens of character portraiture. The stately ensemble dance in Leonato's house was of a somewhat nondescript character, and, although it was not lacking in leisurely dignity, it was very much wanting in movement and variety. The music was a gavotte rhythm, but whether the dance was intended to be a mtnnet or a gavotte, we were unable to discover. :1!.1r.</p>
      </div>
