        <p>friendship of the closest possible kind' remained unbroken to the day of his After he left Warrington and settled in we were regular correspondents„ and nl)' occasions I lent the late Mr. John WO° of his letters, which he published 0 Examiner." They/ were of a very inroel character and created wide interest. .1.1 leaving England he had been unsettled lief his wife's health being delicate he decide( abroad, but for a time was undecided go. However, a certain lady—the mother C. E. Parker, of Pen_keth—gave a lecture, Bold-street Schoolroom on "California ing, I think, largely with Los Angel° Passedena. She gave such a glowing ec,°, the country that Mr. and Mrs. Winstarne. were present, decided to go out West. diately on arriving at Oregon he bought a and he has been settled there over eine, had a very rough experience at first. directly they settled on the farm the melted on the mountains, and the river., flowed past their place, became flooded. were carried down the river bodily; pigs' horses, and the wild panthers of the nisi) were also carried down, many of then' I trunks of trees. They had a most tell experience. However, during their I"t' there of 26 years they never had another ence of the like kind. They prospered ir new home, though they had a good deal I judice to overcome. We have heard a ff°° at times of American friendship for 9) and that Americans loved Englishmen' other old friend: Mr. Whitham, did eat, so; neither did I when I was there, and did Mr. Winstanley. On the contrarl people were very much given to abusive adjectives about England nlv English who were settled amongst the° Mr. Winstanley was a man of fine ohs° and character is bound to tell. In his ; told to such an extent as to compel tbe ' of all who knew him. He was the nte getting the farmers for miles around t' themselves into an association for the 6 their products. He was made presidentd Oregon Fruit Growers' Association, nni over here to open markets for their gee° this he—in conjunction with his son-in-Vj Walter Jenks—was very successful, nrlu, their brand of prunes, apricots, &amp;T., 10; the Pheasant Brand, is known all ore' country, France and Germany, and 0111 the highest prices on the markets. His death came as a great shock to Me', a letter from him a few weeks ago, in tril, asked me to go over next year and v'is via the Panama Canal. lie said he 1011' me the time of my life. I wonder if he had any premonition was coming? Ile finished his letter following words: "The days and moat' away so quickly. I sometimes wonder h°l go. It can only be a little while longerl I, at least, will slip over to the Beyond., I shall be ready when the call comes. ',I ever, your old friend, for `mild lang sYne' WINSTANLEY." And so has passed away one of Warr° worthiest sons. JOHN GASP</p>
