      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <p>After the Game. Two Warrington youths—James Bate and Frederick Massey—were fined les. and costs each ttt Runcorn for game trespass at Halton. A ritaedgeoepse. r said he saw them kill two rabbits Both denied the offence.</p>
        <p>Free Primroses. "Primrose Day" fell upon Sunday, and, as ;_1"al, the dames of the Primrose League distri- "uted flowers free of charge. There was not a PeAt display, despite the fact that 2,000 bunches nad been given away.</p>
        <p>•—.P.C.C. Workpeople's Committee. The w first monthly meeting of the newly-formed N acrrington Workpeople's Committee of the t Society for the Prevention of Cruelty Children was held at the Tows Hall, Mr. W. (11.1,„Itnicm presiding. The meeting was ad- -I)3r Mr. F. J. Mchew, of London. Mr- Taylor, 15, Bold-street, was appointed -°rarY secretary.</p>
        <p>Exciting Street Scene. alarming incident occurred in horse- outtreet on Saturday. A pony trap drew raol. of LYme-street in front of a Gorsey-lane '.(3l' 'bus. The driver of the 'bus just averted jue°llisi°a• and then throwing on his brakes sols.t:iv_Pided a head-on collision with a motor tlrivn,,u a milk float. The promptitude of the ri'g `'` certainly saved a serious accident occur- n. • Oddfellows' Gathering. At ,a Meeting of the "Admiral Nelson" Lodge, o Manchester Unity of Oddrellows, held ix,-041011-d'ay at the Pelican Hotel, the brethren , nt.4,vers thoroughly enjoyed a lecture kindly tn;bll, by P.P.G.M. Massey, entitled The ti of the Order: Its History and Tradi- •ots The lecture was both interesting and .o,,,Netive and P.P.G.M. Massey was warmly StriZeren'ted on the fine example he sets by so tho snlY devoting a large part of his time .to .01 .raternal and social side of Oddfellowship. 43 tinn were one or two musical items in addi- to' nd the Lodge added to its envied reputa- er keeping the lighter side of Oddfellowship pra-%tauch alive. The members make a regular evor„edee of giving papers and lectures on almost to ,-;.„Babiect, ranging from "A Holiday Tour" 14:e Composition of the Human Body." On on,hl P.G. William Webster is giving a paper he Structure of a Bird and Bird Life in arrington District." hCiti7ens' Temperance League. T - ail„„e Tafinual meeting of the Citizens' Temper- .e,aglie was held on Wednesday. Mr. J. gatC',Lainshaw presided over a representative 41r, '•01.'llg. The treasurer's report was read by kr. Sandy. The secretary's report, read by the • T. Parkes, indicated the activity of ague in many directions of public temper- Dost work, open-air campaign, temperance eaalPaign, issuing to doctors and nurses We ' e district the pamphlet "The Medicated lles „ Bessi raud, municipal elections, licensing List and other questions which are import- °la a true citizen's standpoint. The Rev. James was elected president, the vice- Nle—erits being Messrs. J. G. Withinshaw, T. thb p4rld J. Griffiths. Mr. W. J. Walker and</p>
        <p>Na-,,lh'ev• A. J. Che-ssum are to he asked to be- -14-I,e'fce-presidents. The treasurer elected is 4SBj: • Gaudy; secretary, Mr. Benj. T. Parkes; . Alt aalt secretary, Mr. Woolley; auditors, 1". T. WirAill and W. Pilkington. The flit-helve Committee were re-appointed, with 140held1tion of Mr. A. Tucker. Mr. Gandy 11,4 7,,..fe vote of thanks to Mr. Withinshaw for vice during the time he had been pres- -1'44-o°f the league. Mr. Dale seconded, and it 'hrried unanimously. Mr. Witlunshaw, in ;44,13137, thanked the members for their ox- and d appreciation of his services, d 4rt, ..aopedt a to of service to the league in when his many duties would permit it.</p>
        <p>1,1% trnmantiel Vestry Meeting. lo'd-road the Rev. estry ig was held in the Institute, ' 4,8 f eell - William T. Jaye pre- r, The churchwardens' account was pre- ' hY Mr. Robert Lees, sen., and proved to übtn_,- not satisfactory financial statement ren- vc4 for the past twenty years. The income th4e ~ '.331 155., and after meeting all expenses remained a balance in hand of £l6 19s, fitk-"Pg the year £lO6 had been paid off the debt thehl.he Church Institute. The Incumbent leed the congregation for their continued for"'l3' to him, and their devotion to the church, tiR which they had made so many personal sac- He expressed satisfaction at the increas- the 'll4kher of young people who were attaching trieZaselves to the church, and hoped that every' the ,})ter would endeavour to make the work of trieCnrch know-n throughout the town and dis- 'the The following officers were appointed : toe ricurobent nominated Mr. William Ather- hi4 his warden, at the same time thanking ' .Ita, °l' hie past services; Mr. Robert Lees, sen., Atl:Plin elected people's warden; Messrs. W. and J. Bibby were elected Synodsmen; Ite,t 'lle following were elected to the Select bleicrY Messrs. R. H. Armstrong, W. Bibby, A. 141411,8' E. Pludder, II Heald, A. Johnson, T. P. Lloyd, and T. Moon.</p>
        <p>• Albert Pollitt's Water-colours. Thf,„ , ea vil'e is a fine collection of this artist's work 18 ew at 64 Cross-street, Manchester, and it Per.itresting to compare his present method or 3'543 with the pictures he painted some twenitey- , 1.11.4 ot4g°: We remember a very striking itoti his in the Warrington Jubilee Exhibi- , Pictures in the Parr Hall, and which was et,'aSed by the late Mr. John Crosfield. His ~ 'hen („--,,.7/Ilow looks warmer—and, if we may use kid --reSsion , more mellow—a softer outline, lag a' Illore reposeful feeling. Perhaps advanc- -41 age h las something to do with . He has 171 h adoDted other subjects, for now he revels this arbears, shipping and light and sade on No. 20, "East Hartlepool Harbour," •awerfuLpiece of work, for by cleverseleic; 411) 4:114 dexterous use of light and shade, ri 14'8tDrnkr,' water and sky yield their charm Conway Vitlit,,Y' No. 13, " Gerlan Farm in the dew s is an early morning effect. The dur 4,iritiell the foliage and grass, and the vapour sui tt. i'srl4 froin the foot of the distant mountain. Nt. charming rural study. No. 17 The , IN)elwYd," is one of the Welsh subjects, r. Pollitt loves to paint—a trout stream,4 Attet, tilalPid water and a”glorious_esky2. ,No. , tO-Y-Coed Woods, and .147 of his lc:‘ri el Bay," are typical examplesof 44,1;14 manner. A visit to this exhibition [tit, gems would well repay the lover of and art. t Thy, Ancient Order of Foresters. I'l°4: ISiE32,o9th quarterly meeting of the Warring Memorial 471,1; 'let was held at the Wycliffe 'llgs on Saturday. The various uolif7, '''''444l)'esented by the following delegates 4,1.°11.%.` 138, P D CR. W. Hipkiss and P.C.R. lioCourt. ;7-40, P.D.C.R. J. Owen and pLO p'8.),t0,0; Court 430, P.C.R. W. James, P.C.R. et, P.D.C.R. Councillor J. Chums. P.C.R. W. Lightfoot and :1'4)61 Court 161, P.C.R. J. Gatley; Court dA. Corradine. The following brethren Grayson; Court 9,335, Sisters Nn b.vree,,-1".11 Presn t Trustees—P.D.C.R. J. Walsh, -41145 u James, P.D.C.R. L. Davies, P.D.C.R. t,e "41r3", G.W. R. Baines, P.C.R. "b4ctit, maild P.C.R. P. Wilding. The chair was l'Oystt ; S. B. Healey, and the vice- J. IL Hollins. The Mayor Councillor P. Peacock, district trea- t-41'37) :111(1 P D.C.R. R. Thornley (district secre- '4nichi.„7'so attended. The Chairman made a 408tE1f„.48 reference to the loss the district had jthrough the death of P.D.C.R. H. May- 4,:tat' t 041411; rauved that a letter of condolence be Vi 7 " Ividow, the Vice-chairman seconding. k or's,arties and Sister A. Corradine gave the lopte, n,report, which was unanimously Th .R. e D.O Bro. r 2. B. Healey was u- -4 i;ittic Itle elected delegate to represent this zteXt 4tbt the High Court, to be held in August 01111 la eurileinn th Bro. J. Owen was un- antesiSilelectedna final arbitrator in place of °ese ro• TT. A -bu The meeting was titil I -ra'' qm. 137 the D.C.R. reading the usual ec) 41370,i, blet (-81 is nre world renowned. 4-seater with dynamo electric lighting outfit, 4 Q (4° agent- for Warrington and district. . 414480, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warring- - '</p>
