      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <p>Scarlet Fever. There were 66 cases of scarlet fever in the Warrington Hospital last 'week. Six new</p>
        <p>_ were reported.</p>
        <p>case</p>
        <p>Territorial Captain Resigns. The "London Gazette" announces that Capt. Halsall Owen has resigned his Commission in the local Territorials, the 4th Battalion South Lancashire Regiment.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Sunday Scpool Anniversary. The Wycliffe Sunday School anniversary takes place to-morrow. The special preacher will be the Rev. Fred llibbert, of Southport. At 2.30 there will x.e. a special musical service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>Mill Girls' Present.</head>
        <p>Mill Girls' Present.</p>
        <p>A large number of girls, members of the Card- room and Blowing-room Association, met at the I.L.P. Rooms on Wednesday night, when a pre- sentation was made to Mr. James Wilkinson, formerly an overlooker at Coekhedge Factory. Mr. Hargreaves, of Wigan, president of the society, made the presentation on behalf of the girls. Mr. Wilkinson has taken up a position under the union.</p>
        <p>Young Architect's Success. At the recent examination held by the Society of Architects, Mr. Isaac Alexander Simpson, son of Mr. Charles Simpson, 101, Bewsey-road, quali- fied for membership, and is now entitled to use the letters M.S.A.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>Life and Death.</head>
        <p>Life and Death.</p>
        <p>The births in Warrington last week numbered 45, giving a rate of 32.6, compared with 37.9 in the same week last year. The deaths totalled 17, giving a rate of 11.8, compared with 22.4 last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>Education Official Honoured.</head>
        <p>Education Official Honoured.</p>
        <p>The Warrington branch of the National Fed- eration of Caretakers on Tuesday made a pre- sentation to Mr. Elliott, at the Education Office, in connection with his recent marriage. Mr. E. Hewitt presided. Mr. Carman made the pre- sentation, which consisted of a handsome umbrella. Mr. Elliott suitably replied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>School Caretakers' Wages.</head>
        <p>School Caretakers' Wages.</p>
        <p>_ The following resolution in regardio the wages of school caretakers will be submitted to the Education Committee on Monday for approval: " That, on account of the increased cost of living, the present wages of all caretakers of elementary schools be increased by 2s. per week; such increase to take effect from and including the week ending on tie 6th May, 1914; and that the scale of payment for elementary school care- takers be amended accordingly."</p>
        <p>Mayoral Courtesies. On Wednesday night the Mayor and Mayoress (Councillor and Mrs. Peter Peacock), accom- panied by their niece, Miss Gladys Peacock, of Pairbourne, attended the children's dance pro- moted by the Lord Mayor of Liverpool, at the Liverpool Town Hall. The first prize in the hockey toy game, a silver inkstand with name inscribed, was won by Miss Peacock. The Mayor was a guest at the Lord Mayor of Lon- don's reception last night.</p>
        <p>Latchford Without Burials. At the Christ Church Vestry, Latchford, on Saturday evening, the question of the rights of burial at Grappenhall was again discussed, and the feeling was that the arrangement should be ended. While now the parish was within the borough of Warrington, the burial right S should be transferred to the Cemetery. The Vicar (the Rev. G C. Briggs) appointed Mr. J. Lowe his warden. Mr. G. Venn was elected people's warden. It was reported that the debit balance at the bank had been reduced from £6 12s. to £3 17s. The accounts were passed.</p>
        <p>Vaccination Exemptions. In his annual report to the Warrington Dis- trict Council, Dr. R. Sephton, the medical offi- cer, states that the birth-rate was lower than last year, and equalled 18.8. The death-rate was the lowest for the last seven years and equalled 9.3 per thousand of estimated pripulation. Them were 27 deaths over 65 years of age, and 24 were caused- by zymotie disease. The infant deaths under one year of age -numbered 25, which was lower than last year. There had not been any case of smallpox during the year. Vaccination' had not been so successfully carried out as in former years owing to the !eater number Of exemptions which were being grauted. Seventy- three cases of scarlet fever -vero nozilicd, and none proved fatal, but there were four deaths from diphtheria. Warrington's Leather Trade.</p>
        <p>Easter seems to have had a much less effect upon our local trade than usual, says "The Leather World," as during the second half of the week business was surprisingly good. Mer- chants were taking in leather very freely, and were also picking up a fair weight of leathei from the odd lines which were offering, many of them buying very largely at current prices and for stock. Export trade continues to develop strongly; week by week shows an increasing outgoing, and such large figures have been attained that the effect upon our home market will be severely felt at any such time as any stronger deniand is made; the increase in the foreign demand shows no signs of ceasing, ani from the latest returns is growing at about the rate of 100 tons per week.</p>
        <p>Knutsford May Day. For 50 years the Royal May Day Festival at Knutsford has attracted thousands of people, and this being the Jubilee year a special pro- gramme has been arranged. On Friday next (the first day) a gorgeous procession will leave the Town Hall at two o'clock, and the May Queen will be crowned on the Heath at 3.30. There will be the usual spectacular pageantry and exhibitions of dancing. The St. Joseph's Industrial School Pipers and Dancers will give a variety entertainment in the ring enclosure from 1.30 until the procession arrives. On Saturday Foden's Brass Band will play selections, and there will also be special attrac- tions on the Heath. The May Queen of 50 years ago will take part in the proceedings. Special trains will be run at reduced fares from all stations.</p>
        <p>Oldest Guardian Dead. The Warrington Board of Guardians lost its oldest member on Wednesday evening by the death of Mr. William Houghton, the represen- tative of the Woolston-with-Martinscroft dis- trict. Mr. Houghton, who resided at The Grange, Paddington, was 68 years of age, and had been ill for about six weeks, although his health had not been all that could be desired for some considerable time. He joined the Board of Guardians in April, 1883, and later be- came a member of the Warrington Rural Dis- trict Council, and by virtue of that office he has continued to be on the Board of Guardians. His work with the Board of Guardians and the Rural District Council was greatly respected by the members, so much so that he had been ap- pointed chairman of both bodies. The funeral will take place this afternoon and will leave deceased's house at 1.15. There will be a service at Padgate Church at 1.45, and the funeral will be at the Warrington Cemetery at 2.30.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Golborne-street Baptist.</head>
        <p>Golborne-street Baptist.</p>
        <p>Golborne-street Baptist Schoolroom was the scene of an interesting event on Thursday. An American tea had been arranged by Mrs. Ogden, the Pastor's wife, and Mrs. B. Few. The pro- ceeds will be devoted to the finances of the Senior Ladies' Stall in connection with the forthcoming sale of work on June 11th. The principle of the tea was that each lady attend- ing brought and also purchased an article, and! the price, which goes to the tea, was 6d. each. The schoolroom was tastefully decorated with flowers and the stalls were draped with muslin. About 3.30 p.m. between 50 and 60 ladies had assembled. The proceedings were opened by prayer by the Rev. H. Ogden, after which an excellent musical programme was rendered by the following artistes : Miss Oakley, solo accom- panist, who very tastefully occupied her post; praise was also due to Mr. Williamson, Miss Winstanley, Miss Hughes, Mrs. Heywoog and Mrs. Kenworthy for their delightful solo. Mrs. Ogden and Miss G. Roweling rendered their pianoforte solos with excellent taste. The effort for the day realised about ~t 6. Great credit was due to Mr. and Mrs. B. Few for their successful management of the tea.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 25, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 25, 1914.</p>
        <p>THE STRIKE ENDED.</p>
        <p>Fletcher's Men Gradually to Return</p>
        <p>to Work.</p>
        <p>ABOUT £lO,OOO LOST IN WAGES.</p>
        <p>After a dispute of nearly eight weeks the strike at Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co.'s gas stove factory at Wilderspool came to an end on Thursday afternoon. Yesterday a number of the men had re-started, a larger con- tingent is to follow next week, and it is hoped that in a fortnight the works will be in full operation again.</p>
        <p>- It has been roughly estimated that the men have lost about ..tlO,OOO in wages, and that the strike has cost the trade union about .X 3,500 while the public have subscribed to the strike fund. What the strike has meant to the firm on the other hand it is difficult to estimate. They had a quantity of stock on hand when the strike commenced, and this is reported to have been cleared out at remunerative prices.</p>
        <p>A conference took place on Wednesday afternoon. Then it become apparent that a move for peace was in the air, although it was not made known until Thursday what were the terms of the proposed settlement. These proved satisfactory to the men and the strike came to an end.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>TERMS OF SETTLEMENT.</head>
        <p>THE TERMS OF SETTLEMENT.</p>
        <p>It was agreed that any individual or depart- mental grievance shall be first reported to the foreman, and, failing redress, the foreman shall meet a deputation from the department affected. If the dispute cannot be settled by the fore- man, the manager shall then meet the deputa- tion, and, failing a settlement, the manager and a director shall meet one of the deputation along with one outside union official. It was also agreed to restart five hundred men during next week, and to reinstate as many men during the following weeks as possible, and all men to be reinstated within four weeks, and that no new men were to be engaged until all the old hands had been reinstated. A guarantee was given that no workman would be victimised or in any way suffer in consequence of the dispute. The summonses against the men in connection with the dispute to be withdrawn.</p>
        <p>Interviewed by" The Examiner" representa- tive, Mr. Charles Dukes (chairman of the Strike Committee) considered it was a satisfactory set- tlement, and embodied union recognition and everything the men wanted. MR. CHARLES DUKES' POSITION. The Joint Strike Committee's Explanation. We have been asked to publish the following statement :</p>
        <p>Whilst the dispute has been in progress at Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co.'s works, a statement has been well circulated that Mr. Charles Dukes has created it as a means of making money for himself by offering to speak at works' gates, and at Sunday meet- ings at Bridge Foot. Whoever has circu- lated this has either done so with a view of creating a bad impression of the man, or has never examined the fabrication that they were circulating. I feel in duty bound to correct this circu- lation. Mr. Dukes has never received any money or other recompense, nor has he been offered any by any of the societies. He has not in any way been responsible for the dis- pute at Messrs. Fletcher, Russell's, other than being a member of the Joint Com- mittee which is made up of representatives of the five societies which have organised the men at Messrs. Fletcher, Russell's and Messrs. Richmond's. As a matter of fact, Charles Dukes re- ceives less remuneration for his work than do the majority of the workers who were engaged in the dispute. These are facts and cannot be denied truthfully by any person.</p>
        <p>Signed for the Joint Committee, A. BENNETT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>CROSFIELD'S BRIEF STRIKE.</head>
        <p>CROSFIELD'S BRIEF STRIKE.</p>
        <p>Dockers Come Out on Account of a Peculiar Question. ETTLED ZN TEN HOURS.</p>
        <p>A somewhat peculiar strike developed at : Crosfield's Wharf yesterday. Some 175 men came out because four men there were in arrears in their payments to the National Union of Dock Labourers. Thus the strike practically resolved itself into a fight between 175 dockers who had paid their levies and four who had not, and in consequence the work at the wharf was brought practically to a standstill. Efforts were made last night to secure a settle- ment. Mr. James Sexton, the men's general secretary, of Liverpool, and prospective Labour candidate for St. Helens, was in Warrington last evening and addressed a private meeting of the men. He then saw Capt. Crosfield. It was stated that the men involved promised to Pay the arrears. The strike seemed about to end in peace, when a fresh complication arose. It was discovered that three men in the shipping de- partment were outside the union, and another deputation was hurriedly sent to see Captain Crosfield.</p>
        <p>Later it was reported that the strike had been settled, and that work would be resumed to-day. The question of the men in the ship- ping department is to stand over for future consideration. OFFICIAL STATEMENT.</p>
        <p>An official statement, read in the presence of " The Examiner" representative, stated:</p>
        <p>We have given our word to a settlement, which Capt. Crosfield has signed, and which reads: On the understanding that the men on the wharf commence work at 7.20 on the 25th inst.,</p>
        <p>I undertake, on behalf of the firm, that the in- terruption of to-day's work will be ignored, and will not prejudice any men entitled to the usual privileges. The men cheered the statement, and on a show of hands the strikers unanimously decided to accept the conditions. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Hodson, the local secretary, said be placed before Captain Crostield the fact that certain •'Jaen had agreed to pay the contributions to the Inlion. and he asked what would be done if thee men refused to pay again Captain Crosfield replied that if these particular men who gave the promise in this matter drew back after the matter was settled he would be prepared tc interview them. (Cheers.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>MOTORIST'S TERRIBLE INJURIES</head>
        <p>MOTORIST'S TERRIBLE INJURIES</p>
        <p>Serious Condition of Warrington Man</p>
        <p>St. Albans, Friday night. While driving home a new open four-seater car from London Concessionaires of Mathis Motors, Limited, Harry Howarth, of Willis- street, Warrington, was terribly injured on Thursday night in a collision between the car and,a bicycle ridden by Private R. Kempton, of the 12th Lancers, stationed at Redbourne, Hert- fordshire.</p>
        <p>Both men were rendered unconscious, Howarth's face being battered in by the violent contact with the wind screen.</p>
        <p>Both the car and the cycle were extensively damaged. The injured men were attended by a local doctor, and then conveyed to St. Albans Hos- pital, where Howarth remains in a serious con- dition.</p>
        <p>- He was taking the ear to Warrington for Mr. J. S. Garland, of tridge-street, where he was eni ployed. Mr. Howarth's mother and brother and Mr. Garland journeyed to St. Albans on Thursday night.</p>
      </div>
