      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>GROUP AT VINCENNES. Back row—Rhoda Grant, Miss Forsyth, Miss Clark, Marjorie Knowles. Middle row—Charlotte Furness, Ruth Balmford, Hilda Fitter, May Maginn.</head>
        <p>A GROUP AT VINCENNES. Back row—Rhoda Grant, Miss Forsyth, Miss Clark, Marjorie Knowles. Middle row—Charlotte Furness, Ruth Balmford, Hilda Fitter, May Maginn.</p>
        <p>Front row—Vera Bowes, Ethel Binell.</p>
        <p>The party of eight girls and two teachers, Miss Clark and Miss Forsyth, who spent their Easter Holidays in Prance, had a most enjoyable time. They saw' as much of London as possible on the Thursday afternoon and evening before Good Friday, and they set out for Folkeston, early on Friday morning. After crossing to, Boulogne, they made their headquarters at a Boarding School at Vincennes, on the outskirts of Paris.</p>
        <p>m the sights of the most charming city imagine nattilled the world made a great impres-I sion on the minds of the girls, who saw it for the first time.</p>
        <p>After having attended morning service on Easter Sunday in the beautiful Notre Dame, where "a most gorgeous service was held, they went to the great cemetery of Pere Lachaise to see the tombs of the famous men and women of past generations. It was in these grounds that the first " colli- sion " with the natives occurred—and collisions are not infrequent when one is in a strange land, notwithstanding the fact that one may have been studying the strange land's language for half a life-time.</p>
        <p>One of the mistresses was photographing a noted tomb, when 11P came a Frenchman greatly excited and gesticulating as only an excited Frenchman can gesticulate—unless it be an excited Frenchwoman—and began to threaten all kinds of things if the camera was not taken out of the grounds. In the presence of his threats the Warrington young ladies remained aggravat- ingly calm—as only Warrington young ladies can remain calm. Gallic excitability and Saxon imperturbability did not mix very well, and the gentleman, as is customary in such cases, fell back upon the expedient of explaining who he was. He was nothing less than a gend'arme, a terrible foreign name for the homely police- man. He explained, further, that lie was off duty, or he would have locked somebody up, at which the representatives of Warrington's youth, beauty and learning appear to have laughed. "Ce n'est pas</p>
        <p>pour rire," shouted the</p>
        <p>besieged gend'arme; "ce n'est pas pour sire." This sent the thoughts of some of the party to the pierrOts at Prestatyn or Llandudno last summer, who were very fond of saying : "It's no maffing latter," for "It's no laughing inatter," a very free translation of the gend'arme's declaration. And there was more laughter in spite of its danger. He, however, prevailed in the end by drawing their attention to a notice on the entrance gate, which was to the effect that in Paris cameras, like dogs in England, are not admitted to the cemeteries. As was quite natural, the home school boys had a passage with the visiting school girls. The youth of France found what they thought was the weak spot in the armour of the girls. " Regardez les chapeaux! regardez les chapeaux !" they cried. In England we should have been content by asking "Where did you get that hat?" but the French boys called to- gether all and sundry to look at girls in straw hats such as they thought were specially re- served for the masculine half of mankind. The girls had taken their straw hats with school badges to secure quick identification of one , another in crowds and strange places, a very wise I arrangement. IThe girls on their part were not slow to sum up the peculiarities of their boy critics. They , do not admit that they called after them: "Regardez les socks; regardez les socks," but they declare that the most striking things they saw in France were boys sft. 10in, in height, or as tall as (naming the biggest boy in the Secondary School), wearing short socks. These young giants in socks, backed up by Trilbies, high turned-down collars, and knee breeches, were well calculated to attract the attention of the girls in straw hats—and they succeeded. -</p>
        <p>Li berg, Versailles, the Louvre, the Seine, St. Cloud, the Pantheon, and all the other won- ders of Paris and its neighbourhood were well investigated. The party reached home last Saturday, having had a "most glorious holiday."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>GUARDIANS' CHAIRMAN.</head>
        <p>GUARDIANS' CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. Jolley Elected to the Position. HIS LONG EXPERIENCE.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held on Wednesday, the Clerk being elected to the chair pro tem. In moving that Mr. A. J. Jolley be appointed chairman, Mr. J. W. Knowles referred to Mr. Jolley's long experience of Poor Law adminis- tration and his well-known ability for the able conduct of public business. Mr. Jolley had al- ways been found to give every member fair treatment had had always conducted the busi- ness of his committee, and of the Board, when he had been called upon to act, with courtesy and tact. Mr. J. Billinge seconded and the proposition was supported by Messrs. Flanagan, Mather and T. Dale. The proposition having been carried unani- mously, Mr. Jolley thanked the iri.,,imbers and assured them that no effort would be wanting to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <p>, • ~,.:. ("'",,"',:•:.-'-''' . , . • : , I, .-...„. „.,,,,,:.,.....,..,...,..,. . ~i ',.-,*'s;i ~.. ...•-•i•4)).,.' ' ' •., ', •• .."4" ~..;•''i.,:,5,.7.•,,,;‘•,,,,,';‘, -..• , : '44 '-,"' ::: •. ' . , ,&lt;,i4i,) . ; .;,.•?. ;,, •‘ 1 ' ',V,</p>
        <p>Photograph by Birtle. THE NEW CHAIRMAN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <p>conduct the business in an efficient and smooth manner during the year. (Applause.) It was unanimously decided, on the proposi- tion/of Mr. Knowles, seconded by Mr. Hughes, that Mr. W. Furness be the vice-chairman. Mr Furness returned thanks. THE COMMITTEES.</p>
        <p>_ The committees were all re-appointed, aml the names of Mr. Garnett and the Rev. A. M. Mitchell were added to the Padgate Homes Committee. The Building and office sites Building Committees were merged into one com- mittee, to be called the Building Committee, and a Farm Committee for the two institutions was appointed for the first time, the following being the members: Messrs. Mather, Robinson, Bent, .Lee, Longton and J. Backhouse. WAY TICKETS FOR TRAMPS.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the Secretary Pf the Inter-County Committee on Vagrancy, stating that the Executive Committee had decided to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <p>submit a scheme for the establishment of the way ticket system in the inter-county area of Lancashire, Cheshire, Cumberland and West- morland for vagrants, notwithstanding the fact that several Boards of Guardians had not yet expressed their decision to join in the proposed combination. _</p>
        <p>It was explained that the Warrington Board hud agreed to join. The Board approved the resolution of the Assessment Committee fixing the Clerk's salary to that committee as £l6O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>OVERSEERS.</head>
        <p>OVERSEERS.</p>
        <p>The following Overseers were elected : PoultOn-with-Fearnhead : Messrs. G. Walker, R. Denham, and W. Potter; Penketh : Messrs. J. Ditchfield, J. G. Withinshaw, and Dr. Murray; Burtonwood : Messrs. T. Norcott and J. Warbur- ton; Houghton; Messrs. William Stanyer and Daniel Massey; Woolston : Messrs. N. Massey and 11. T. Houghton; Ilixton : Messrs. John. Hol- land and A. Robertson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>RESOLUTIONS.</head>
        <p>RESOLUTIONS.</p>
        <p>The following resolutions were carried by the Board :- 1) " That the Board be represented at the North-Western and Central Poor Law Confer- ences when convened by the Conference Secre- tarieS, such representatives to be the Chairman and Vice-chairman (or as the Board may other- wise appoint after the receipt from the Clerk of special notice of such meetings) and the Clerk." (2) "That the Board be represented at the meetings of the association of Poor Law Unions when convened by the Secretary (Mr. Jolley and the Clerk have for several years past been ap- pointed the two representatives, the work being largely of a continuous character)." (3) " That a Ladies' Visiting committee and a Brabazon Ladies' Committee be nominated the House Committee for appointment by the Board, with power to all members thereof to visit the Workhouse and Cottage Homes and Schools at their discretion."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>SCHOOL HYGIENE.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL HYGIENE.</p>
        <p>Medical Officer Reports on the Year's Work.</p>
        <p>Dr. G. W. N. Joseph, school medical officer, ' yesterday issued a lengthy report on school hygiene. In the early part the doctor deals with the condition of the schools, and says some im- provements have been effected. Dealing with the prevention of infectious disease, he says measures are taken whereby the Education Department is kept informed by the Health Department of all children suffering; from infectious disease and also of those who: have been brought into contact with the disease, and is at the same time advised as to the period of exclusion from school necessary in each case. Before a child who has suffered from scarlet fever or diphtheria is allowed to return to school, he is examined by the Medical Officer of Health at the office of the Health Department, and in the case of a diphtheria convalescent, the return to school is not permitted unless a bac- teriological examination of the child's throat has failed to detect the presence of infection. In no instance is a convalescent case of scarlet fever or diphtheria allowed to return until a lapse of a fortnight from the discharge of the; case from hospital, or, if treated at home, from ' the final disinfection of the •house. One hun- dred and forty-five scarlet fever convalescents and 57 diphtheria convalescents have been thus examined during the year before their return to school.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>IRISH PICNIC.</head>
        <p>THE IRISH PICNIC.</p>
        <p>Kind of Food Upon Which the " Apostles " Feed. " IRISH FREEDOM."</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—Your correspondent, "One of the Apostles," endeavours to saddle Irish Home Rulers with responsibility for the statements made in a paper called "Irish Freedom." It is ' as if trade unionists were to be held responsible for the utterances of a Syndicalist journal like "The Daily Herald." Now, the fact is that "Irish Freedom" is as bitter a foe of the Irish Nationalist party as any Unionist paper, while it speaks for an insignificant fraction of Irish people. I would advise you to accept your correspondent's offer of an inspection of the files of the paper and publish a list of the articles to be found in "Irish Freedom." upon Mr. Red- mond and his followers.</p>
        <p>W. IL DAVEY. "The VlStet guardian," 29, Rosemary-street, Belfast, 21st April, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>BELFAST LABOUR CONDITIONS AND WAGES.</head>
        <p>BELFAST LABOUR CONDITIONS AND WAGES.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>he Apostles" are unanimous on one point—that Nationalist Ireland is poverty- stricken and unprosperous, whereas Orange Ire- land is industrious and prosperous .I'ney give as an example that dear wee home of tolerance, Belfast, and praise with the utmost zeal its re- markable prosperity, certainly as an Irishman I wonder how has the thing which they desig- nate "prosperity" been obtained? Let us con- sider the realities of life in Belfast, facts about which your local stalwarts are fond of dogma tising—incorrectly.</p>
        <p>Trades Unionis-ts! There are 28,000 women workers in the mills and factories of Belfast, between the ages of 18 and 60. If the number of women employed in the warehouses be added, the number is 33,000. A high percentage of these women are married, their husbands being labourers in other than shipbuilding yards. The wages of these labourers run from 10s. 6d. to 17s. per week. The labourers employed in the ship.</p>
        <p>building yards earn from. ,t 1 to 1 3s. per week. The low rate of wages paid to the men makes , it absolutely necessary for the wives to go to work. The hours of labour for mill-workers in Belfast are, as a rule, from 6.30 a.m. to 6 p.m., and the average weekly wage is about 10s. 6d. to 12s. It has been said by some moralists of in- dustry that a woman can easily earn 275. per week in a Belfast mill. I have made many inquiries on this point, and whenever I sug- gested to a worker that she could make 275. a week comfortably I was laughed at for my pains. "Yes" was the reply, "we can earn that twice or three times a year by working like slaves, but our average wages are from 10s. 6d. to 12s. per week "—and for proof a secretary of one of their unions showed me Government figures for 1912-13. The wages paid in Belfast in the textile trade, as shown by these figures, are as follows: MEN. s. d. Roughers (piece work) 1 2 6 Weavers (piece work) WOMEN. Spinners (time) Feelers (piece work) Weavers (two looms) o 12 0</p>
        <p>The married woman worker in Belfast earning from 10s. 6d. to 12s. a week pays 3s. 6d. per week to a neighbour to take care of her baby— if she has one. This sum does not include the provision of food, and if she has older children she pays an additional sum of is. Rents run from 3s. 6d. to ss. 6d., and, generally speaking, the houses are better than might be expected. This is due to the fact that the bulk of houses in the city have been built within the past 25 years. About 15 years ago the speculative builders erected so many houses of good quality that they were unable to find tenants for them all, and many of the builders were ruined. The effect of this, of course, wa-§ to keep the rants low. •</p>
        <p>I met a woman in Belfast who was suffering from English cholera. When she spoke she did not make complaint. Things were and there was no more to be said about it! She was ter- ribly ill, and nothing short of six months' care- ful nursing, rest and feeding would make her well again; and when I learned the conditions under which she worked I was not astonished at her appearance. She is a weaver, and works continually in a temperature of 85 to 90 degrees of steam in order to weave fine linen. The yarn has to be kept moist, for if it were not damp it would become brittle, and fine linen could not be made, and all day long the woman has to work amid pipes from which steam is constantly blown out. In such an atmosphere health must decline. If the reader will bear in mind the meagre wages paid to these women. the poorness of their homes, and the inadequacy of the food they eat, and will also remember that they spend about 10 hours a day in a hot, steamy, sickening atmosphere, he will not find it difficult to understand why it is that so many are ill. Dr. Bailie, the Medical Officer of Health for Belfast, in his last report, said:•" Many of the workers are utterly unable for such work, owing to want of sufficient nourishment and clothing." These "Apostles," who "hum" the prosperity of this whited sepulchre, filled with the bones of sweated men and women, should remember that it is not a question of religious intolerance, which rankles in the minds of these poor sons of the soil. They are anxious to find an Ulster problem, to prove that their holiday in Ireland shows a dismemberment of the Irish nation, and to show that Ireland does not want Home Rule. They return from Ireland, having enjoyed the "bounties" of those who sent them, and ,attempt to throw a dirty, odoriferous red herring across the nostrils of the Warrington people. Gentlemen, here is your real problem—a problem which can only be settled by an Irish Parlia- ment, and not by such Shauneens as those who • try to make King Billy kick harder. F. W. R.</p>
        <p>P.S.—I note that one of the noble gang of soothsayers claims the "Irish Freedom" as an organ full of Nationalist sympathies, and states that the Irish party do not repudiate its views. The "Irish Freedom" is run by the Irish Republican Brotherhood, and has one idee fix 4 of denouncing the Irish Parliamentary party.— F.W.R.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
