      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>PLAGUE OF FLIES.</head>
        <p>PLAGUE OF FLIES.</p>
        <p>Disease Carriers that Should Receive no Quarter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>DESTRUCTION TO THE EGGS.</head>
        <p>DESTRUCTION TO THE EGGS.</p>
        <p>BY C. E. PARKER.</p>
        <p>—••• The breeding season of the common house fy is now beginning. A calculation made- by Dr. L. 0. Howard, an authority on the 'subject, illustrates the enormous possibilities of increase- He begins with a female fly laying 120 eggs OR the 15th of April. On the supposition that all eggs and all individual flies survive, he makes out the possible number of fiies, the issue of the one female, by the end of the season, to be 5,598,720,000,000. There is a new generation about every fourteen days, varying with the weather, from the middle of April to the raidd/o of September, so that the original fly that had survived the winter at the end of the season becomes a grandmother with about ten prefixes of "great" attached. Of course, this enormous quantity never come to life. Many eggs never mature, and all female flies do not reach the breeding stage. Like all other things they have their natural enemies, both in the egg and maggot stage and as developed flies. Birds and fowls, for example, devour the eggs and maggots.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>HOW FLIES DEVELOP.</head>
        <p>HOW FLIES DEVELOP.</p>
        <p>Mr. Edward HaHord Ross, M.R.C.S., L.R.C.P., in his paper on "House-flies and Disease," says:—</p>
        <p>"Each female fly lays about 120 eggs at a sit- ting—there are frequent sittings when the weather is warm and the eggs are laid in batches, little clumps of small white oval ob- jects, in and around any putrid, fermenting, or rotting thing.</p>
        <p>" In a few hours, if the surroundings are warm, each one cracks and gives birth to a tiny white 1! crawling maggot or grub, like a silkworm cater- pillar. Each maggot eats the filth it lives in, ' and as it eats it grows and moults, leaving pale transparent skins to tell its tale of life behind it. These empty bags of skin help us to recog- nise fly-breeding places, and enable us to find and deal with them. At the end of five summer days from the time of the hatching of the egg, the maggot becomes a chrysalis—a minute, rolled-up, bean-like body, less than a quarter of an inch in length, white at first, then brown, lying motionless amongst the straws of horses' bedding, or appearing in small but numerous clusters at the corners of a manure heap, or at the edge of a dustbin, or around a refuse tub. Within this bean-shaped body the fly we know so well is made:"</p>
        <p>DISEASE CARRIERS. John Ruskin described the fly as "The queen of the air." Had he realised her life, and the work she does be probably would have altered his description. The house fly is more aptly described as the queen of everything and any- thing unclean, but then it only follows the dic- tates of its being. Flies swallow and wallow in spit, excreta, and any other moist mess lying about. They flit about from filth to food and carry the dirt upon their feet, wings and mouth from one to the other. Students of diseases are finding that the fly is the enemy against which a relentless war must be waged. House flies con- vey several diseases, such as typhoid, opthalmia, cholera, tuberculosis, swine fever, and possibly diphtheria and smallpox. But the most serious disease carried is infantile enteritis, or sum- mer sickness of children, which is terribly fatal to child life. This is carried by the common house fly, indirectly by the infection of food and directly by transference from one infected per- son to another. The question of the transmis- sion of this bacillus from one child to another was considered by Morgan and Ledingham. A number of flies caught in houses in which there had been children sick with this infantile enteri- tis were examined, and the same germ found in them. They were killed with ether vapour, and their bodies examined, and the germs found in and on the flies caught. Thus house 'flies are convicted of being directly concerned in the transmission and spread of the disease. In 1911 (very warm summer) 36 per 1,000 in- fant deaths resulted from enteritis, while in 1912 (u-et summer) only 10 were recorded. . .</p>
        <p>, Tlie total deaths from diarrhceal disease under two years of age in England and Wales in 1911 were 38,467; 1912, 7,445.</p>
        <p>The Warrington figures are not, by contrast, so marked as the general average, because the number of deaths did not rise in 1911 so much as in other parts. The deaths from enteritis under one year in 1911 were 19; 1912, 9.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>FLIES MORE DESTRUCTIVE THAN</head>
        <p>FLIES MORE DESTRUCTIVE THAN</p>
        <p>BULLETS.</p>
        <p>Serious attention was drawn to the house fly during the South African and Spanish-American • Wars. It was during these expeditions that the part played by house flies in the spread of typhoid fever became obvious. During the American War in Cuba one-fifth of the soldiers contracted typhoid fever, and the prevalence of the disease (typhoid or enteric fever) amongst our officers and men in South Africa, with the Bloemfontein epidemic, will be remembered by all. The disease carried by flies disabled more British soldiers than the bullets of the enemy. These insects were responsible for the outlay of some millions of pounds and many lives in the Boer War.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>ELIMINATING HOUSE FLIES.</head>
        <p>ELIMINATING HOUSE FLIES.</p>
        <p>- - --- It is useless to try to tackle the problem by attempting to kill individual flies; the breeding- -1 places of such must be got rid of. Fortunately, flies remain in the maggot stage for at least a week, therefore we need only deal with the breeding-places once a week, but this should be done regularly and perseveringly throughout the whole of the fly-breeding season. All manure heaps, stables, slaughter-houses, garbage, refuse depots, old bags, collections of waste food, dust bins, ash boxes, middens, and all the filth near dwelling-houses should be cleared once a week. Each householder should be responsible for look- ing after his own premises. Sanitary authorities can do a good deal. Our local authority is pre- pared at any time to spray the walls of dwelling- houses and disinfect the premises if the inmates are troubled by an excessive number of flies. In the work of eradicating the pest they must have the intelligent co-operation of the public. ,Care should be exercised all through the summer. Food of all kinds should be properly, covered, especially such an article as milk, which, from its nature, is specially adapted to the cultiva- tion of disease germs. Everyone should con- sider the house fly as a•loathsome, dangerous pest, talk about it as such to his neighbours, and fight it at every point. In the towns on the Suez and Panama Canals mosquito breeding- places are all dealt with regularly once a week by organised gangs of sanitary inspectors, known as "mosquito brigades." The building of UT Panama Canal almost came to a standstill until sanitary engineers tackled the problem of the flies. Why cannot we have "fly brigades"?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>BOY SCOUTS TO THE RESCUE.</head>
        <p>BOY SCOUTS TO THE RESCUE.</p>
        <p>An interesting experiment in fly reduction has been recently conducted in New York. The organiser of this work is the chief of the New York Association for Improving the Conditions of the Poor. In the above Boy Scouts were em- ployed. The Boy Scouts in Warrington could undertake a supremely useful piece of work by tracking down the breeding-places of the° flies and destroying the eggs and maggots.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall Independent Methodists. The Grappenhall Independent Methodists are holding a bazaar in their new Assembly Room, Knutsford-road, on 7th, Bth and 9th May, to raise £3OO needed by the Building Fund.</p>
        <p>ne. •</p>
      </div>
