      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>[ILL nit:HITS RESERVED]</head>
        <p>[ILL nit:HITS RESERVED]</p>
        <p>ROMANCE IN THE GUTTER.</p>
        <p>To the stranger who has wandered fromi the main roads and wealthy quarters of a' town and lost himself among the small streets and alleys frequented by the poor, no sights could appear more sordid or more empty of the glamour of romance, than those which everywhere drive .in upon his eyes. Penury and squalor, ugliness and de- cay, and above all, a dead monotony of sombre brickwork; these are the materials which go to the making of the scene. As for the living elements of the picture, they are in keeping with the rest. They consist for the most part of children; babies untend- ed are squatting on the pavement; boys in noisy gangs rush to and fro with an aimless activity; girls are gathered in groups round the doorways now listless and now seething with a frenzied though irrelevant energy. A WORLD OF WONDER.</p>
        <p>But whether it is the houses where the paint is flaking off in spots from the smoke- stained walls, or whether it is the children clad in miscellaneous garments of varying antiquity and shod with boots whose sizes bear, no relation to the feet they strive to cover, all that catches the attention of the stranger, stamps on his mind a single thought. Here, he says to himself, is a kind of life, which might well be a ,nightmare if it were not appallingly real, a life furnished with the bare minimum which, makes exist- ence possible but deprived of all which makes existence pleasant and beautiful or ever tolerable, a life which those that lead it choose to continue merely because the blind instinct to survive will not permit them tc seek an end of the sorry business. But the stranger, so far at any rate as the children are concerned, would be altogether wrong in his gloomy impression. To the child for a faw brief years has been given nature's most precious gift, the gift of imagination. While this treasure is his he can conjure up out of the most commonplace and uninspiring sur- roundings a world of wonder, of mystery, of romance.</p>
        <p>If you could transport yourself into the centre of that ring of girls who are grouped on the pavement, you would find yourself plunged in the heart of a great conspiracy. Some maiden has invented a secret and im- parted it to her most trusted friends. Tc reveal its meaning to the casual readel would end in merely exciting his ridicule, we will therefore keep silence on the subject. But the girls drink in the words with bated breath, form themselves into a society, whom common bond is the bond of common know- le)clge, and vow to speak of the awful thing only among themselves. Watch them as they mix with their companions and note their conscious superiority. The others detect the atmosphere of mystery, stamp their feet in impotent rage when they fail to fathom its significance and, finally, when wheedling and threats have proved alike in vain, with- draw in sullen disappointment. But to the conspirators life has become rich and intense --never did band of anarchists feel keener thrills of excitement than do these quaint dishevelled maidens—so long as the friend- ship lasts and treachery lifts not its fatal head.</p>
        <p>A FOX-BITE.</p>
        <p>----- Or turn to those boys, who are gazing at a companion in awestruck admiration, and in- vestigate the cause of their wonder. He is revealing to them an ugly sore upon his wrist. You are inclined -to pity him, but with disdain he scouts your sympathy. In- quiry tells you that the acre is of his own making. With diligence throughout the past week, openly it? the street and furtively in the schoolroom, he has scratched his wrist with a pen point or gnawed the skin with his teeth, keeping the round open until an , angry patch of red swelling appears. If you are a friend he will inform you it is a ' fax-bit.", and, though he has never seen a fox and Would not recognise one if he did, he has all the elated feeling of a hunter who has dared unmentionable dangers . and emerged not scathless frcm the terrible adventure. Here and there you may light on strange ceremonies, all out of keeping with the sur- rourdings of a town and suggesting dim • memories of some former existence when life lay amid fields and flowers. A few of the old English singing games survive; you may hear of "'wall flowers growing, up on high," you may watch a group of children "gather- ing nuts in May," while from some secluded corner the plaintive strains of "round and round the village" strike monotonously on the ear. On a certain day in the year, fal- ling somewhere about the first of May, you may encounter little gangs of children wav- ing emaciated branches, to Which are at- tached a few withered leaves—they call them May-poles. Or again early in August you may discover groups of children squat- ting on the pavement and intently engaged on a most serious piece of work. They have gathered together a few pebbles, several handful of duet and some oyster 'shells. Of these they have constructed a small mound, decorating the ton with blades of grass, bor- rowed from a distant park:—they call it a grotte. yet never a one has heard of St. James of Campistello. To the players these different occupations appear chareed with some deep meaning and are Performed with a grave and dignified solemnity MA OF THE STREETS. But "we have no need to hark back upon ancestral memories or grope amid mysterious customs to find romance in the life of the , • slum child. Even the streets, to the stranger altogether dcpressina, are to the children an enchanted land of wonder. A walk there resembles a journey in fairyland. They are transported from the dense shad- ews of their natural dwelling into a light dazzling to the eye in its bewildering brilli- ancy. They pass from the narrorw confine- ment of a prison into a Space where many steps can be taken without serioas, risk of collieion. Over its boundless fields is sown a 'whole universe of enthralling attractions. Two monstrous serpents, trailing their un- ending length in opposite directions, drag cargoes 'of helplees victims numbed past re- sistance, and, insatiate of prey, are ever halting to gather, more. Awesome dragons . - controlled by goagle-eyed monsters hurtle by. leaving behiud them thin wisps of evil- smelling rmoke. Behind sheets of crystal are spread out all the treasures of the world, -rXVishing to the eye, but, like fairy-land it- self, jut beyond the children's reach. Kind- ly wizards for the price of a farthing offer to them the sea-green nectar Of the gods or • frozep ambrosia fresh come from Heaven, while troubadours, clad in a picturesquely peaelei.d garb, sing in dolorous strains th-e Lay of another and a.happier land. THE SLUM CHILD'S HOME. When the magic of the streets is gone the • child's thoughts turn from the romance of the woed nihside to the romance of the world within. Hidden deep in the heart of one of those ponderous wastes of brickwork lies a tiny nook or cranny the child calls his own. Dark, crowed. with over many people, confined, p the stranger t seems a place yet moreto i dismal than the dismal accessories of the ste-at not to he called a home by one' careful about the meaning of the words he • But at-rain macs. the child sees it with dif• avant eyes. To him it is a place woafltslrdeif t nt uz.heitort. 'watch he mny repair, when tlhe out proves harsh and unk filled too with articles which in some strange fashion are woven into the very texture ci his existence ? Was it not on that email bed, propped up by afirN.3l:ina,nttosne,lcorinaetruanneoc. strewn ever _ 'rap. that his sister years ego-sdee)1()Il becdoweour,t her little life Are cot thc rl ea and dried grasses in the cracked vase on the mantelp:ece treasures or:6e brought back w bnaock from an excursion to the country cherished as the. fond memories o!.?.. long vanished dream .</p>
      </div>
