      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>IN THE COURtS. BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURtS. BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY. • Before Alderman Tinnion (in the chair), Mr. A. J. Jolley, and Mr. T. Pemberton. In Jeweller's Shop.—Michael Preston, 37, Bewsey-street, was, charged with being found on the enclosed premises of Mr. Ryder, jeweller, Bewsey-street, 'for an unlawful purpose at-one. a.m. the previous Saturday morning.—The Chief Constable said that Acting-Sergeant Whitby heard an unusual noise -in Bewsey-street that morning, and on making a search found the prisoner at the rear of the premises with a long iron bar in his possession. Prisoner made a small excuse. The bolts of the door had been broken, but although prisoner had got into the back of the shop he had not reached the shop properly.—Prisoner : "I am sorry for what I have done. I have been out of work for six weeks and the temptation came on."—He was sent to prison for one month. Footballer and His Wife.—Patrick Gill; 92, Hoyle-street, was charged with being £9 15s. in arrears of maintenance towards his wife, Agnes Gill, 3, Elizabeth-street.—Mr. Unsworth ap- peared for the prisoner.—Mrs. Gill said the order for 7s. 6d. per week was made in 1909, and her husband had not paid anything for three years. She had lived with him, for six months about three years ago, and since that time she agreed to have ss. per week. Her husband was an amateur footballer and had had a trial with Notts Forest.—Cross-examined by Mr. Unsworth,l she did not know her husband had been incap- able of earning money owing to an injury ta his foot. She would sooner come to terms than send her husband to prison.—Mr. Unsworth said the man had not-.worked for nine months, owing to an injury received whilst playing foot- ball as an amateur. Prisoner had no money, and was not in ,work. He suggested the case be adjourned for 'one month until the man got pay bsse.enperswwaneekkin;.- Thewo work, Chief tchoalti s eb es h: o unl de has round the town enough. The arrangement for ss. per week was mutual, and be has come back saying what he can do and what Ire can't do. The order is ultra vires.—Mr. Unsworth : He has receipts for a lot of 5. payments.—Mr. Laws: She will be satisfied with 7s. 6d., not ss. —Mr. Unsworth : We are prepared to pay if we can get work, but we cannot get blood out of stone.—Prisoner said he had played for the Barnsley team and was then getting 50s. per week. He had not been getting compensation for his injury.—The Chief Constable: Our ex- perience with him is that he can almost get money out of stone.—The Bench ordered prisoner to pay £5, or e. to prison for two months. "A Dirty Trick."—James Lawrinson, 28, Jockey-street, was fined 10s: and costs for an assault on Mary Jane Lawrinson, 46, Warbur- ton-street, and Mary Toby, 41, Warburton- street.—Prisoner said he had had drink, and was "sorry for doing such a dirty trick as to hit the women."</p>
        <p>TUESDAY. • Before Councillor H. Roberts (in the chair), Messrs. J. Rymer Young, and C. ,Jackson</p>
        <p>Holmes.</p>
        <p>Cruelty to a Cock.—David Algy, 39, Plutnr.ton- street, and Frederick Simcock (15), 95, Plum;:' ton-street, were charged with cruelty to a cock by stoning and kicking it.—Mrs. Worrall, 101, Evelyn-street, said that when she went to fe0:1 her fowls On Thursday, April 9th, she missed the cock. On looking round she found it " bleeding tremendously." AS the result ef the two defendants were accused, and tiiey admitted the offence to Inspector Dee, of if te S.P.C.A. They now pleaded not guilty, and Algy was fined 20s. and costs, or a month, and Simcock was ordered to pay the costs. Deserted From Cairo.—William Stokes, alia Walter Williams, was charged with being a deserter from the 2nd Battalion Devon Regi- ment, stationed at Cairo, since March 16th. Ile surrendered himself, and the polh-y_, found I:is name in the "Police Gazette." He as ordered to await an escort. .</p>
        <p>THURSDAY. Before Messrs: J. Rymer Young (in the A. J. Jolley, W. Roberts, Dr. tia,cien, aiJ</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett.</p>
        <p>Hidden in Mail Cart—Annie Domigan, ife of Thomas Domigan, of Bruche Lodge, Man- chester-road, was charged with stealing 29yds. of zephyr dress material, value 9s. lid.. fium the Manchester Warehouse, 83 and 85, ButLer- market-street, the property of Mary Eliza Eaton. She was further charged with stealing .a child's satinlrock, value 10s. 6d., the property of Mary Fisher, from a stall in the Market- place.—The Chief Constable said that the pre- vious afternoon the prisoner made a small pur- chase at the Manchester Warehouse, and on leaving the shop she took the dress material and placed it in a mail cart. She was detected and the police were informed. About four o'clock the same afternoon prisoner was in the Market, when she took the other articles. She admitted the thefts to Inspector Forster.—The Chairman said that the prisoner taking two lots of goods within a shot time of each other did away with the idea that it was a sudden temptation.— Prisoner, who pleaded guilty, was fined 10s., in- cluding costs, in each case. £5 Breast Pin Sold for 6d.—Catherine Taylor, 19, James-street, married woman, wilts charged with, on or about June 7th, 1913, stealing, by finding, a gentleman's gold breast pin, value £5,1 the property of Mr. James Cassidy, 27, Orchard - street. John Patrick •Mahon, 10, James-street, was charged with receiving.—The Chief Con- stable asked for a remand until Saturday.! Some twelve months ago a Mr. Cassidy was! passing along Dial-street, when he was inter- fered with by a woman, and later he missed the - pin. The matter was then reported to the police, and a woman was charged in connection with the pin. Every possible inquiry was made, without any success. During the present week the male prisoner unsuccessfully attempted to pawn it for 65.. He next turned up at a jewel- ler's shop and asked what it was worth, and upon being informed it was gold prisoner said "Let's have 6d. for it." The jeweller was sus- picious and gave the man the money and im- mediately informed the police. The female prisoner said she found it and had been waiting for a reward to be offered.—The male prisoneb said he was asked to pledge it, and that was all he knew about it.—The Chief Constable said he was prepared to reduce the value of the pin to £2, so that the case could be decided that morn- ing and save prisoners from going for trial, if they would admit the offence. He only asked for the remand to get the necessary witnesses.— , The Bench ordered a remand. "A Window Breaker."—Annie Moran, of no settled abode, .was charged with breaking a win- dow at the Fives Court in 3duseum-street. She informed Acting-Sergeant Whitby of her offence at 4.40 that morning, saying she had done it because she wanted to get locked up.—The Chief Constable said that on a previous night during the week she had been accommodathd in the Police9eenr.s_. eTeS\i,lete antion because she had no where to go. —t The Chairman: Is she a Suffragette? (Laugh- &gt;. ChiefasthCeyonhsatavle)e :(LShaueghhatsein‘.)o_t sahse was sent to prison for a month. '</p>
        <p>FRIDAY. Before Dr. Joseph (in the chair), Alderman Bolton, Messrs. S. Wright, G. Pilling, W. Fur- ness, A. J. Jolley, and T. Locker. Boys at Back Doors.—James Bowles, 129, Cart- wright-street, was summoned for assaulting Arthur Terry (6), 95, Cartwright-street, on the 19th April.—lt would appear that severali boys were playing round the defendant's back door. and defendant stated that they were throwing stones over the wall and one nearly hit his child in the bassinette. He ran after the boys and caught the lad with the intention of shaking him. The boy fell and cut his face.—lt was stated by another boy that complainant ,was about to kick the gate, but did not do so.=The Chairman said there was considerable provoca- tion and the clie would be dismissed. Unhappy Marriage—_Roger Burrows, 25, Sand- hill-terrace, off Wash-lane, was ,summoned for</p>
        <p>Continued in next column</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 25, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 25, 1914.</p>
        <p>iffmsmicTfrlEL</p>
        <p>STOCKTON II EATH</p>
        <p>TOWNSHIP TM:LE-TATTLE. The modera Adams prayed this week For a copious downpour of rain. •• These amateur gardeners got it Yesterday, but not in the quantity desired. The motor cyclists meet at the Square to-day. They are going to a hill-climbing test. Everybody is glad Fletcher's fitrike is ended. Many of the strikers live in the Heath, And the shopkeepers there felt the effect of the strike. The police are pleased that peace has come. They had a worrying time -during the' strike: The manner in which they acted is being • Praised by the public and the strikers alike. They are very taetful men Ure the Heath police officers.</p>
        <p>Measles Epidemic.—lleasles is rather preva- lent in Stockton Heath at present, and several new cases were reported last week. Nursing Association.—At the annual meeting of the Stockton Heath Nursing Association Mr. Vere B. Davies was re-elected president and Mr. Woodcock was elected secretary. :Committee Re-appointed.—At the meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council on Tues- day, at the Dutton Workhouse, the Walton Sewerage Committee was re-appointed.</p>
        <p>Parochial Committee.—The members of the Stockton Heath Parish Council, with the addi- tion of Mr. Pilling, were ,elected 'the Parochial Committee at the meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council at Dutton Workhouse on Tues- day. Local Wedding.—A pretty wedding, in which Miss Ethel, only daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Brocklehurst, and Mr. Arthur Mills Dunn, were the contracting -parties, took place on Thurs- day. A full account, with the list of presents, will be found on another page.</p>
        <p>Motorists' "Meet."—The members of the War- rington and District Amateur Motor Cycle Club Meet at Victoria Square, Stockton Heath, this afternoon at 2.15, prior to their departure to Bucklow Hill, where a special meet and gymkhana is to be held under the auspices of the North-Western Automobile Association.</p>
        <p>Appeal for the Choir.—A special appeal for the choir fund was made at Walton Parish Church on Sunday, and there was a good response. The congregation includes many Stockton Heath people. 'A special feature of the music rendered was "I know my Redeemer liveth," to the Qmposition of a former resident of the district, Mr. T. M. Pattison. The Rev.' D. Darwall, son of the Vicar, and now of Essex, was the special preacher. Hill Cliffe Anniversary.—There were enjoy- able doings at Hill Cliffo Baptist Church on Sun- day, when the Sunday School anniversary was held. The speccal preacher, morning and even- ing, was the Rev. William Reid, of the Presby- terian Church, Warrington, and he delivered two Mbst appropriate discourses. At the even- ing service Miss C. Hastings and Mr. E. Walker rendered excellent solos. Mi. Smith was the organist. A special musical service was given in the afternoon, and this was attended by the Boys' Brigade, Scouts, and the children of the Sunday School. Mr. Nelson presided, and Mr. A. Moore gave an address that greatly appealed to the children. The solos vere admirably ren- dered by the Misses B. Brough and Eva Tanner. The collections, in aid of the Sunday School Eund, realised upwards of ca.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>PEN K ETH.</head>
        <p>PEN K ETH.</p>
        <p>Coming-of-age Celebrations.—A gathering of relatives and friends to commemorate the com- ing of age of Walter, the second son of Mr. and Mrs. J. Jr Poden, Warrington-road, Penketh, was held in the Wesleyan Schoolroom. About fifty guests sat down to tea, after which a musi- cal evening followed, interspersed with various mirth-provoking games.</p>
        <p>Child's Miraculous Escape.—The one year and eight months' old baby of Mr. W. Pye, Victoria. road, l'enketh, had a miraculous escape from death on Saturday. Taken by its father for an outing, it was left in its carriage for a few moments while Mr. Pye transacted some busi- ness a short distance away. In the meantime a high :wind blew a 25-foot ladder down from a building, this falling across the carriage. The child only escaped instantaneous death by its body being cleared through the aperture be- tween the rungs. Band at Whist.—The Penketh Tannery Prize I Brass Band held their annual whist drive and dance in the Great Sankey Schoolroom. A goodly company, nunabering about 140, showed their appreciation of this event. The M.C.'s Were: Whist drive, Messrs E. Dumbill and G. Carman; dancing, Messrs. Plumpton and A. Molyueux. The whist prize-winners were— Ladies—i. Miss P. Moores, umbrella; 2, Mrs. J. Hall, handbag; 3, Mrs. E. Dumbill, purse; gentlemen-1, Mr. B. Darlington, travelling bag; 2, Mr. Herbert Davies, shaving set; 3, Mr. E. Blackburn, pipe. blusic was supplied by Messrs. J. and P. Hall (piano and violin respec- tively), who are -in great request for these social gatherings. The" Wedding Glide" Lancers was a special feature of the programme, and requests for a repetition were made again and again.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>PARI,SII COUNCIL MEETING. The Supply of Electricity.</head>
        <p>PARI,SII COUNCIL MEETING. The Supply of Electricity.</p>
        <p>At the annual meeting of the Parish Council the question of electric lighting was raised. The Lancashire Electric Power Company, Manches- ter, hold statutory powers to supply electricity for power purposes. therefore the Warrington Corporation cannot obtain an order to supply electricity within the area of the company with- out their consent. Correspondence with the !Power Company shows that at present they are not prepared to bring the/it- mains anywhere near this district. Therefore the Clerk was authorised to write to the Electric Company asking them that, as there was no prospect of their mains being brought within a working distance, would they object to the Warrington Corporation supplying Penketh, as they are willing, providing there is sufficient support. DUSTY ROADS. ' A complaint had been sent to the Surveyor in regard to the watering of the roads. Councillor R. Routledge proposed and Colin. eillor W. Davies seconded that the Surveyot should be thanked for his letter promising that roads in the district should be watered fre- quently.</p>
        <p>In regard to the condition of the road and path through, the village, the reply received from the County Office, Preston, was that in the estimates for the coming year, allowance had been made for surfacing the carriage way through the village with tar macadam, and the work would be put in hand as soon as possible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>HOUSING QUESTIONS.</head>
        <p>HOUSING QUESTIONS.</p>
        <p>It was reported that the fsPec—t.o7.was making practical progress in house inspection through- out the Council's district. The Co-operative Society wrote that the price and conditions rendered it impracticable tc build working-class dwellings at a rent which could be met by ,the ordinary family. A complaint had been made in regard to weak pressure of gas, so tests were made from Stocks- lane to Houghton's Wire Works, and in no case found to be under two inches._</p>
        <p>Dr.' Murray was elected chairman and the vice-chairman is Councillor J. G. Withinshaw The Overseers, Who were re-elected, are Dr. Murray, Mr. J. Ditch&amp;ld, and Mr. J. G. Within-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>1141 THE COURTS—continued.</head>
        <p>1141 THE COURTS—continued.</p>
        <p>deserting his wife, Elizabeth Burrows, and she asked for a separation order.—The wife stated that she had to leave her husband. on Good Fiiday night. They had been married two years and there were no children. For the past 18 months her husband had continually told her to "Sling her hook."—Defendant said he did not. want to have his wife back.—The Bench made an order and granted aliment at is. per week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>GREAT SANKEY. ST. MARY'S PARISH CHURCH.</head>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY. ST. MARY'S PARISH CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Doings at the Vestry Meeting.</p>
        <p>The vestry % meeting was held in the Sunday schoolroom on-ThArsday, when there were preS- ent : Rev. J. R. Jones, chairman; Messrs. New- bold, Littler, Dumbill, Clarke, J. Ray, sen., Graham, Hackney, Haslam, Ricketts, B. Ray, Purness, Garnett and Griffiths. •._</p>
        <p>The accounts showed : Receipts, £266 125.‘2d.; expenditure, g'265 58. 10d.; leaving a balance of £1 Gs. 4d. The special repairs fund accounts showed: Receipts, £B4 2s. Id.; expenditure, £B3 is.; leav- ing a balance of .21 Is. id.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS. The following officers were .elected: Vicar's warden, Mr. F. T. Newbold; people's warden, Mr. E. Dumbill; wardens' sidesmen, Messrs. F. G. Kirtland and J. Ray, sen.; sidesmen Messrs. T. Ray, J. Ray, Howard, Kilshaw, LTaylor, Clarke, Garnett, Millington, Littler, Furness, Haslam, Hustable, Dutton, Fairelough and Webb; auditors, Messrs. George Clarke and F. T. Newbold; church representatives, Messrs. Littler and 'lnstable, providing the hours the meetings were held could be arranged for in the evening instead of the afternoon, otherwise it would be impossible for them to attend. The Vicar proposed a vote of thanks, to all workers in every. department of church life;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>Long Services Fittingly Recognised.—A purse containing has been collected by some of the residents in Grappenhall and given to their late postman, T. C. Pitchford, in recognition of his long services to their district.</p>
        <p>Committee Re-appointed.—At the Meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council at Dutton Workhouse on Tiiesday, the Grappenhall, Latchford Without and Thelwall Sewerage Com- mittee was re-appointed.</p>
        <p>The Late Mr. A. T. Dakin.—As a mark of respect to the late. Mr. Da.kin's connection with the Parish Church, a muffled peal was rung on Sunday. The Rector, in the course of his ser- mon, on Sunday morning, paid a glowing tribute to deceased's work as an ex-church officer, and said no words of praise were too high for the way he carried out his duties.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>PARISH COUNCIL MEETING.</head>
        <p>PARISH COUNCIL MEETING.</p>
        <p>Housing Accommodation Question.</p>
        <p>The annual L'arish Council Meeting was held in the Couit.-., Schools on Tuesday night. Coun- cillor A. L .E.aye was unanimously, elected to the chair. Councillor A. L. Kaye and Mr. F. Pox were a diminted as Overseers. On the proposition of Mr. K. W. Wilde a vote of thanks was accorded to .the Chairman and Overseers for their services, and this was carried. Councillors present Were: Messrs. A. L. Kaye, A. Earwarker, -li, Allen, W. A. Wooding, W. Lomas, 11. W. Wilde, W. Winstanley and J. Bradford, with Mr. C. H. Hoult (clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>QUESTION OF HOUSING ACCOMMODA.</head>
        <p>QUESTION OF HOUSING ACCOMMODA.</p>
        <p>TION. The'Clerk read a letter from Mr. G. P. Ashton, of the Runcorn Rural District Council, which he had received from the Local Government Board as follows: " The Local Government Board have recently written to this Council ask- ing (1) whether there is any shortage of houses for working classes in their area, and, if so, the number of houses required to provide for neces- sary accommodation; (2) the number of vacant .houses in the area suitable for persons of the working classes, and in all respects reasonably fit for human habitation. The Council instruct me to ask that your Perish Council or Parish Meeting will kindly consider these two questions at the earliest possible date, and that you will afterwards give me -the replies so far as youi parish is concerned." , Councillor W. A. Weeding suggested that the Clerk should get in touch with the various firms in the vicinity as to whether the workers had any difficulty in getting houses. ' Ultimately it was agreed to leave the matter in the hands of the Clerk and the Chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>MORE LIGHT WANTED.</head>
        <p>MORE LIGHT WANTED.</p>
        <p>Councillor W. Winstanley brought forward a motion to erect an additional gas lamp at the end of Bell House-lane. Councillor E. Allen proposed that the matte, might be deferred for three months, as the sum- mer time was coming. This was eventually agreed to. The Council sanctioned payment of £3 10s. to Mr. C. James for cindering the footpath between Knutsford-road and Chester-road.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>SEWERAGE SCHEME.</head>
        <p>SEWERAGE SCHEME.</p>
        <p>—.— _ The Chm sa air an id that at a ratepayers' meet- ing he and Councillor J. Bradford were appointed to oppose the above in conjunction with Thel- wall, but, unfortunately; they came in the field I too late. The expenses of the special engineei which, the Chairman said, would be met by subscription. An account of £l2 19s. 7d. as part payment for legal expenses I was passed.</p>
        <p>BOUNDARY QUESTION. Councillor E. Allen said that they could not overlook the successful efforts of ,Vieir,represen- tative, Councillor A. Lister Kaye, in opposition to the boundary alteration, and he moved that a hearty vote of thanks be accorded to him for his services.</p>
        <p>This was carried unanimously. Councillor A. Lister Kaye briefly responded, and said that he had no idea that he should have had to sit there for four days. As far as Grap. penhall was concerned the opposition had not a leg to stand upon. (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild Challenge Shield.—A few years ago, °wing in a great measure to the enthusiasm of Dr. Bennett, the Guilds of the Warrington Circuit decided to compete annually for the holdihg 'of a challenge shield. The test is by attendance at the monthly devotional meetings of the Guilds, and the successful Guild this year proved to be Sankey Bridges. The presen- tation was made at a special meeting held in the schoolroom on Monday evening. The Rev. W. A. Beckett presided, "%nd a report by Mr. K. Bishop was given as to the position of the various Guilds in regard, to the competition. The Rev. E. Morgair made the presentation, and th during e evening songs were rendered by the Misses Mason and Whitmore and Mr. Angus. At the close Mr. Wilcockson yoiced the thanks of the different Guilds to the Pidgate Guild for their hospitality and entertainmeht.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>LAYNIMR</head>
        <p>LAYNIMR</p>
        <p>Death of Mrs. Molesworth.—We regret to an- nounce the death of Mrs. Molesworth, the wife of Mr. W. N. Molesworth, of Brookfield, Lymm, who passed away on' Wednesday morning. Being the descendant of a family of earnest Church of England workers, the deceased lady took an active interest in the local Parish Church.</p>
        <p>Cycling Accident.—As Mr. Thomas Hatton, butcher, of Booth's Hill, was cycling down Barr's Bank-road something went wrong with the back wheel, and as he neared the canal bridge he was thrown on his face to the ground. Mr. Hatton lay there until an employe of Mr. G. Dalton, of Eagle Brow, found him and had him transferred to his home. His face was severely cut and bruised.</p>
        <p>Horse v. tamp.—Whilst bringing milk to the station on Thursday morning, Mr. S. M. Hough- ton's milk float collided with a lamp post near to the Sion Chapel, Cherry-lane. It transpires that the horse took fright at a handcart which some workmen were wheeling up the lane, and swerving to one side dashed into the lamp, with disastrous results to the float. Fortunately the driver escaped injury, and the milk was trans- fnnned to Mr. Darbyshire's float and taken to the station.</p>
        <p>journed until Thursday. It is too late to say anything now..</p>
        <p>THE ADJOURNED MEETING.</p>
        <p>When the meeting was resumed on Thursday night the members immediately resolved into committee, and a ratepayer, after a two hours' wait, entered the room and made a protest. The members present were Messrs. Warbur- ton (chairman), Lockyer (vice-chairman), Groves, Gotobed, Dixon, Cowper, Holt ' and Critchley, and the Rev. G. B. Thurston, to- gether with the -Clerk and other officials. . . .</p>
        <p>For about two hours the Council were in cora- mittee discussing the estimates, and ultimately they announced that the district rate would be 2s. 10d., against 2s. Bd. last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>WEDDING OF MISS E. RATCLIFFE. A Brilliant Function at Lymna.</head>
        <p>WEDDING OF MISS E. RATCLIFFE. A Brilliant Function at Lymna.</p>
        <p>A pretty wedding was solemnised at the Lymm Congregational Church on Thursday, when the contracting parties were Mr. J. 11. Lovesey, son of Mr. and Mrs. J. 11. Lovesey, of Hale, and Miss 'Ellen Ratcliffe, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. John Ratcliffe, of Lymm. The bride, who was Charmingly attired in a Saxe blue costume, with black hat with ostrich feather, carried a bouquet of lilies and roses, and was given away by her father. The Misses Annie Ratcliffe, Jessie Ratcliffe, Hilda Ratcliffe, Annie Lovesey and Stevenson were in attendance with the bride. Mr. Wil- liam Fryer carried out the duties of best man. The Rev. Chris. H. Ratcliffe, of Banbury, cousin of the bride, was ;the officiating minister. Prior to the happy couple leaving for Black- pow by car, a reception was held at the home of the bride's parents, where a number of guests were entertained.</p>
        <p>The bridal pair were the.recipients of a large number Of handsome presents. The bride is well known in the district, and has for many years been connected with the local Congrega- tional Church and Sunday school.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>LIVELY COUNCIL MEETING. The Chairman Makes a Remarkable _ Speech.</head>
        <p>LIVELY COUNCIL MEETING. The Chairman Makes a Remarkable _ Speech.</p>
        <p>"KEPT OUT FOR TWO YEARS.". The air Was electrified in the Lymrri Council Office on Tuesday night, when the annual meet- ing of the Urban. District Council was held. The new Chairman (Councillor T. 11. Warbur- ton), after thanking the Council for his election, made a remarkable speech, in which he stated that his claim to the office had been ignored by certain member* of the Council. - The members present were: Councillors T. H. Warburton (the new chairman), John Hardy Holt, William G. Groves, Samuel Davies, Gotobed, F. Lockyer, T. F. W. Dixon, Charles Edward Cowper, W. Kenneth Kershaw, and Septimus Critchley, with Messrs. William Mul- lard (clerk and. collector), D. Mort . (surveyor), and W. L. Donaldson (gas manager). Mr. W. Mullard (clerk) formally occupied the chair until the election had been made, and as returning officer for the recent elections stated that they had resulted in the election of Messrs. Cowper,. Kershaw and Critchiey. Councillor Gotobed moved that Mr. Davies be re-elected chairman. He had held the office with acceptance and had been regular in his attend- ance.</p>
        <p>Councillor Dixon made a suggestion that they should. form some basis of superiority for the office. Councillor Kershaw seconded Councillor Davies's. nomination.</p>
        <p>Councillor Cowper moved as an amendment that Councillor Warburton be elected the chair- man.</p>
        <p>Councillor Lockyer, in seconding, said Coun- cillor Warburton had been a member for eleven years, and had occupied the vice-chair for four years, and it was only right and just that he should be elected. ([Tear, hear.) On a vote being taken, Councillor Warburton was elected by five votes to four.</p>
        <p>"WICKED CONSPIRACY." Councillor Warburton, in reply, said it was one of the pleasures of his life to take the chair. As most of them knew, by seniority he should have taken the chair two years ago, but by a wicked and infamous conspiracy which existed amongst the members he was ruled out, and 12 months ago the same thing occurred. It was not. sportsmanlike, it was not just, it was not right, but they could afford to wait. We have come to the front at last, he said, and I am much obliged to you for giving me this honour.</p>
        <p>There was a lot to be gone through during the year. Amongst the several items to be dealt with was the water question, which would be admitted was a rather serious one. When a poll was demanded in the village, members of the Council went round and held meetings "all around the place," and promised the rnte- payers that not one penny more would be , charged for their water. It would hit the vil- lagers hard if the rates went up, for the rents of the cottagers would be risen, a thing that had I already occurred... Threepence a week did not ' appear very much, but in twelve months it be- came a lot. They had the new sewage farm which had to be tackled, .and he thought the thing was being' tackled in a way not very creditable to them. There was too much money being wasted down there, and he would be care- ful how he used his pen. In the gas department the stokers were work- ing better now than ever. They had an eight- hour system, which was working economically all round, not only for the men, but for the Council. There had recently been an increase of wages in the department, and the only man who had not received one was the gas manager.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>ELECTION OF VICE-CHAIRMAN.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF VICE-CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>The Chairman then continued with the busi- ness, and stated that they would proceed to elect the vice-chairman, when</p>
        <p>Councillor Davies, evidently wanting to reply to the Chairman's remarks, interjected :As a member—</p>
        <p>He was not allowed to proceed, as he was ruled out of order, and was told he could make his comments later on.</p>
        <p>Immediately afterwards Councillor Davies Proposed Councillor Gotobed for the vice-chair- manship, saying that he had been a member for a long time and had been a good councillor. Councillor Holt seconded. Councillor Cowper proposed the nomination of Councillor Lockyer, Councillor Critchley sec- onding, and upon the vote being taken,'Council- lor Lockyer was elected by a majority of one. The method of voting was responsible for a protest by Councillor Holt, who pointed out that some of the members had not voted at all. Surely, he said, they had their own ideas, and they should vote upon them. It was absurd to abstain from voting. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>CHANGE OF 'COUNCIL NIGHT. The majority, of the members stated that the present night of meeting was inconvenient, and Monday: Tuesday and Thursday meetings were nut to tl,e vote. It was alti t 1 tl,at the meetings be held on the last Monday in the month at 7.30.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that the rate. made on May 29, 1913. realised £2,964 2s. 6d. The amount of .624 15s. 7d. was in arrears., A letter was read from the Rev. G. B. Thurston suggesting that the consideration of the estimates for the ensuinr, year should be left over until Thursday night', and a resolution was paased adjourning the meeting until Thurs- day evening. With that the Chairman de- clared the meeting closed. Councillor Davies: I would like to men- tion— The Chairman: The meeting has been de- clared closed.</p>
        <p>The Clerk: That 40 so. Councillor Davies: I think it is an opportune time to---</p>
        <p>The Chairman: The naeetirg has been ad.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>RATEPAYERS' PROTEST.</head>
        <p>RATEPAYERS' PROTEST.</p>
        <p>• At this stage a ratepayer entered the room and 'queried: Why have we, as ratepayers, been kept out of the meeting for two hours? I strongly protest against being kept out whilst servants of the Council are allowed in.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: You are a spectator; sit down.</p>
        <p>WHAT IS THE STATEMENT -</p>
        <p>The Chairman (to Mr. Gotobed) : You can make your statement now. Mr. Gotobed replied that he would do so at next Monday's meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>LYMM LADS' CLUB.</head>
        <p>LYMM LADS' CLUB.</p>
        <p>The Annual Gymnastic Display.</p>
        <p>It was a pity that there were not more people at the Drill,Hall on Wednesday night to wit- ness the foOrth annual gymnastic display of 'the Lvmm Lads' Club members, for the exhibition proved to be a marked advance to those given on previous occasions. The programme was opened with a series of marching exercises, after which the junior members gave a good performance on the paral- lel bars. The barbell item, contributed by the senior team, was excellent in the precision with which the various movements were carried out, whilst their next contribution, parallel bars, was also well executed, the solo work of the in- structor, Mr. S. Hinde, being heartily ap- plauded. The display also included exercises with, dumb-bells and on the horse by the juniors; Indian club swinging and vaulting on the horse bithe seniors, and concluded with the joint teams presenting a series of pyramids. Colonel J. E. G. Groves, in presenting the prizes, commented ion the excellent display, especially mentioning the efforts of the junior members, and said that it reflected very great credit upon their instructor, Mr. S. Hinde, and the leader, Mr. H. Barber. In addressing the members, Cblonel Groves asked them to show their appreciation of the committee's efforts •by striving to become the best-mannered -lads in the district. He said that many of the seniors were members of the local Territorials, and that he hoped to see the day when many of the juniors would also jdin their ranks. In putting forth a plea for mili- tarism,lie said that their forefathers had passed on to them a heritage which they should zeal- ously protect, and that it was only by being prepared for war that they could keep war away. He wished the club success not only in numbers but efficiency. .</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks was accorded Colonel Groves. on the motion of Jr. W. M. Beckett, seconded by Mr. J. Bell.</p>
        <p>•Pianoforte accompaniments and interludes were provided by Miss Hill, who was heartily thanked, on the proposition of Lieut. Hartley.</p>
        <p>, A PLEASURE TO TEACH. ' In response to the eulogistic remarks that had been made as to the training of the team, Mr. S. Hinde said that it was a pleasure to teach the members, and that they bore a very favourable comparison with midi teams as War- rington. He expressed, his appreciation of the kindly interest of Mr. J. G. Lister Farrar, the hon. secretary.</p>
        <p>Mr. Farrar, in giving a brief history of the club, said that though their membership showed a numerical decrease, the interest and efficiency of the present members was a. decided increase. The following is the prize list:— Club badges awarded: to J. Guest, G. Knott, J. Broadey, Druce, H. Moss, F. Moss, T. Kirk- patrick, G. Goffin, Jennions. H. Fogg, A. Sut- ton. jun., Dawson, H. Readett, R. Atherton. k. Goffin. Mostori, E. Brookman, Ratcliffe and E. Griffiths.</p>
        <p>Challenge cup and medal for best senior gym- nast, .T. Guest.</p>
        <p>Challenge medal for best junior gymnast, A. Sutton. jun. Bagatelle: 1, If. Readett: 2. S. Bromley. Shooting: 1, J. Washington; 2, A. Druce.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>PRESTON BROOK.</head>
        <p>PRESTON BROOK.</p>
        <p>GUARDIANS' ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>Members' Generous Help to the Blind.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, when Mr. C. E. Linaker, jun., was re-elected chairman and Mr. G. L. Jones vice- chairman. Mr. Linaker, in returning thanks, said it was gratifying to him to lind that he had done his duties to their satisfaction. ,</p>
        <p>THE LATE MRS. WHITLEY. Miss Whitley, of Stretton, wrote asking the Guardians to accept thanks for the kind letter of sympathy in their great sorrow. Mrs. Bower also wrote stating, "I was much touched with the kind letter of sympathy I re- ceived on behalf of the Guardians and yourself. My dear mother's death is a bitter grief to us all; and I know there are many who will rise up and call her blessed, for she was a succour of many. Please convey to the Guardians my grateful thanks for their kind sympathy and appreciation of my dear mother's work."</p>
        <p>MEDICAL 'RESIGNATIONS. Dr. Finlay McDougall, of Runcorn, wrote: Will you have the goodness to intimate to the Guardians at their next meeting my resigna- tion as parochial medical officer and public vac- cinator for the Runcorn district of the Runcorn Union, to take effect 'On the 30th day of June next?</p>
        <p>Dr. W. M. Brunton, of Runcorn, wrote :,For some years I have acted in the capacity of deputy for Dr. McDougall. Unfortunately, my health has broken down, so that I have been obliged to retire from medical practice, and ' have therefore to ask you to accept my resigna- tion. I may mention that Dr. Roswell has suc- ceeded to my practice, which he is carrying on at the same address. The resignations were accepted with regret. and on the proposition of Mr. Gibbs, Dr. McDougall's 26 years' service was placed on record.</p>
        <p>HELP FOR THE BLIND. The Treasurer of the National Institution for the Blind wrote asking the Guardians to follow the example of the Hampton Urban District Council, at which, upon the suggestion of one of the members, a handsome eollection was made on behalf of the institution ind forwarded to the Mansion House Fund. The Board decided to "send the hat round," with the result that ...C3 Cs. 41d. was collected.</p>
        <p>WAY TICKETS FOR TRAMPS. The Executive Committee appointed at the Liverpool Conference intimated that they had acceded to submit a scheme for the' establish- ment ..of the way ticket system in the inter. county' area of Lancashire, Cheshire, 'Cumber- land and Westmorland, notwithstanding the fact that several Boards of Guardian's have not yet eicpressed their desire to join in the pro- posed combination.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>VACANT SEAT.</head>
        <p>A VACANT SEAT.</p>
        <p>Arising out of ilie..electron—Of the Finance Committee, it was moved that Mr. J. W. Tem- perley be invited to give his reasons for not attending the meetings, but an amendment was carried declaring the seat vacant,</p>
        <p>Mrs. C. E. Linaker, of Sutton, and Mr. Kidd, of Frodsham, were thanked for gifts to the kuget</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
