        <p>—with the possible exception of the Pi Army, of which I know nothing at as for the delusion that the officers of the', Army belong to a rich and aristocratic talistic class, well, I can only say this, tbs,,' convinced the ordinary British officer 13°.,', less than the ...€4OO a year which the member of Parliament considers to be lii quisite, and represents perhaps less work. •</p>
        <p>If you are going to attempt to democrati Army, you must at once adopt a different .53, namely, the system of universal service. hear.) YQU adopt that system, then i ever/ n the country passes through the ranks, may fairly say that the man who serve; private has in him just as much the caPal, become an officer as the son of a =blew-a': passes into the Army in the ordinary present circumstances. Until you are Pr, to talk of doing away with the voluntarY.: anY• talk of democratising the Army ''' waste of breath.</p>
        <p>Liberal Club "Army." Apparently the view of the Labour an*, cal politician is that the British soldier d'r take the oath to fight against the King's el alone, 'but takes the oath of allegiance National Liberal Club and the Ancient On Hibernians—(langhter and applause)—a"ciii remarkable circumstance that the peoPleV, country who are supposed to be the 'e advocates of peace are the most deteriail.lo vocates of using force in Ulster, and 0.1 British Army for the purpose of coercniti shooting down people whose one desire 15 under the British flag. (Hear, hear.) A 5 I can see, they look upon the British Ara mere sort of tool to be used for carrying °I Parliament Act. 4.1</p>
        <p>I wonder what the attitude of these gen° would have been if, for instance, the CO tive party was to suggest that the ArmY be employ,A, let us say, against the anti•l ators—(laughter)—or forcing consCientiotls' tors to sell their teapots, or toast-racks, or. ever portions of their furniture they . sacrifice on the altar of their consle (Laughter.) Mr. Asquith, we arc told, is more than san—a statesman. Well, if he is a statesul,i</p>
        <p>him show it by abandoning the ;Ole partisan. Let him take his courage 11 hands, and let him consult the voters country. As we have said, we are prell abide by the result.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>RUNCORN SENSATIO</head>
        <p>THE RUNCORN SENSATIO</p>
        <p>"It is all through this married man," remark attributed to James Simpson, 14. on Thursday, brought .up at Runcorn, c with ',.he attempted murder of his wile' 'Superintendent Owen said it appear°, early on Wednesday morning the prisollo` his wife commenced quarrelling in 11)111 and whilst the wife was in the back kW': bit prisoner obtained a razor and put —t around her neck, inflicting wounds on their one measuring about three inches long an inch deep. . _ i</p>
        <p>- be I! Constable Tudor,• who happened to ee neighbourhood, found the prisoner's on a couch with a cloth held to her l'acc from what was said he apprehended the ,rif Upon bringing him into custody the Pci said, It is all through a bother." • charged 112 said, " It was all through ried man!' •</p>
        <p>The constable said that the woroao ered with bine&amp; and when he got to t'lti Station he found on the prisoner a blotw" razor. 440 On the .solicitors' table there were 5 white cloths exPosed, covered in blootls`l')3 a black-handled razor. The prisoner appeared to be very 05 was a white bandage around his throst,r, fingers on the left hand were wrapPeciti;;if When asked-if he had anything to 6'l' a remand, he just nodded his head. The prisoner was remanded unto May Ist. • a From inquiries in the neighbourhoooo learned that Simpson had been ill for :10111 and hkid been in the Workhouse 110,1).1 three months ago. Since that time 0' • been able to follow 1.2 y e-,i;loyZao't.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY .AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY .AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>.</p>
