        <p>Ciontinued in next column,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>TABLE TALK.</head>
        <p>TABLE TALK.</p>
        <p>The World's Tribute of Interesting</p>
        <p>Items of News.</p>
        <p>QUAINT AND AMUSING.</p>
        <p>Sleeping in the moonlight in the tropics is at- tended, so it is said, with danger. There is an affliction called "moon blinks," which has been traced to the reflected light of the moon, caused by polarisation of reflected light. This has no connection, with the one-time popular supersti- tion that sleeping in the moonlight caused in- sanity, whence came the name "lunacy.' One scientific publication concludes that the bad effect observed in fish exposed to the rays of the moon may produce sickness in humans.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>Richest Gold Field.</head>
        <p>Richest Gold Field.</p>
        <p>Unless a long list of most experienced engi- neers have committed an error of judgment the Gastineau district of Alaska is destined to be- come, during the next five years, the most im- portant gold-mining district of America. Around Juneau there is now being developed a deposit of gold ore, which, in point of tonnage, prob- ably exceeds anything else that is known in the world, except perhaps the Rand. If estimates be borne out, we shall have a new production of £3,600,000 per annum. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>Carp to Purify our Water.</head>
        <p>Carp to Purify our Water.</p>
        <p>At Munich they have been experimenting with carp as purifiers of water supply, says "Science Siftings." These fish eat many microbes and grow fat on impurities. Dr. Hofer demonstrates that carp thrive in polluted water. Fish'weigh- ing one pound were placed, in August, .in the ponds into which the sewers of the city pour, and in November they had grown to three pounds' weight. Dr. Hofer asserts that carp so fed may be eaten safely, for they do not eat the poisons, but the microbes that produce these poisons; while cooking guarantees their flesh against any possibility of carrying infection.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>Parisian Remedy for Drunkenness. _ . . .</head>
        <p>Parisian Remedy for Drunkenness. _ . . .</p>
        <p>A cure for confirmed drunkards by means of an anti-alcoholic serum called antiethyline, taken from horses, was discovered in 1900, and has been submitted to scientific tests in Paris during the past twelve years, says "Science Sift- ings." More than 250 drunkards of both sexes and ages, varying from 09 enty-six to fifty years, and of different nationalities, have been treated and medical reports just made show a result of 83 per cent. completely cured. Simultaneous observations of drunkards treated by otrdinary methods in special asylums show only 30 per cent. cured.</p>
        <p>Napoleon's Horse's Disappearing Tail. Napoleon's famous charger Vizier, having worn out its third tail since his master's death, has just been supplied with a fourth flowing appendage for the benefit of next summer's visitors to Paris. Vizier, except for his tail, is the "authentic" war horse ridden by Napoleon. After the animal's death the skin was stuffed and placed among the Napoleonic collection at the Invalides Museum. Of the three tails that have already disappeared at the hands of sight- seers, despite the vigilance of the guardians, it is believed that at least two are in America in small detachments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>What Mail Boxes Indicate.</head>
        <p>What Mail Boxes Indicate.</p>
        <p>A German statistician has figurei:l;it that the number of collection boxes used by the postal authorities of each nation may serve as an indi- cation of commercial activity. According to his information the nations rank commercially as follows :—Germany, with 155,766 boxes for col- lection of mail; the United States, with 144,640; Prance, 79,724; British India, 75,083; United Kingdom, 71,986; Japan, 67,694; Austria-Hun- gary, 59,503; Italy, 39,767; Russia, 31,714. Other countries range from Switzerland with 13,472, to Persia with 17, and Abyssinia with only G. Edible Cactus. Experiments have been in progress in America with the object of producing a variety of spine- less cactus which will be suitable for eating. A certain measure of success, we gather from the "Chief Steward," has already been attained, and for some time past consignments of edible spineless cactus have been shipped from Mexico to Boston and New York, at which ports they have arrived in excellent condition. The fruit lis unique in form and colour, exceedingly hand- some. unusually wholesome (the large amount of vegetable salts it contains being regarded as very beneficial), and far superior to the banana in flavour. It is usually sold at the same price I Per box as oranges, and can be produced at less I than one-tenth the expense of growing apples, I oranges, apricots, grapes, plums or peaches.</p>
        <p>Steel Railway Carriages. Steel is not only displacing timber in rail- way carriage construction, but also for freight vehicles of various classes. In order to obtain rigidity and strength it is usual to ridge or cor- rugate the plates in various ways, but a new method of construction is finding favour in some quarters, says the "Railway News." Particu- lars are available of a freight car in which the entire end is formed of a single sheet of steel pressed with concentric annular corrugations, no posts, braces, or rivets being required. The plate is attached to the car body by means of bolts or rivets engaging the corner angles, and it is claimed that the design releases additional space inside, adding about a foot to the length available.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>Stalked by Fifteen Lions.</head>
        <p>Stalked by Fifteen Lions.</p>
        <p>A Central News Message from Cape Town gives details of the terrible fate which befel a Greek trader named Sataras while on a journey from Barotse. Accompanied only by a few natives, Sataras was travelling in charge of some cattle, and one night when they were in camp, about forty miles from Sesheke, fifteen lions and lionesses appeared out of the scrub and carried off one of the oxen. The natives asked Sataras to remain in camp, but he was anxious to over- take another herd of cattle, which was travelling in charge of a man named Laurie Kerr. All day the lions stalked the cattle, and, darkness over- taking the party, Sataras fell a victim to them. The terrified natives ran on to Kerr's camp, but when he returned nothing but a few bones re- mained to tell of the tragedy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>Tobacco and Hiccough.</head>
        <p>Tobacco and Hiccough.</p>
        <p>- - - In a Russian medical journal, Dr. G. Tate- vosoff draws attention to the excellent service which may be obtained from the ordinary snuff tobacco as a means for cutting short hiccough. Tle relaers an instructive case of a patient with some chronic chest disease, accompanied by vio- lent cough attacks in whom the latter used to be followed by extremely 'obstinate hiccoughs. The common remedies (including cocaine) failing to exercise any controlling influence on the most distressing symptoms, Dr. Tatevosoff at last de- cided to give a trial to the said old-fashioned pOpUlar means, making the patient on each occa- sion thoroughly snuff into his nose a pinchful of the powder until the appearance of lively sneez- ing. From the first treatment the effect was truly brilliant, the hiccough subsiding' as if by magic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>_ JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST —continued.</head>
        <p>_ JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST —continued.</p>
        <p>all are derived from Adam. As was noted by a critic when Mr. Weekly's book was issued, Run- ciman is the than in charge of rouncies or nags. The Chancellor of the Exchequer has a philo- logical affinity with the Foreign Secretary, for his Lloyd is the Welsh for Grey, the name as oopplied to their respective ancestors being no doubt descriptive. Asquith is a local appella- tive, probably equivalent to Ashford, and Pank- hurst a corruption of Pentecost. I have myself been puzzled not a little as to the origin of some others, but gradually discovered that Many of them were place-names.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 25, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 25, 1914.</p>
        <p>THE WORLD OF LETTERS.</p>
        <p>New Books.</p>
        <p>The Pride of the Fancy. By George Edgar. London: Mills and Boon, Ltd.</p>
        <p>This book has a certain amount of local in- terest, inasmuch as the author belongs to a Warrington family, and the opening chapters deal with Newton Races and the immediate vicinity, including Winwick and Warrington. The story, however, deals mostly with life in London. As its name indicates it is chiefly con- cerned with the 'noble art of boxing or prize- fighting. The gentle reader need not turn from it with scorn on this account. Who will forget George Borrow's descriptions of his hero's pugi- listic efforts? And of living writers, both Dr. Conan Doyle and that popular novelist, Geof- frey Farnol, have glorified the profession. In the pre-Victorian era prize fighting was the fashion. Public opinion changed and it sank into disrepute. It seems as if it were again returning to favour, if one may judge by the way it is written about in the daily Press, and the interest which is excited by the boxing con- tests which take place from time to time. It is not necessary to discuss whether this is desir- able or otherwise. The professional fighting man is supposed to be rather a brutal and ignoble creature, but no doubt there are among them to-day, as there were in the past, men of fine character and clean life.. The story, however, deals with other matters than those which concern "the fancy." We see a good deal of the shady side of life in the Metropolis, and are taken to places which,. though the names are disguised, anyone who knows London will easily recognise. The reader may be disposed to think that the vile- ness of some of the characters has been over- drawn, but we do not think this is so.</p>
        <p>........ —„ The author, with an unsparing hand, has re- vealed the wickedness which exists in certain quarters. And he has also shown us how much that is loyal and noble may be found where it is least expected. The story is somewhat melo- dramatic, but it is thoroughly interesting. Mr. Edgar has considerable descriptive power, and he uses it well. He writes of many things that belong to Bohemia, and are outside ordinary everyday life, but the tone of his writing is such that it will give nothing but wholesome pleasure to his readers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>My Lady of the Chimney Corner. By Alexander Irvine. London: Eveleigh Nash. 3s. 6d.</head>
        <p>My Lady of the Chimney Corner. By Alexander Irvine. London: Eveleigh Nash. 3s. 6d.</p>
        <p>"My Lady of the Chimney Corner," by Alex- ander Irvine, is a delightful book. It reminds one very much of I3,arrie's "Margaret Ogilvy," that wonderful tribute to his wonderful Scotch mother. Presumably the chief character in the book, Anna, is a picture of Irvine's Irish mother. No doubt a certain amount of literary licence has been used in the portrayal and in the surroundings. The book is full of pathos, but is brightened with mirth. There is that which dims the eye, but also much that makes the reader glad. On the page on the frontis- piece we read the following : " This book is the torn manuscript of the , most beautiful life I ever knew. I have merely pieced and patched it together, and have not ' even changed or disguised the names of the little group of neighbours who lived with us at 'the bottom of the world.' " "The bottom of the world" is in Ireland. Ireland is not the bottom of the world, but the circumstances of the life of a very poor Irish family are so considered. The father, Jamie, is a shoemaker, not a master but a helper, who takes work home. At the door of this family poverty always stands; work and wages do not often get ahead of hunger. But in this home , there is something, bigger than poverty, yes and bigger than religion, and that is love. As Anna herself says: " Love is bigger than religion. Two things are sure One is love of God. He loves all His children and gets huffed at none, The other is that the love we have for each other is of the same warP and woof as His for us, and love is enough, Jamie." This is the keynote struck at the beginning of the family history, and it is the note that Anna sounds on her deathbed, for this is the message she leaves with her husband for the children: "Tell th' childther there's only wan kind ov poverty, Jamie, an' that's t' haave no love in th' heart," says she. Judged by this standard this family was one of the wealthiest in Ireland, and Anna was its queen. Here we have a description of the home in whieh most of the years of the family life were spent "The stone cabin was thatch covered, and measured about twelve by sixteen feet. The space comprised three apartments. One, a bed- room; over the bedroom and beneath the thatch a little loft that served as a bedroom to those of climbing age. The rest of it was Workshop, dining-room, sitting-room , parlour, and general community news' °entre. The old folk slept in a bed, the 'rest of us slept on the floor and be- neath the thatch. Between the bedroom door and the open fireplace was thetchimney corner. Near the door stood an old pine table and some dressers. They stood against the wall and were filled with crockery. We never owned a chair. There were several pine stools, a few creepies (small stools), and a long bench that ran along the bedroom wall, from the .chimney iNrner to the bedroom door. The mud floor never had the luxury of a covering, nor did a picture ever adorn the hare walls. When the floor needed patching, Jamie went to somebody's garden, brought a shovelful-of earth, mixed it, and filled the holes. The stools and creepies were scrubbed once a week, the table once a day. I could draw an outline of that old table now, and accurately mark every dent and crack in it. Ido not know where it came from, but each of us had a hope that one day we should possess ' pig. We built around the hope a sty, and placed it against the end of the cabin. The pig never turned up, but the hope lived there throughout a generation !" The following is a gem, and there are few such in literature to match it. It arose out of the little lad being forbidden by his mother to ex- hibit in a local flower show a plant that had been lent to him, and he was bitterly disap- pointed:—</p>
        <p>"I sat down beside her and laid my head on her lap. She drew me up close to her and told me a story. 'Once, a long, long time ago, God, fellin' tired, went to sleep an' had a nice wee nap on His throne:- His head was in His han's, an' a wee white cloud came down an' covered Him up. Purty soon Ho wakes up an' 'says He, "Where's Michael?" ' " Here 1 am, Father!' said Michael. "'Michael, me boy,' says God, I want a chariot and a charioteer!' "'Right ye are!' says he.. Up comes the purtiest chariot in the city of heaven an' finest charioteer. " Me boy,' says God, take a million tons ov th' choicest seeds of th' flowers of heaven an' take a trip around th' world wi' them. Scatter them,' says He, by th' roadsides an' th' wild placed of th' earth where My poor live.' "'Aye,' says the charioteer, 'that's jist like ye, Father. It's th' purtiest job of m' afther- life, an' I'll do it finely.' "'lt's jist come t' Me in a dream,' says th' Father, that th' rich have all the flowers down there, and th' poor haave nown at all. If a million tons isn't enough take a •billion tons!' " " At this point I' got in some questions about God's language and the kind of flowers. -</p>
        <p>" Well, dear,' she said. He snakes Irish t' Irish people, and the charioteer was an Irish; man.'</p>
        <p>" ' May he it was a woman 1' I ventured. "'Aye, but there's no difference up there.' " Th' flowers,' she paid, 'were primroses, but- thereups, an' daisies, an' th' flowers that be handy, t' th' poor, an' from that day to this there's been flowers a-plenty for all of lig every- where!' "</p>
        <p>And so the hoy is made to gather wild flowers;</p>
        <p>grown. and these are made into a wonderful bouquet, Ila,iii.diteirtm7nion: up, thus tells of a talk he a priz.e. ,' ' • Towards the end of the book the son and y had with his old father after Anna's death :-•-• "Every day we took long walks along thes country roads. These walks were full of ques- tionings. Big vital questions of life and death an,d, Tihmermevt a aliltoyt. off were about another quaintl put." world, bhoy.. On 3-er oath now, d'ye think there is wail?"</p>
        <p>"I do." "If there is wud Ire keep rne frum Anna jist beeause I've been kinda rough?" "I am sure He wouldn't." "He wudn't be s' d—d niggardly, wad He?" "Never! God is love, and love doesn't work that way!"</p>
        <p>At the railway station he was still pouring in his questions "D'ye believe in Prayer?" , "Aye."</p>
        <p>" Well, jist ax sometimes that Anna an' me be together, will ye?"</p>
        <p>"Aye."</p>
        <p>The central stores of the llebden Bridge In- dustrial Society were broken into in the early hours of Saturday morning and jewellery and cash were stolen to the extent of about £5O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <p>Continued in rext column</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>Literary Gleanings.</head>
        <p>Literary Gleanings.</p>
        <p>BOLIVIA.</p>
        <p>Bolivia is an inland country, until lately very difficult to reach from outside. By the loss of Mollendo, on the Pacific Coast, to Peru, and of Acre, on the upper water of the Puriis, to Brazil, no direct access to the oceans was left but the apparently open door of the rivers Madre de Dios, Beni and Mamore, leading to the Madeira, the Amazon, and thereby to the Atlantic. Ocean steamers can sail up the rivers to San Antonio, which is not far from the frontiers of Bolivia, but from there a long series of cataracts begins, and the surrounding country, through which the river Madeira flows, is hidden in dense jungle. The open door was not really open, therefore, and owing to the natural obstacles, the magnificent possibilities 'of Bolivia have been greatly hindered in their development. An idea of the risks to be en- countered by the daring navigators who attempt to shoot the rapids of the Madeira in charge of merchandiseo that almost fifty per cent, is lost every year. F efforts have been made to r along ng . maytim ebe gained when it is known skirt the rapids by means of a railway. Two unsuccessful attempts were made, but at last the principal portion gr really portion has been completed, and w Hy accessible to the outside world. will become easy, and great developments in mining, agriculture, and trade in general are certain to follow. The now Bolivia is Madeira-Msmore railroad is considered to be a remarkable achievement in tropical engineering. Another outlet, tha railway from La Paz to Arica, the Chilean port on the Pacific, has just beenpart of the indemnityl lsoss of its sea-coast. It long. AEI soon as Bolivia idsu e t three fio n hundred e d Bolivia b y f o Cr milesl l he e a has completed its internal main lines of railroad its future as a united, compact, and powerful country is absolutely asgured, for it has great natural advantages in extraordinary variety. Railroad building, as may easily be understood from a study of the nature of the country—high mountains and plateaus, and tropical plains of dense jungle—has been very slow. In 1892 Presi- dent Arce formally opened the first railroad, and w s t huecit.ei o aren, d ln 1,100 planned.—By C. J. Ryan ,500 miles in operation, 280 under in "The Theosophical Path." construction, a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>.CISTERCIAN MONKS.</head>
        <p>THE .CISTERCIAN MONKS.</p>
        <p>_ In regard to the ordinary apparel of the monks the same simplicity was observed. The material was the coarse cloth in common use among the peasants. The dress of the Cistercians, it may be observed, consisted of a long white cassock, with black scapulary and hood, and black wool- len girdle—the scapulary being a strip of serge or stuff through the middle of which the head was passed, and it fell, front and back, over the shoulders. Outside the bounds of the monastery the monk wore over this in addition a black cowl or cucullus. Inside the monastery, how- ever, a cowl was not worn except in choir, in processions, and on all specially important occa- sions, and at such times the cowl and hood were white and not black. This predominance of white in the garments of the Cistercians earned for them the name of the White Monks, in contradistinction to the older Benedictines whose habit was black. The monks received their clothes from the abbot, each monk beinr required to keep his own clothes in repair. The Cistercians wore shoes, not sandals. In the matter of food, also, these Cistercians lived a simple life. During half of the year they had only one meal a day, and not more than two cooked dishes were provided at this single meal, exception in this particular being made in the case of the sick, the aged, and some others. At first the Cistercians were strict vege- tarians. Not until 1335 was there any relaxation of this rule, when Pope Benedict XII. granted a limited measure of indulgence. Other indul- gencies followed, with the result that by the middle of the fifteenth century flesh meat was allowed the monks three days a week, on Sunday, Tuesday and Thursday, except during certain of the Church seasons. In further illustration of the severe discipline to which the monks were subjected, it may be stated that silence was strictly enforced amongst the members of the order. Only certain times and certain places were recognised when and where speech might take place, and any infrac tion of this rule exposed the delinquent to the discipline of the chapter.—Prqm "The Abbey of Dundrennan," by the Rev. Alex. H. Christie, B.D.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>SOPHISTICATED EAST.</head>
        <p>THE SOPHISTICATED EAST.</p>
        <p>In "The Land of the Lotus" J. .111. Graham tells of life in India, and gives impressions gathered in the course of a journey through that country. .</p>
        <p>"There is a wonderful and beautiful fairy, tale of a,land where one is waited on hand and foot and rendered for ever helpless and unfit for English life—that land is called India." As a lover of slums, I was greatly disap- pointed in my visit to Calcutta. The city was too clean and civilised to please me—even so' early in the season. Later on, I believe, froek_l coats and top hats mar the picture with their proud pretentiousness. We were strongly ad- vised not to attempt any excursions into the native quarters at that time, so that ray view of the city was a necessity one-sided, and my acquisition of samples of native handiwork at a standstill. "Calcutta is built on the banks of the Hooghly, :which is part and iparcel of the Ganges, and when we had nothing else to do, large r andgo steamers going. Up and moved which was often, we used to walk along the banks and watch the channel traffic. Despite the dangerous shifting sands, there seemed to 1d) eo na;grtehaet deal e to. f roadco m n pompously on their way, tugs tugged, and little steam launches darted like importunate mosquitoes from point to point. Then the sunset and the water took on a sudden hue of gold with an undertone of rich claret, a feast for the eye; it cast a glamour over the earth and a spell over the senses; and lo! while we looked and wondered it was gone. The passing of another day was accomplished, and night fell •upon us, blotting out all save here and there a light, while up the channel passed a phantom barque, its outlines blurred save where the two green lights burned, steady and unblinking signals in the darkness." •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <p>NEW BOOKS—continued.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>0 n CHILDREN'S CORNER. Conducted by UNCLE JACK.</head>
        <p>0 n CHILDREN'S CORNER. Conducted by UNCLE JACK.</p>
        <p>AFTER THE HOLIDAYS.</p>
        <p>My Dear Children,— Back at school again! I hope all of you had a good holiday, and I suppose you are already counting the days until Whitsuntide. I know you are all busy each night—playing out. There- fore I will not trouble you with a long letter, but will close by asking you to read the matter below, of which I think I have got a good selec- tion. What about the tale?</p>
        <p>Your loving</p>
        <p>UNCLE JACK.</p>
        <p>A GAME OF VOWELS.</p>
        <p>A member of the party ashs a question of 'his right-hand neighbour. The answer given should be brief, suitable and prompt, but must not include the vowel interdicted by the questioner. For instance:—</p>
        <p>George: "Do you like skating, Harry? Answer without an E."</p>
        <p>harry: "I was always fond of it, and I am now." •</p>
        <p>Harry : "With whom did you go to church yesterday, Fred? Answer without an A." Fred: "With my mother's sister," answers Fred, cautiously, knowing that if he said "aunt" he would have to pay forfeit.</p>
        <p>Tito game continues until a number of forfeits are collected.</p>
        <p>THE ROBIN'S SONG.</p>
        <p>Came a robin-redbreast</p>
        <p>Hopping to my door; He was quite a stranger, We'd not met before; "Pleas'm, half a crumb," said he. But, as plain as plain could be— " Pleas'm, half a crumb," said he. "Mrs. Robin-Redbreast,</p>
        <p>She is frozen numb; But, if you can spare us Only half a crumb, I will sing a song," said he, "high up in the leafless tree."</p>
        <p>Soon the robin-redbreast Spreads his little wings; And, up in the tree-top, "Thank you mum," he sings; "You have been so kind to me, I shall come again, you'll see." —II. J. Davarey, in "The Little Animals' Friend."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>SAVED BY A LAUGH.</head>
        <p>SAVED BY A LAUGH.</p>
        <p>"How about an Indian story, colonel?" asked one of the Boy Scouts. "A story about some fight that you saw with your own eyes?" The colonel reflected as he rubbed his stiff knee—the knee-cap that was smashed by a ball from the rifle of a swarthy brave. "Very well," he said. "I'll tell you about Olaf and Jens, two young Swedes we had with us when we went after some Indians that were on a rampage in Arizona. Olaf and Jens were good fellows, but Indian fighting was something new to them.</p>
        <p>"How they got into the army I declare I don't know. They were always too far ahead, or too far behind, or too far to one side; and at first we had almost to hide them when we met Indians, for they didn't know enough to come in out of the bullets.</p>
        <p>" We were in pursuit of the Indians, who were retreating through a very wild and dangerous region. We had marched steadily since sun- rise, and we were dusty, dry, hungry, tired and cross. Moreover, we had good reason to suspect that Indians, and plenty of them, were near by. " We approached a broad, shallow arroyo that was full of scrub trees, bushes, cacti and huge boulders. Our scouts, deploying cautiously, were just entering the fringe of low thickets on the edge of the arroyo, when a volley from behind the boulders and chaparral killed them to a man. The enemy bad tricked us in spite of our watchfulness. They were on exactly their own kind of battle-ground. We knew What sort of a murderous job it would be to oust them from those boulders and brush.</p>
        <p>_ "We simply had to get into the arroyo, where We could hide ourselves and fight the Indians in their own style, so the men scattered, and, yell- ing like the Indians themselves, ran at top speed for the arroyo. More were hit on that short run than we could afford to lose.</p>
        <p>"We made another dash, a short one, diagon- ally across the arroyo, and flung ourselves behind a low bank. Crawling and running on our hands and feet, we gradually made a flank move- ment, until at last we-reached the rocks and had a fighting chance. Our loss was heavy by that time, and, so far as we knew, not a single Indian had been killed.</p>
        <p>"After about two hours of peep-and-shOot- quick fighting, night began to draw on. The Indians got bolder, too, and began to close in on US. If we showed an inch of a hat, it was snipped off. _ . .</p>
        <p>" Just then a big Missourian named Bill Humble suddenly bawled out: '"Look at the Swedes, will you? They've gone crazy at last!" "All the men stopped firing to look. We saw Olaf and Jens Waging a hitherto unknown kind of Indian warfare."</p>
        <p>"What were they doing?" asked one of the younger Scouts, a bit impatiently. The colonel smiled. "Olaf and Jens were hid- ing behind a big boulder, and they were shoot- ing straight up in the air as fast as they could -load and fire. _</p>
        <p>"We Watched them, so amazed, that we almo§t forgot about the enemy. Bill Humble shouted at them: . _</p>
        <p>" 'Here! What are you two doing?' "Jens blazed away at the zenith, and then asked: "'Vat ban matter?'</p>
        <p>" 'Why are'you crazy men shootin' up in the sky?' Bill yelled back. ' Vell,' yelled back Jens,''e can't see dem " Indian fallars, and ve can't shoot drough de rocks, so ye shoot up in de air so de bullets fall on dem.' "A shout of deep-throated laughter burst from the men who heard him, and the others took it up as the word passed along the line. In a few minutes every one was laughing. Why, a man who had a bullet in his back grinned in spite of the pain. " The way that laughter took the Indians was as strange as it was unexpected. They were sus- picious, and began to peep furtively round and over the boulders. One of them, a big, hawk- beaked savage, exposed himself too much, and a soldier left off laughing long enough to shoot _ _</p>
        <p>Ire happened to be. the chief, and his braves, probably thinking that .his end was 'caused in some way by the paleface-laughter, broke from their hiding-places and were soon going hotfoot down the crooked gulch." The colonel paused, and the Scout who had proposed the story asked:- " Was that all?"</p>
        <p>"Well." the colonel answered, "it was-1- about all. It was the 'last fight of any conse- quence. I don't. suppose that the official records mention it, but Olaf and Jens really ended that war."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>3 WARRINGTON ci;k).'' , DOAN'S</head>
        <p>3 WARRINGTON ci;k).'' , DOAN'S</p>
        <p>Backache Kidney Pills</p>
        <p>Colds Affect the Kidneys.</p>
        <p>On January Bth, 1913, Mr. P. Cross, of 6, Wilkinson-street, Marshhouse-lane, Warr- ington, said: " At my work at the forge I am subject to catching cold, and this has affected my kidneys. About four years ago I was off work for a week with terrible pains in the back. They were sharp, cutting pains, and caught me when I stooped, and my back seemed to be locked, so that I could hardly straighten. The first thing in the morning the pains were worst, and I found it hard to get out of bed.</p>
        <p>"I could tell it was the kidneys because the water was disordered.</p>
        <p>"I happened to read about Doan's Back- ache Kidney Pills and thought I would try them. They proved to be just the medicine I required. After taking the first box I was able to return to my work, and before long all signs of the complaint had gone. I have used Doan's Pills occasionally since, and they always do me good.</p>
        <p>(Signed) "P. CROSS."</p>
        <p>On November 22nd, 1913—NEARLY TWELVE MONTHS LATER-Mr. Cross said: "My cure by Doan's Pills has been a lasting one. I still keep very well indeed, ' and am constantly recommending Doan's Pills to others. I have not lost a day's work through illness since I took Doan's Pills."</p>
        <p>w</p>
        <p>back and loins were fearful. I could not stoop, and in order to get out of bed I had to roll out. All the strength seemed to go out of my back, and at last I had to leave work.</p>
        <p>"I was at. home for a week, and my back was terribly stiff and sore. Every time I moved the pains attacked me.</p>
        <p>"At last I made up my mind to try Doan's Backache Kidney Pills, and I was soon thankful to find that they were doing me good. After taking two boxes I was quite cured. The pills cleared away a lot of gravel, and the kidney excretions were put in good order again. I shall always be grateful for the good I derived from Doan's Pills, and you can rely upon my recommending them to others.</p>
        <p>The above cases speak for themselves, and need no comment. If the reader has any signs of kidney trouble, like weak or lame back, Irregularity of the kidney's action, rheumatism, dropsy, gravel, stone, or any urinary trouble, we will be pleased to send, free, a busk dealing with this subject, and with Long Life Laws in general. (See address below.)</p>
        <p>(Signed) "T. FELLOWS."</p>
