        <p>tended for use where platinum effects are re- quired, but where the cost of platinum paper itself is prohibitive. The booklet issued by the company states that the new paper is prepared with platinum and silver salts, and that the finished image contains both silver and platinum. " Although it is possible to remove the silver in this image by chemical means, the unaltered platinum will remain in sufficient quantity to represent the shadows and all the detail in the original picture. Hence the prints on this paper are permanent in the sense that all the detail is preserved." • • The manipulation of the new paper is simple enough. It is printed in the same way as platinum paper, but is less susceptible to damp; so much so that the company advise us that in smaller Sizes it will be sent out in packets and not in tins. The printing is very rapid: We have not made direct comparison, but it seemed to us to be distinctly more sensitive than platinotype paper, the change in the appearance of the surface, which in the unexposed condi- tion is yellow, being also similar. A wide-mouthed jar makes a convenient re- ceptacle for hypo solution. The prescribed quan- tity of water may be put into the jar and the hypo tied up in a piece of muslin and hung from a stick laid across the top, so that the chemical is just below the surface of the water. In this position it dissolves very quickly—sur- prisingly quickly, in fact, to those who have not tried the method before. •</p>
