      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>AMATEUR PHOTOGRAPHY.</head>
        <p>AMATEUR PHOTOGRAPHY.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting of the Warrington</p>
        <p>Society.</p>
        <p>BY SILVER-STREET.</p>
        <p>Again at the annual meeting of the War- rington Photographic Society, held on Tuesday evening, the Secretary and Treasurer were able to report favourably on the year's work, although the treasurer had to regret a small decline both in membership and in funds. The President, Mr. A. Davis, occupying the chair, congratulated the society not only on the con- tinued success in numbers, but on the brilliant programme just concluded. The lectures dur- ing the past six months included some of the highest names in the present photographic world, and everything points to an equally in- teresting summer session. Few changes re- quired to be made in the council, the chief being Mr. Osborne and Mr. S. Massey, who were elected delegato to the L. and C. P.U.; Miss Jackson, portfolio secretary; and Mr. W. R. Jackson, Mr. Sykes and Mr. Minards, lantern- ists and curators.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>INVENTOR OF THE PHOTOGRAPHIC</head>
        <p>THE INVENTOR OF THE PHOTOGRAPHIC</p>
        <p>FILM.</p>
        <p>Some very interesting facts in history can he obtained from time to time by examining the reports of law cases. Here is a series referring to the man who first discovered celluloid as a support for rollable films. The extract is from the "British Journal of Photography ": A settlement has been reached which "ends the long legal fight between the Eastman Kodak Company and the Ansco Company over the 'Goodwin patent' of the pliable film. It is announced that the Eastman Company, which it was alleged used the invention, agrees to pay 'substantial' sum to the Ansco Company, which controls the patent; the figure is not stated, but it is generally supposed to amount to millions of dollars." Hannibal Goodwin was a clergyman residing at Newark, New Jersey, for thirty years prior to his death, which occurred December 31, 1900. His salary was small, and for ten years prior to his death he had no regular charge. Ile was interested in chemistry, and spent much of his spare time in chemical experimentation and re- search. The necessity for a transparent, sen- sitive pellicle for use in roller cameras had long been felt, and Goodwin, though hampered by his inadequate surroundings, undertook the task of supplying it. At the time of Goodwin's invention the art was vehemently demanding, as a substitute for the glass plates then in use, a transparent photographic film capable of supporting the sen- sitive emulsion necessary in photography, and also capable of being rolled up. Goodwin en- tered the field as an inventor, for the sole pur- pose of overcoming the difficulties then existing, and he did so when, speaking broadly, he dis- covered the process of dissolving nitrocellulose in a menstruum containing nitrobenzole and : alcohol or their equivalents, and then spreading the sensitive emulsion on the hardened film. He was subjected to almost unprecedented delays and disappointments in the Patent Office, which it is unnecessary to consider in detail, but he held tenaciously to his original conception of the invention.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>"TABLOID" " RYTOL " UNIVERSAL DEVELOPER.</head>
        <p>"TABLOID" " RYTOL " UNIVERSAL DEVELOPER.</p>
        <p>I This developer possesses exceptional all-round and gives capital results with, plates, 1 utility,films, colour plates, bromide and gaslight papers or lantern slides. It works well in all methods of development—machine, stand, tank, &amp;c. "Tabloid" " Rytol" dissolves immediately, giving a clear bright solution without sediment or scum. It works free from stain or fog with the most sensitive plates.</p>
        <p>- it gives a fine series of warm tones on gas- light papers, and a varied range of colours on lantern slides; black, brown, sepia, purple and red, without the addition of ammonium carbon- ate. Each pair of products makes four ounces of normal solution, so the contents of each car- ton make 88 ounces of normal negative de- veloper._</p>
        <p>" Tabloid " " Rytol" developer does not irri- tate the skin nor stain film or fingers.</p>
        <p>SNAPSHOTS.</p>
        <p>A new paper has been put on the market recently by the Platinotype Co., of 22, Blooms- bury-street, New Oxford-street, London, W.C., ',under the name of "Satiate," which is in-</p>
