      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY,</p>
        <p>4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. + 4.4. 4.4. 4. 4 .4. 4. 4. + +4.4. + 4. 4 4 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 44 4. 4. 4. .4. 4.4 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. .:,..? 4. 4. + + 4. ++4.+4, 4. + 4 4 4.4. +l.+4 4. + 4. 4. 4. 44 4. 4. 4. 4 4 4.44 4. +4. 4.4. 4.4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. +4.4.4. + 4. +++++4. 44 4. 4 .:4 •:. te •.• 444 0). 411 4:0 4,d • 40 11:* 41, I, :* 0) • 0 . WOMAN'S WORLD .. .0 .:..:.. • . 5.... .:..:.4..:. ~..:. • 4.4. .:..:. 1,,....&lt; .:. .:. 4. 6 , 1</p>
        <p>about May, for not only does she brave the un- lucky month, but has chosen the 13th for her wedding day. Her mother was at one time on the stage, and after her death—eight years ago —Lady Beryl was taken charge of by her grand- mother, the late Dowager Lady Clancarty.</p>
        <p>The Taste for Dancing. Nothing could have"proved a better testimony to the interest Mr. Cecil Sharpe has aroused in English folk dancing than the Country Dance Ball recently held at Chelsea, where everyone seemed to enjoy the lovely haunting music as thoroughly as the charming figures of these beautiful old-time dances. The movement has not spread in London to the extent it has in the provinces, where it has given a much needed stimulus to country life, and provided a num- ber of villages with an interest and amusement during the long winter evenings. As an exercise for those leading sedentary lives, the Morris dance is without a rival, but the country dances require less strenuous exertion. London's new- est diversion is the after supper dance at the Savoy, and judging from the number of dancers who fill the Winter Garden evening after even- ing, the idea bids fair to be as popular here as in America, whence it came to this country.</p>
        <p>corsage shows to full advantage. One of the latest models seems to have been designed from the cloak of an Italian soldier, and the old- fashioned Inverness cape. This type completely hides the figure, and requires a tall woman to "carry it off." The short woman must avoid this particular garment, but there are others which will be found quite suitable for women of medium stature, for there is as much diver- sity in these.capes as in the coats of the hour,</p>
        <p>The Newest Millinery. After Easter fashions settle down. Before that time new styles and models are on their trial, and these are always more whimsical in the spring than, at any other period. Very few of the extravagances, either in garments or mil- linery, attain popularity, for there are always a sufficiency of smart styles that satisfy the women who place suitability before eccentricity, the latter being seldom in sufficiently good taste to meet the approval of thoseeleaders of fashion who understand the art of dressing well. There will be drastic changes in coiffures this season, and the hair now shows not. only at the back, but at the sides. This is necessitated by the new millinery that raises the hat from the head and brings back the almost forgotten bandeau. There is O profusion of flowers on fashionable millinery and with all blooms black leaves are used. Sometimes the crown of a hat is almost •Oovered with black foliage, brightly coloured roses giv- ing relief, in small bunches at either side. The foundation Of such a hat should be of some pale colour, not black, then the effect is good. In fact, the crown of a hat seems to be the favourite place for the moment for heaping flowers or other trimming. Many of the newest models are excessively eccentric, but there are numerous shapes that are simple and becoming. .</p>
        <p>Concerning Bags. The b9g shows no sign of losing its present hold on the affections of its patrons, and every season new and charming designs make their appearance. This year bags of white velvet are among the prettiest shown for afternoon smart wear. These bags are embroidered with gold thread, among whicli coloured stones gleam and scintillate, or they are sewn with sequins of mother-of-pearl, dyed in many colours. Very striking are the magpie bags in snow-white .vel- vet, worked with jet embroidery and finished with jet tassels. Post Impressionism and Futur- ist 'effects influence bags as well as other items of women's dress. The deepest, richest shades, and the quaintest, queerest designs are embroid- ered in dull jet beads; or in the most vivid colours, hand-painted pictures take the place of embroidery. Silk cords in opposite shades to those of the pictures are used to suspend these bags.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>Royal Windsor, Windsor is the favourite residence of Queen Mary, and it is a matter of regret to her Majesty that Court arrangements permit her to spend so little of her time at the Castle during the spring and summer months. The Queen has done much to beautify the interior of the Castle, and in rearranging many of the apartments has studied the comfort and convenience not only of the Royal family, but of all the household and de- pendents. The kitchens at Windsor Castle are not the least of its many wonders. The chief kitchen is a very fine rbom, lined with white tiles, with a lofty ceiling. It contains a mar- vellous collection of ancient and modern copper pots and pans, and other utensils, varying in size from a boiler to an egg saucepan. The serving -table is of steel, heated by means of steam, which keef)s the entire surface very hot. - A separate -kitchen "is devoted entirely to the preparing and cooking of vegetables and salads, and a large room is given to the table deckers, who are responsible for the decorations of the Royal and other dinner tables. During King Edward's reign great improvements were made at Windsor, both internally and in the beauti- ful gardens, and these have been continued by their Majesties since they have otcupied the Castle, which is in many ways unique among the Royal palaces of Europe.</p>
        <p>The Month of the Year. The merry month of May is no longer' ushered in with old-time rites and observances, but the French have retained the pretty custom of send- ing bunches of the sweet lily of the valley to their relatives and friends on the first of May. Germany, the home of this charming flower, sends immense quantities, which are hawked about the streets of Paris on barrows heaped with fragrant blooms. Everyone wears a spray with its beautiful foliage, in the belief that it will bring good luck. The fiance chooses as big a bunch (tied with pearly ribbons) as his means will allow, as an offering to the lady of his choice, and everybody on that day enjoys the beauty of this simple, delicate floweret, which with its delicious fragrance appeals to high and low, youth and age. There has been the cus- tomary rush of weddings between Lent aria May, but at least one bride-elect—Lady Beryl Le-Poer Trench—has no superstitious prejudice</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>Craze for Black.</head>
        <p>The Craze for Black.</p>
        <p>Black has lost none of its popularity; in fact, its vogue is nothing short of a craze, and a gathering of fashionable women rather resem- bles a funeral than -a festive meeting for the purpose of enjoyment. At the same time there is a distinct tendency among older women to wear bright-coloured ribbons or flowers in black hats, and to relieve a sable dress by means of a bow, often of Futurist design, on the corsage, or some other touch of bright colour. Spring sun- shine generally comes, as this year, suddenly, and then every woman, whether old or young, feels a desire to break out into bright, gay colours, in imitation of Nature. This is evi- denced by a vest or sash of Roman silk, striped in the gayest tints, which relieves a dark blue suit, and in the many parti-coloured woollen materials that are used for this spring's new tailored suits, in conjunction with plain cloths.</p>
        <p>The New Capes. The cape was revived some time ago, but its old-time character for dowdiness clung to it so Persistently that it made little progress in popu- lar favour. This season's new designs, however, are so smart that it will prove a serious rival to the dainty little coats that are so much in evi- dence. As an extra wrap that may be donned with any dress, the new cape i a useful addi- tion to a woman's wardrobe, and, being worn open in front, the fashionable waistcoat or pretty</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
