        <p>(Reproduced by special permission of the Proprietors of TYPICAL SPRING BLOSSOMS IN OUR GARDEN SUBURBS. 'at" ‘2lOll/4 Vimik 11190. diuk ‘11" Valk Wok 'Usk Nilllak %ask Nallik Noseulk V" IA VOW Nal" 'essik \I" wok %oak siggak sisik Napo, %am,</p>
        <p>oeogt Pfg. arloau noidhd oe forvßeai. thatlfthl iho eror sb, n tively rude health to see her quite herself again," and her eyes clouded as- she sighed. e edeewdns hett, i taconahk r e and e te hp se somethen ehs rai ni bdg a, wk" hI D' cm ho ltti i isha I; n; ash you think the servants give her any of the "t'hat. Not an atom of anything ever goes into the room without Ruth seeing. it. And Ruth t 3 awfully careful. I wonder how she can stand it all. She seems never to he out of the room, day or night. I don't know what we should do without her. She is perfectly wonderful." Hugh Palmer smiled. " She is certainly very attentive," he agreed. " What a modified phrase, Hugh. Very at- tontive. I say she's wonderful; and my fear is that she will break down under the strain." He made n reply, the secret wish in his mind being that it might be far the best thing that could happen if she had to give up the nursing. " Well, what are you thinking now ?" cried Christabel, bantering him. " I have been considering whether we ought not to have a pro`fessional nurse. I agree that the strain on Miss Pallett is very great indeed.- " I thought of that too, Hugh, do you know, and I hinted at something of the sort to Aunt Elsie; but she wouldn't hear of it. She won't acknowledge that she's a bit ill, and she declared that if a processional nurse went into the room she'd order her out there and then. But of course it would be different if you arranged it. I wish you would. Are you coming to the Beeches now ?" " I am on my way, but there are two cars I must make first." " Good-bye then. But don't be longer Than you can help. I see so little of you; and last night you hurried off in such a scurry," she pouted. This sent his thoughts back into their for- mer groove, for it had been on the previous eight that his dark suspicions had first been Verified. When he reached the Beeches, Christabel met him with a smiling reproach for being so long in following her • but as their hands met 3 he held up her lovely face for him to kiss, and clung to him lovingly and kissed him ered on him the proofs of her love. passionately, detaining him while she show- They went upstairs tegether, exchanging the sweet whispers of love, and at length she tore herself away from him with a last en- trancing embrace and a murmur that he voia to come to her the instant he left the sick room. He paused a few moments in the dressing room to recover self-control before going in to his patient. .Ruth Pallett was in a chair 1:7 the bedside and rose as he entered, bid- ding him a low "Good morning." He looked searchingly at her, and it crossed his thoughts that she appeared less composed than usual, and was inclined ti shrink from his gaze. Her eyes appeared to question him nervously, as if seeking to read the thoughts behind his rather cold scrutiny. " I shall be in the dressing-room if you need. me, Dr. Palmer," she said, and went away, casting a last quick nervous glance round the room. , Ha made his examination of his patient.</p>
