      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>COMPLETE EXAMINER STORY,.</head>
        <p>COMPLETE EXAMINER STORY,.</p>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.) DR. FALMER'S PATIENT.</p>
        <p>ARTHUR W. MARCHMONT (Author of "By Right of Sword," "An IMe perial Marriage," etc.).</p>
        <p>:r • • At • 4 L</p>
        <p>t'. ;i 1 „ ,</p>
        <p>k</p>
        <p>7951</p>
        <p>,ia ,</p>
        <p>,1 , .. . „ -.. 44.:- '-s- 3/40 ;:-.tit:4i, 4tii; 411t;lit'. :.111; • .:1:r. ekk e'•' • . AS",2. 'lll4:ilf., - ~%, • 'T''' 't . 'to,„'J • • " r.i.E • ''.</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>; i I</p>
        <p>"Congratulations, doctor. I was delighted to hear the news." Hugh Falmer started, rousing himse:f with an apparent effort from the train of thought which had drawn his handsome intellectual features into a pent frowning expression, and smiled as he stopped to take the rector's out- stretched hand. "You mean about—" he paused. " My dear fellow, what could I mean ex- cept about your engagement to Miss Christa- bel Manners ?" replied the rector with a genial laugh. "Of course; thanks. But I was thinking, and for the moment my wits were wool gatn- ering, Fletcher." " I could see that. 'You certainly were not thinking of her beautiful face, or there wouldn't have been such a frown on your face. Bothered by some case ?" " Yes, that's it. By more than one case, I'm sorry to say." "Not by Miss Welbeck's, I hope? Every life is precious, but her's especially, seeing the good she does. In all Wisbury there is no one who gives away a tithe of what she gives. Well named the Lady Bountiful of the Beeches. She would be a grievous loss to many of my poor folk. I met Miss Manners just now and she said her aunt seemed bet- ter." "1 have not seen Miss Welbeck this morn- ing," replied the doctor; " but I hope she'll pull right." ghl trust l sri you feel more confident utnihrpohu, than your look suggests, Palmer. By the way do you think that that Miss Ruth Pallett experienced enough in nursing to do all she needs ? Of course I've no right to interfere but it's a big responsibility she's carrying, and somehow I—l—well, 1 have a preference for professional nursing in any really serious "She's a very good nurse, Fletcher, very good. No need to worry r3rut I that the handsome young doctor himself was -worrying, the rector could not fail to per- ceive; and as he glanced after him he shook Lis head with some misgivings. He was right. The young doctor was both troubled and sorely perplexed about Miss Welbeck's, ease. There were symptoms which d had baffled him, and he had passed a part of the night studying the case, makintg. experiments and analy.ses, in the min a tempt to shake off some very ugly suspicions which had occurred to him the day before. The issue was indeed serious. enough to monopolise his mind to the expu.sion oi even kis thoughts about his recent engagement to</p>
        <p>Christabel Manners, whose beauty fascinated him. This might vvir3ll be the case; for his sus- picions, rapidly crystallising into certainty, were that the patient was being poisoned. He found himself in an extremely difficult por.ition. Miss Welbeck was an old friend and patient of his father, atcl Hugh had re- turned to Wisbury, afier a distinguished uni- versity and hospital career to take his part in the practice. Before he left the little town he had seen a great deal of Ruth Pallett, a poor relative of Miss Welbeck's, and the friendship had at one time appeared likely to ripen into an engagement. But on his return Christabel's beauty and charm had swept all such thoughts out of his head; and the brightest day of his life had been that on which she had promised to marry him. He had found Ruth's presence at the Beeches more than a little embarrassing, for he was young en3ugh to feel some self-con- sciousness in her company; and that added to his intense disquiet now. Fight as he would against the evidence of facts, he cou7d not resist the terrible conclusion that of all those at the Beeches woo would have any op- portunity to carry out the foul play which he more _than suspected, Ruth herself was the most prominent. His position was the more embarrassing be- cause his father was away on the Continent, and there was no one whom he could at that juncture consult. Yet something must be done! and that immediately, or it would be too late; and the responsibility weighed heavily upon him. " Why, Hugh, I declare you were going to pass me.'' " Christabel," he exclaimed, rousing him- self from his gloomy thoughts. She laughed. " Why, it would have been a rhee;ulalwanresb‘evhg yourdsecaradenqa lula!r"reislheed.:ried. "We should have had all the gossips in Wisbury declar- ibh nleu,a,euyteyo.o makeme in epekarclgonupwiinthte all my y glorious s' m, Yes, and feasting his senses in her rare yboeuarevetl,mourghrtast,h"er tino:eo, fhseOrmgeblaodncyeselseel.ogNuoewnt, ceofyanofleuleoslswew:,e rc,shea. rAete hsnsidinnklo shl jealous, aominfdrsa,iindHugh. I want all add- edl• yPehireaphYaet hitllioegsunlightlaughed.te of her presence changed him " Mustn't I even think lof . dearest ? Su have all my was it whovabnrtout,,htsec .y forgive andot you. alwaysßut smiling which g pa- tient \think I nil f aunt, Miss Welbeck. 0: such a gloomy look into the eyes I N I admit I am " Your puzzled about her." ; only " You don't think she's worse, Hugh," she criedthweitirlecor ny m quick anliety. WmhoYr n gw as thattell- ing thouF,,ht she was better." " No, I don't think she's worse; but I want to see her getting quite well again." "Poor dear Lunt Elsie, so do I. I declare I'd willinutY give un some of my own nosi-</p>
