      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>APRIL 25, 1914.</head>
        <p>APRIL 25, 1914.</p>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTH.</p>
        <p>Children's Teeth and Food.</p>
        <p>Writing on this subject, Dr. Sims Wallace, late dental surgeon to the London Hospital, states his belief that there are in this country some 21,000,000 teeth in a state of ruin through eating sweets,, and goes on to urge the adoption of a diet containing farinaceous food in a form which will stimulate mastication—e.g., brown bread, and the eating of fresh.fruit at the con- clusion of a meal. By means of the fruit the teeth are cleansed. The best fruit for this pur- pose is probably the apple, which, if eaten at the end of a meal, leaves the mouth delightftilly fresh and -physiologically clean. To enjoy health the mouth must be kept clean. Foods must be eaten which require chewing,' such as bread toasted, meat, and fruit, such as apples. These foods cause a flow of saliva, which assists digespon and cleans the mouth and teeth. Because we do not chew thoroughly and much of our food is soft and sticky, our teeth want brushing. Our teeth should be brushed as we would brush out a hair comb, not across, .but from the gum towards the free edge. De- caying roots of teeth and tartar or " fur " are a serious danger to health. White or Brown Bread.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Leonard Hill, in the "Lancet," tells of an experiment which lie made with pigeons, feeding one on white, the other on brown bread and water only. The pigeon throve well on brown bread and water, but the white bread and water bird gradually faded into a miserable specimen. The question of white bread does not concern the ell-to-do, who get their supply of vitamines from milk, eggs, fresh vegetables, &amp;C.; but it is a vital matter to the children .of the poor, brought up on white bread, margarine, and tea. For them whole-meal bread is essen- tial, and the nation suffers an, enormous econ- omic wastei.of labour spent uselessly in the pre- paration and bleaching of flour to make it white.</p>
        <p>Dry Eczema. This is a very common ailment, and it is certainly a most irritating one. It is often left „after an acute attack, but just as often appears to' arise of its own accord. It must always be remembered that eczema is simply an inflamma- tion of the skin, and as such usually demands a soothing application, either ointment or lotion. But when the acute stage is over and the erup- tion assumes the form of dry red patches, cov- ered with thin scales, then something more stimulating is required. Here is a useful oint- ment: Liquid Pitch Ointment one drachm, Oleate of Bismuth Ointment four drachms, Zinc Ointment to one ounce. Should this produce any irritation after a few days then it would be well to leave it off and apply the Oleate of Bismuth Ointment alone. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>'HEMS. FOR MOTHERS.</head>
        <p>'HEMS. FOR MOTHERS.</p>
        <p>Children's Diet in Spring.</p>
        <p>Great care should be paid to the children's diet in these Spring days. The food ,need not . be costly, but good and plain, and if attention is given much of the sickness and skin troubles found at this season. can be avoided. Plenty of fruit should be used. There are oranges cheap. •\ They can be cut in thin slices and seasoned with a little sugar or cut in blocks and sugared. Bananas and oranges can be sliced and served together. Pineapple, too, can be advantageously mixed with oranges and bananas. The apples now available are fit only for cook- ing- But doctored with a little lemon peel and cinnamon stick they make good apple sauce. Then there are prunes and dates; and rhubarb, which, cut in short lengths, stewed and sweet- ened, ought to make a favourite part of every child's diet.</p>
        <p>Eggs and Vegetables. Eggs should be generously served. Their price IS coming down as the days get longer, and they are not extravagant, for they are nourishment of a concentrated sort. And nourishment is needed. Carrots, turnips, macaroni, potatoes many of thb stand-bye of the winter—may have palled on the childish appetite, and devious ways must be used to make them attractive. Macaroni, boiled until tender, and then heated in a' double boiler with a little unthick- enefl meat gravy, is tempting. So are scalloped potatoes made of boiled potatoes chopped and baked slowly with milk and melted butter. Carrots, cut in crescents and diamonds and other fancy shapes, boiled tender, and served with cream sauce or with melted butter and minced parsley are appetising' Serve as many green vegetables as possible. Cauliflower, boiled and creamed, is usually acceptable if it is daintily served. Onions, boiled very tender, covered with cream sauce and baked, are well liked, as a rule, by children old lenough to eat them. &lt;ask</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <head>COOKERY CORNER.'</head>
        <p>COOKERY CORNER.'</p>
        <p>Some Simple, Useful Dishes:</p>
        <p>Scotch stew is a•substantial and excellent dish for hot weather. Wash well four ounces of pot barley—the pearl looks finer, but it is not so good—simmer gently in one pint of white stock for an hour, then add two or three scraped carrot, cut in quarters, two or three small turnips, cut into quarters, a Spanish onion sliced (or a few shallots), some cauliflowers in sprigs is a welcome addition; add the latter only half an hour before serving. Simmer for an hour or till quite tender, adding more stock if necessary.</p>
        <p>Young Turnips. These make a delicious and wholesome dish, if stewed in the following way: Wash and very thinly pare the turnips • (I refer to the young white kind), and cut them into small, fancy shapes. Arrange them in a wide, shallow stew- pan, in which you have melted two ounces of butter. Cover, and stew slowly for about an hour, adding no water. When done, season slightly with salt and pepper, and add a few drops of lemon juice. Serve on a well warmed dish.</p>
        <p>To Boil a Tongue. Cover the tongue with cold water, cook gently for about four hours, and remove all scum.l When done lift on to a pastry board, trim off any rough edges, and shape it into position with a tiny skewer piercing the tip of the tongue; when cold brush over with glaze. Glaze is simply stock reduced by boiling to a jelly and keeping it well skimmed. To Make Brawn.</p>
        <p>Take half a pig's head pickled, a blade of mace, two, cloves, pepper and salt. Remove all the veins and other uneatable parts, put 'into enough cold water to cover, and add the mace, cloves, pepper and salt. Boil very slowly until the bones will slip out from the meat and skim well. then strain through a colander, remove the bones, and chop up the meat into dice. Let the stock boil down to a quart, add the meat and mix well. Pour into a mould, and turn out when set. To Boil a Ham.</p>
        <p>Soak for twelve hours in cold water, then scrape and weigh it. Allow half-hour to the pound, put it in cold water and cook very slowly, skimming well; allow it to cool in the water in which it has boiled. Finally lift it out, remove the skin, and cover with brown breadcrumbs, fastening a paper frill round the knuckle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>WORK-A -DAY WONDERS.</head>
        <p>WORK-A -DAY WONDERS.</p>
        <p>11.-TELLS HOW A FADED SILK BLOUSE IS MADE TO LOOK BETTER THAN WHEN NEW.</p>
        <p>"There were six of us in my kitchen," said! Patty, "when I started on Mrs. Finch's best ' blouse, so you may guess I felt a tiddly bit ner- vous at first. But I had read up my Diamond Dye Direction Book thoroughly, and knew my work, and directly I got to the mixing of the colours a sort of strong feeling came over me and I said to myself—' You go right ahead, Patty, and see if you don't score." "Mrs. Finch's blouse was made of a lovely bit of white crepe de Chine, though gone a bit yellowy here and there where the sun had caught it. She had chosen lilac for her new colour, and I had got the two packets of silk-and-wool dye for her—one pink and one purple—threepenny packets this time. The chemist, when I asked him, gave me another of the Diamond Dye Direction Books, and this I passed on to Mrs. Finch as being an encouragement to her to do her own Diamond-dyeing in future. " Well—l dyed that blouse LOVELY, and I wouldn't let it out of my hands till it was dried and pressed, and YOH SHOULD have seen what it looked like when I held it up before the neigh- bours.</p>
        <p>"Mrs. Britiges—who had been very cutting over the failures I used to have with the one- packet-for-everything, dyes—actually asked me quite respectfully if I would very much mind helping her Diamond-dye a long piece of faded linen she had just bought cheap at the sales." (To be continued.)</p>
        <p>A census of traffic passing through Cheadle village is now being taken daily, and it is ex- pected the results will show some extraordinary figures in connection with the motor traffic.</p>
        <p>The executive of the Liberal party in the Chester-le-street Division decided on Saturday to contest the seat, which is at present held by a Labour member, at the next General Election.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>.isr..</head>
