        <p>nurse." he spice sternly, Tor to nis surprise her question had been almost a challenge. " Why ? I presume you have courage enough to give your reasons; and we need not pretend that they have anything to do with splicitude for my health. She was turning at bay; and her hardness had its ef- fect on him.</p>
        <p>"1 have reasons of course if you com- pel me to state them." Her eyed flashed—brown eyes capable of great expression. ." I cannot compel you. You know that. But you will give them, if you .are not a coward."'</p>
        <p>" They are connected with certain symp- toms I have observed in Miss We:beck.</p>
        <p>"That is nearer the truth than my health, at any rate. What are they ?" " You are making this needlessly painful to me, Miss Pallett." "And what of me ?" she threw back with a quick lift of the head. It will be less painful for all concerned if you admit that you have broken down anti feel that you must have rest." "Possibly; but I don't admit anything of the sort. I am perfectly well—as you know —and I refuse to do anything unless you speak pi.ainly." it was clear that she understood him and he was surprised by her attitude of compara- tive defianc,e. "I wi:l do so if yJu wish." She paused again and her answer added to his astonishment. " You think that Miss Welbeck is being poisoned 1;" He started with a catch of the breath. " I _ .</p>
        <p>am sure of it," he said quietly. _ " And that I am the poisoner ?" "You have been always in the sick room, and I know that everything she takes passes through your hands. I hate to have to say this."</p>
        <p>"I am sure of that. And in order to avoid saying it you offered me the chance of going away quietly. I thank you; but I refuse it. You are wrong, Doctor Palmer. Dear Miss Welbeck is in no such danger. You yourself have only just told me that you found her better to-day. You have misled yourself. That is all." " I have not misled myself unfortunately, Miss Pallett. My suspicions were started two days ago; and yesterday I learnt the truth of them. I found these by the bedside last evening and I analysed tw.) of them in my surgery last night," and he laid some choco- lates on the table. " I saw where you kept such things—in your own desk. And I found others in it this morning."</p>
        <p>He expected to see her overwhelmed by this evidence; but she was unmoved. '' Very dramatic, but scarcely convincing, Dr. Pal- mer," she replied indifferently. " It.is not all. I found also a packet of face powder; at least that was the label on but the contents were—arsenic." He spoke with the utmost impressiveness; but she only shrugged her shoulders. " You charge me with attempting to poi. - on my dear relative—the on:y relative I have in the world, almost the only friend I have, with arsenic ? Why do you suppose that I should do such a dastardly thing ?" I He threw up his hands with a sigh. I "And you believe I have put arsenic into these ?" she asked, as she picked up the I sweets and examiudd them closely. "T admit</p>
        <p>THREE YEARS IN PRISON.</p>
        <p>Severe Sentence Passed on a Warrington Woman.</p>
        <p>A severe sentence of three years' penal servi- tude Was passed on a Warrington woman on Wednesday by Justice Bray at the Liverpool ,Assizes. She was the only woman prisoner at the Assizes, and was Annie Smith, a young and respectably - dressed married woman. She pleaded not guilty to forging and " uttering" a document on February 11:</p>
        <p>According to Mr. Riley's story, the prisoner's husband wag a depositor at the Warrington Savings Bank. She applied for a withdrawal form, which was given to her on her informa- tion that her husband was ill: The clerk sug- gested that, in accordance with the rules of the bank, which require that a withdrawal form must be witnessed as well as signed by the de- positor, she Should get the doctor to witness her husband's signature. Later she returned with a.form bearing the written name of Joseph Smith, and "Dr. Ghandi" (a medical man practising, in Liverpool-road, Warrington). The clerk was not satisfied, and declined to pay the £lO, which „ was applied for. Both names, counsel contended were forgeries._</p>
        <p>Dr. Ghandi denied that he had ever attested a document for either: prisoner or her husband. The signature on the document produced was not his.</p>
        <p>PRISONER CREATES A SCENE. Prisoner said her husband knew all about her signing the form, and she signed the name of the doctor, not thinking it mattered. She was intending to bring the money to her husband, and it was not for herself. She was convicted of the offence, and to their verdict the jury added a recommendation for mercy, having regard to the prisoner's little children, the youngest of whom is only ten months old. Evidence was given to the effect that the woman had been convicted of twenty-two cases of a similar kind, involving £lBO, in 1909. When the Judge was addressing the prisoner, and pointing out that she had been convicted on the clearest evidence, there was a painful scene. Mrs. Smith sobbed hysterically, and had to be held by two warders and a wardress. When a sentence of three years' penal servitude : was pronounced, she uttered a wild shriek, and clutched the handrail of the dock, frantically appealing for mercy. She lad to be forcibly removed, and, amidst her cries as she was lifted down the stairs, she wailed time after time, "My little children."</p>
      </div>
