        <p>putting his questions in his calm, procession- _ al manner, and was gratified to find that Miss Welly)ek was decidedly better. The symptoms he had expected to find were pre- sent, but less pronounced; his conclusion was inevitable. Either something had occurred to prevent the awful work he suspected being carried on, or the person who was engaged in it had taken fright and desisted. 'ho that person was, he had now no doubt; and as he sat by the bedside he rcsolved that she should have no further opportunity of continuing it. The crisis must be ..2accd at once. , - I find Miss Wlelbeck decidedly better ' this morning, Miss Pallett," he said. to Ruth as he went into the next room. "I think 60 tot), doctor," she agreed. She was very pale and trembled. " But you are not looking well. I am afraid the i strain is to) xnuch for your strength. You are on the verge of a break- down." She startcd and caught her breath quickly. "You are not going to try and send me away, doctor ?" she cried with evident agita- tion. Her rapid divination of his purpose sur- prised him. Why shou:d such a suspicion oc- cur to her ? He had meant to let nothing but solicitude show in his manner. " I am think- ing of your health," he replied. If he could get her to accept his verdict and consent to g° away it would be the best solution of the •• difficulty. rin She paused before a nsWeg and appeared to be making an effort for self-control. "What has made you suddenly fear this ?" she asked next. " I am quite well." Pardon me, but I can see your condition of nervous tension." • "When did you discover it ?" This was almost a scoff. "The strain of the nursing is too much for you. I ought to have spoken before about it. She glanced" ' up at him. and then turned her hcad away and stood fidgeting with a 1 book on the table close to her. She was very , slight and graceful, and the pose shewed her profile tn her which ch as ap- pealed p- tt ey, and there t deli- cate but not prwas a sugges- tion o f helplessness , pealed to him despite the ugly thoughts he i entertained. _ ..</p>
        <p>Her attitude recalled a moment of the pact when he had in truth been on the point of asking for her love. The recollection chilled him for a second; but at the same time made his present task infinitely mare repulsive. It was hateful to have to tell her that he knew her purpose, and that he was resolved to pre- vent her entering the sick room again. A heavy sigh from her ended the pause, and she raised her head and looked him steadily in the eyes. " You have s3mething in the back of your mind, Dr. Faliner. What is it ?" " I think that someone should relieve you from this heavy nursing,- he said, trying to make her feel by his tone all that was benind the words.</p>
        <p>" Christabel ?" Her lip curled as she rapped out the word. This angered him. on," he replied coldly. " You think I am neglecting my duty or am unfit for it ? Which ?"</p>
        <p>"I have given my opini-</p>
        <p>. .• . I think you should not continue to act as</p>
