      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0243" n="art0243">
        <head>HOW TO USE A CHEQUE BOOK.</head>
        <p>HOW TO USE A CHEQUE BOOK.</p>
        <p>Nothing is so unwise—slid husbands but realise the fact—than- the suburban habit of doling out small sums in petty cash to the housewife. A woman with a few shillings in her purse wastes them more often than not; if she has a cheque-book she will jealously watch every penny of a dwindling five pound balance. It is an excellent plan, by the way, to write the amount of your balance on the opposite side of your counterfoil each time you draw a cheque, remembering to- deduct the sum charged for a cheque-book as it is ordered, 'and bank charges (if any are made), quarterly. The woman who invariably does this can never be— however bad an arithmetician—more than a few shillings out on the sum total she possesses, and she should make a point of seeing and check- ing her pass book from time to time. In my own business, which was, of course, ex- ceptionally intimate' and personal in view of a new venture, I took pains to explain a few ele- mentary rules to clients, and found trouble was consequently saved with the accounts. As I started with some four hundred customers whose accounts at their request had been trans- ferred from the head office, the post was no sinecure, even at first.—" Woman at Home." \Mk</p>
      </div>
