        <p>these are mine, out rney are not poisonea, Dr. Palmer:: "I made another analysis, and I know that my patient has had arsenic given to her. The nroo:a are beyond question." Then pointing to the sweets, he said, " Dare you taice them yourself ?" She started and hesitated, and he saw her Muscles stiffen as she bit her lips. The 'first a took up reuarlmsuigrd betweenneoffear her sheehadsh2wll. " Yes," she mteeth and one of the sweets to place it between her lips. He put out his hand suddenly and stopped her. ' Think what you are doing," he cried. his face much pa'cr than hers. " I merely, wished to .prove your mistake." she answered steadily; but he t‘ole the sweet from her and tossed it back on the table. " Let this miserable thing end. Go away and no one shall know anything front me,'' he declared very earnestly. Again she looked away irom him as she ap- peared to think over the offer. Then she shook her head. "I will not go, Dr. Palmer," she said very resolutely, lifting her eyes to meet his. " Miss Welbeck trusts me; she is restless whenever I am out of the room; and I will not desert her—even for this." "You leave me no other alternative, al- though heaven knows how unwfrangfy r take it. But there is no other way." Once more she paused. 'l'here is another way," she said then, speaking earnestly. " You can leave things as they are, and I will givte you my solemn word ef honour that not a thought of this kind of thing shall ever have to cross your mind again. You will listen to me ?" she cried with a quick change to a tone of almost piteous entreaty as she seized his arm and looked pleadingly into his eyes. 'For heaven's sake listen to me. You were not always hard like this. Dr. Pal- mer, Hugh, I beg and pray to you, hear me, and do this little thing I ask. Ah, dons turn away like that," she cried, as he drew back. "Don't! Don't! You know I have never breathed a word of the old feeling there used to be between us. I would not now for any other cause. See, I go on my knees to you. I plead to you. I know that once you cared for me, and I appeal to that old feeiing now, and urge you by it to—" She stopped sudcienly as the door opened and Christabel entered, to stand in amaze- ment at seeing her clinging to him. Hugh Palmer drew back in great confu- sion, and Ruth got up with a delicate sigh. " Hugh! Ruth!" exclaimed Christabel. "What—?" "I had better explain," said Ruth, for the young doctor was speechless. "Dr. Fainter has—'' "It was Hugh I heard just now," inter- posed Christabel, her eyes flashing. He has accused me of trying to poison igloo Welbeck, and declares that I must not remain to nurse her any longer," continued Ruth in a dull level tone. `• I was begging him not to force this. That is all. Ile declares that the poison has been administered in these sweets." " Hugh! How horrible," cried Christabel aghast. " But it is of course nonsense. I'll soon prove that;" and she took up one of the chocolates. "No. You must not," cried Ruth as she seized her hand and wrested the sweet awaT:</p>
