        <p>804 4iLtazu OF THE MATCHES. 'l'44 f :illatc'hes naturally provide the best --111 which one can criticise, as the</p>
        <p>in it—Warrington. The forwards, however, threw away Eionia fine chances to score. '</p>
        <p>QUICK SCORING.</p>
        <p>The first portion was well advanced before there was any score. Then points came quickly. From a: scrummage Nicholas got the. ball out smartly and he sent Young away on one of his lightning, dashes, although a minute before he had been laid out by a nasty knock in the face from Brain. He snipped the ball to Baker, who was well placed on the wing. The winger was quick to avail himself of the opportunity, dodged half a dozen hull Kingston men, and scored a clever try well but. In the absence of Jolley, Nicholas took the kick and just missed goaling. Almost immediately the Rovers got a try through McGeiver, and this was converted. Two points to the bad, Warrington woke up, and Clare let Young away and he scored the best try of the match. It was his initial effort in the scoring line. At half-time Warrington led by six points to five.</p>
      </div>
