      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0255" n="art0255">
        <head>Local Clubs Wind Up Their krogrammes.</head>
        <p>Local Clubs Wind Up Their krogrammes.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the Latchford Club brought their season's fixtures to a close, playing a couple of watches in the afternooji at iiroadheath and Bowdon Vale. Previous to the day's play the La tchfordians had good prospects of gaining second position in the League table, but the players simply threw their cnances away, as the committee were only able to place four of their cup team in the field, the vacancies being filled with reserves. • At the conclusion of the match with Broad- heath, Latchford journeyed on to play their return fixture with Bowdon Vale, and owing to the exertions put forth in the first game it was soon evident that the players had had quite enough for one day, as the game proved to be one-sided, the home team scoring four times in the first half and adding two further goals before ,the close, while Latchford were only able to find the net on one'oecasion.- Crosfield's Recreation were disappointed with their fixture with .Altrincham St. Vincent's. The home players were in readiness to take part in the game and waited until 4.30, but the visi- tors failed to put in an appearance. Orford St. John's met and defeated the Emmanuel Old Boys on the Stockton lreath ground, in the semi-final ' for the Warrington Minor Medal Competition. The first half was keenly contested, but no scoring took place, altheugh the Old. Boys 'should have taken' the lead from a penalty kick: St. John's had the best of the play in the second half and eventually ran out winners by three goals to nil.•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0256" n="art0256">
        <head>NORTH CHESHIRE LEAGUE. Broadheath v. Latchford.</head>
        <p>NORTH CHESHIRE LEAGUE. Broadheath v. Latchford.</p>
        <p>At Broadheath. The visitors won the toss and played against the wind. Straight from the kick-off the home forwards wit near the visitors' goal, when Green brought off a good save. Latchford transferred play to the home end, and Maddock centring from the right, Vernon scored a grand goal. From the centre-kick the home team retaliated and they quickly equalised. Foven play followed and each goal was visited in turn, when P. Webb put Latchford in front again with a good goal, and Clegg equalised. The interval arrived With the score two goals each.</p>
        <p>On resuming, the visitors had the best of the exchanges, but they failed to make the best of their opportunities in front of goal, while the home defenders were at their best, and eventu- ally the home forwards got through and gained the lead. Result: Broadheath, three goals; Latchford, two goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0257" n="art0257">
        <head>Bowdon Vale v. Latchford.</head>
        <p>Bowdon Vale v. Latchford.</p>
        <p>On the Vale's ground. The game opened rather sensationally, for the home team, from the kick-off, took the ball into the visitors' quar- ters and spored within half a minute. Uninter- esting play in mid-field followed. Latchford's display was not very convincing, and the home team had matters much their own way, and at half-time were leading by tour goals to nil. The visitors started the second portion with only nine players, but gave an improved display. Zthe home team were successful in scoring two more'-goals, ',while Connor scored for the visitors. Result: Bowdon Vale, six goals; Latchford, one goal.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT MINOR MEDAL COMPETITION. Semi-final Tie. Orford St. John's v. Emmanuel Old Boys.</p>
        <p>On the West-avenue ground, Stockton Heath, before a fair number of supporters. Teams : Orford St. John's—Forber; Millward and Harri- son; Denny, Stretford and Atherton; W. Smith, Tickle, Meachin, Hankinson and Smalley. Em- manuel Old Boys—Knight; Eccles and Roberts; Garnett, Brookes and Caldwell; Wainwright, Millington, Rigby, Whittaker and Rowe. Lines- men, Messrs. W. Dennett and G. Rushton; referee, Mr. S. Breese.</p>
        <p>Orford won the toss and played against the wind. Rigby set the ball rolling and play was fast and keenly contested. Both goalkeepers were early called upon to clear, and then the Old Boys had slightly the best of play, but the St. John's defenders were in good form and Forber had not much to do. St. John's took up the running, but good work by the Old Boys' defence transferred play to the opposite end, where a penalty kick was entrusted to Milling- ton, and he shot straight at Forber, who brought off a good clearance. Play was now very even and each side strove hard to gain a point, but the interval arrived without any sore being made.</p>
        <p>On resuming, St. John's had the wind at their backs, and they very early assumed the aggres- sive, and Knight was quickly called upon, but cleared well. The Old Boys' forwards at this juncture could do little against the St. John's half-backs. Millward opened the score from a free-kick, Knight being well beaten, and directly after Tickle added a second goal. The Old Boys now seemed to lose heart, and the game was con- tested in their portion of the field, Hank-inson a third goal. tesult : Orford St. John's, , tsleiorreilig egoals; Emmanuel Old Boys, nil.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT MEDAL COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>For the final tie, to be played at Penketh Recreation Ground to-day, between Orford St.. John's and St. Barnabas', the Orford team will he: J. Forloer; C. Harrison and W. Mill- ward; J. Denny, W. Stretford and L. Atherton; T. Tickle, A. Meachin (captain), W. Smith, S. Hapkinson, and W. Smalley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0258" n="art0258">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>--- LATCHFORD CLUB'S ANNUAL MEETING. The annual meeting was held at Atkinson's Cafe on Saturday evening, Mr. Wilkinson occu- pying the chair. .</p>
        <p>The Heonroercaorryd sSefoorrettLy, rinasthisse report, stated were:— that th First eleven: Played 25, won 13, lost 11, drawn 1, goals for 75, goals against 6.3. Second eleven: Played 23, won 12, lost 7, drawn 4, goals for 103, goals against 44. Considering that the fixture list had been much stronger than in previous years the re- sults were not at all bad, and five out of the eleven defeats against the first eleven were only by the odd goal. The second eleven had had a really good season, and only lost one game be- tween December 20th and March 21st. Several very capable young players had been discovered, which made the prospects for next season bright. The principal goal scorers were: J. E. Pearson 40, W. H. l'‘finards 34, W. S. Furness 20, W. Starkey 11, A. Knight 10, A. G. Marsden 9, A. Starkey 10. The following officials were elected for next season: Captain first eleven, W. Fletcher; sub. captain, J. E. Pearson; cantain second eleven, A. E. Bradshaw; sub-captain, C. Newbold; hon. treasurer, T. Dixon; hon. secretary, Arthu; Starkey. 7, Grosvenor-terrace, Latchford; com- mittee, W. W. Starkey (chairman). W. Fletcher‘ J. E. Pearson, A. E. 'Bradshaw. C. Newbold, A. Knight, W. S. Furness and T. Dixon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0259" n="art0259">
        <head>EXCELLENT WEATHER FAVOURS THE START.'</head>
        <p>EXCELLENT WEATHER FAVOURS THE START.'</p>
        <p>Works' and "Peter Peacock" Competitions.</p>
        <p>By “THUMB BIAS."</p>
        <p>Saturday was a glorious day from a bowler's point of view. Several greens were opened, in- cluding the one at the Liberal Club and that at the Conservative Club. All the greens are reported to be'in excellent condition, but we are afraid that if the Mid-sunimer-like weather con- tinuesa ee night there was a slightri e h s downpour. O grumbling amongst d will be bidr7s:i some but this would have ri Wednesday little, if any, .effect. Of course, after Thursday night's rain, We tetreea' l o don't want a "refresher" this afternoon, and this leads me to think bowlers are somewhat on a par with farmers in a desire that they were the control- ling forces of the elements. But still so far we have been favoured, and it is te be hoped that these now pleasant conditions foreshadow really decent weather throughout the season. ' On the Horse and Jockey, green a start was made in the Works' Competition, and keen in- terest was taken in the games in the preliminary round. The players were most enthusiastic over the beautiful running, and the spectators were equally well satisfied with their accommodation.</p>
        <p>Such keen interest is being taken in the Challenge CCu competition in Club that a record "t the P c"oPnnetecertiPoneawcocitkh entry of over 700 is expected. There seems to be such enthusiasm that the committee have. decided. to ‘extend the date for entering until next Thursday, when the draw will take place. Last year there were 600 entries. At a meeting of the a4tmittee on Thursday a large 'numbei of handicaps were fixed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0260" n="art0260">
        <head>WORKS' ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>WORKS' ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>" Finlayon " Cup Competition.</p>
        <p>Five matches were played in the preliminary round of this competition, and the remaining three will be played this afternoon, when, in addition, four matches in the first round proper will be decided.</p>
        <p>In the following results the name of the member of the winning team is printed first in each game : Walker's No. 2 V. Firth Mattress No. 2. Walker's won by 19 points. R. Rose (7), 15; E. Hardman (6), 11. J. Smith, capt. (7), 15; A. Pennington (6), 6. T. Sharpies (7), 7; W. Shelmerdine (6), 15. W. Allen (7), 15; H. Pearson (6), 13. E. Darby (7), 15; A. Edwards (6), 11. W. Gamble (7), 10; J. Bird (6), 15. D. Rudge (7), 15; S. Kennon (6), 7. E. T. Rogers (7), 15; W. Potter (6), 11. J. Leigh (7), 15; W. Hewitt (6), 10. J. 11. Woods (7), 15; W. Osborne, capt. (6), 12. G. Bates (7), 13; F. Lawless (6), 15. G. Percival (7), 13; T. Moores (6), 16. Total—Walker's, 163; Firth Mattress, 144.</p>
        <p>Crosfield's Tin Dept. v. Rylands' No. 4. Crosfield's won by 3 points. W. Pritchard (4), 7; B. Jolley (7), 15. W. Fell (6), 15; W. Gallop (5), 10. T. Dobson (4), 8; J. Lowe (4), 15. E. Warburton (4), is; A. Duckworth (4), 4, W. Lawton (3), 15; T. Peters (6), 11. H. Carter (4), 15; G. Thomas (7), 13. J. Ashburner (4), 15; H. Widdop (4), 5. W. Stokes (3), 10; H. Worsley (3), 15. H. Littlewood (5), 5; C. Mitchell (7), 15. J. Woods (4), 15; H. P. Bate, caPL (5), 13. W. Birchall (5), 15; J. Meakin (4), 12. A. Gleave (2), 11; J. Sinclair (7), 15- Total—Crosfield's. 146: Rylands', 143.</p>
        <p>Crosfield's Glycerine v. Crosfield's No. 1. Crosfield's Glycerine won by 25 Points. W. Johnson (6), 9; J. Mather, caPt. (5), 15. J. Ryder (7), 15; W. Paget (5), 11. W. Skelland (5), 9; J. Savage (7), 15. F. Beardsall (5), 15; J. Battersby (5), 11. R. Brobbin (3), 15; J. Williams (6), 10. J. Smith (7), 15; J. Gleave, jun. (5), 6. J. Hatton (6), 15; J. Bradshaw (5), 9. J. Oakes (5), 15; H. Osbourne (5), 8. B. Jennings, capt (5), 15; J. Gleave, sem (5), G. J. Bolton (6), 15; P. Cliffe (5), 12. A. Webb (3), 7; J. Haywood (6), 15. J. Henshall (6), 13; J. Holcroft (5), 15. Total—Glycerine, 158; No. 1, 133.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall Tannery v. St. Helens Cable No. 2. Grappenhall Tannery won by 12 points. J. Booth (5), 11; J. Shaw (6), 15. _ H. Ratcliffe (7), 15; W. Willcox (6), 12. W. Phillips (6), 15; J. Lampard (7), 14. J. Stanley, capt. (5), 15; R. Woods (6), 11. H. McMahon (5), 15; C. Baker (6), 14. W. Sutton (7), 9; J. Rowlands (7), 15. E. Miller (6), 11; J. Sarginsoa (6), 15. E. Sutton (6), 15; G. Barton (6), 14. H. Stanley (5), 15; A. Fisher (7), 7. A. Myatt (5), 15; W. Morrison (6), 9. J. Hewitt (7), 11; J. Baguley (6), 15. J. Plumpton (6), 15; C. Derbyshire (5), 9. Total--Grappenhall, 162; Cable No. 2, 150.</p>
        <p>Whitecross Wiredrawers v. Richmond's (Grappenhall), Whitecross Wiredrawers won by 18 points. J. Lingard (5), 15; B. Foster (5), 12. ' H. Benion, capt. (4), 15; R. Gallop, capi. (6), 14. T. Ellison, jun. (5), 15; H. Greening (7), 8. C. Cooper (5), 8; S. Brookes (6), 15. T. Sutcliffe (6), 15; W. Nicholson (6), 12. J. Roughley (4), 15; J. Garmston (7), 12. D. Clayton (5), 15; E. Potts (5), 8. S. Leigh, jun. (5), 15; R. Bennett (4), 11. W. Waring (7), 15; F. Garmston (5), 7, J. Lister (4), 6; W. Brookes (6), 15. A. Foster (6), 10; S. Mellor (7), 15. J. Waring (7), 15; J. Finnigan (5), 12. Total—Whitecross, 159; Richmond's, 141.</p>
        <p>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY. PRELIMINARY ROUND—continued. 2.o—Whitecrocs Ropery V. Fletcher's. 2.50—P. and K. Foundry v. Rylands' No. 1. 4.10--L. and N.-W. Railway v. Cheshire Lines. FIRST ROUND (ProPer)e</p>
        <p>3.4o—Bewsey Wire Ropery V. Alliance Box Co. 4.3o—Richmond's (Academy-street) V. Fennel. street Tannery. s.2o—Crosfield's Tin v. Penketh Tannery. 6.10—P. and K. Boiler Yard v. Ilandall Bros.' Tannery. Referee: T. Humphreys. Assistants: A. Peckett and P. Dale.</p>
