      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0261" n="art0261">
        <head>Saturday, May 2nd.</head>
        <p>Saturday, May 2nd.</p>
        <p>2.o—Rylands' Apprentices v. Guest Bros.' Tan. 2.so—Warrington Printers V. Rylands' No. 2. 3.40—Warrington Tan. v. Whitecross Engineers 4.3o—Crosfield'B (Cheshire) v. Crosfield's Glyc. s.2o—Ship Canal (Bridgewater Dept.) v. How- ley Tannery. 6.lo—Stockton H. Forge v. Assurance Agents.</p>
        <p>7.o—St. Helens Cable No. 1 v. Thewiis and Griffiths' No. 1. Referee :F. Dean. Assistants: W. Robinson and J. Broadhurst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0262" n="art0262">
        <head>CROWN GREEN HANDICAP.</head>
        <p>CROWN GREEN HANDICAP.</p>
        <p>Cemetery Player in Semi-final.</p>
        <p>In the finals for the British Crown Green Association Easter handicap, played at the Park Hotel, Birkdale, on Saturday, the two Warrington men who still "survived" were Mr. N. Fielding and Mr. F. Dale, Warrington Cemetery. The former was handicapped at three and the latter at foF. Mr. Fielding, who was amongst the prize-winners, met W. Ley- land, Wigan -Subscription (5), in the fourth round, and althogigh he had the best of matters, the game, which ended in a victory for the War- rington man by 21-20, was a very good one. Mr. Dale beat J. Poulson, Newton-le-Willows, by 21-7, but in the next round was beaten by R. Bradley, Rainford (5), who was subsequently beaten in the final by 21-9. is ,rather singu- lar that after beating P. Mercer; Eceleston (3), 91-15, and T. Golbourn, Litherland (5), 21-19, in the fifth and sixth rounds respectively, thitt. Mr. Fielding should be knocked out by Mr. Bradley, who accounted for the other Warring- ton player, by 21-20. This game, which . knocked the Warrington man out of the final, was a very stiff one, and had Bradley not stuck so doggedly to his man, it is doubtful whether he would have won. In the final IL Wilson. Earlestown (4), appeared the more skilled, and won by 21-15. However, Bradley, by his play, throughout merited the second place. Details</p>
        <p>Fourth Round..</p>
        <p>J. H. Wainwright, Stanley •(5), 21; G. Walker, Wigan (4), 11. R. Wilson, Earlestown (4), 21; R. S. Bridge (owes 5), 12. E. Cropper, Coronation (1), 21; A. Hornby, Earlestown (4), 11. R. Bradley, Rainford (5), 21; H. Bathes, Stanley</p>
        <p>Arms (scratch), 19. P. Mercer, Eccleston (3), 21; E. Naylor, Rain. hill (3), 10. AL Talbot, Earlestown (6), 21; E. Price, Thatto HeaUs,(3), 11. T. Ball, Wigan Subscription (4), 21; J. Prest- , wich, Promenade (3), 15. I J. Heath, Roebuck (4), 21; A. C. Smith, Shrews- bury (5), 15. N. Fielding, Warrington Cemetery (3),.21; W. Leyland, Wigan Subscription (5), 20. F. Dale, Warrington' Cemetery (4), 21; J. Poul.</p>
        <p>son, Newton-le-Willows (4), 7. J. W. Ward, Royal Excelsior (scratch), 21; E. Morgan, Promenade (owes 1), 1 J. T. Hilton, Wigan Subscription (1), 21; W. Lea, Park (1), 16. R. Sanderson, Park (owes 2), 21; T. C. Jones, Promenade (owes 1), IS.</p>
        <p>Fifth Round. R. Wilson 21, B. Waddell 16. J. T. llilton 21, J. Roby 11. T. Ball 21, E. Cropper 15. J. W. Ward 21, J. Heath 9. R. Bradley 21, F. Dale , J. H. Wainwright 21, R. Sanderson 12.' N. Fielding 21, P. Mercer 15. T. Colbourn 21, J. Talbot 19.</p>
        <p>Sixth Round. R. Wilson 21, J. H. Wainwright 15. R. Bradley 21, J. T. Hilton 14. N. Fielding 21, T. GoMourn 19. J. W. Ward 21, T. Ball 13.</p>
