      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0294" n="art0294">
        <head>INER, SATURDAY, APRIL 25, 1914.</head>
        <p>INER, SATURDAY, APRIL 25, 1914.</p>
        <p>LALL RIGHTS RESERV2D.7 • WHERE SOCIETY BRIDES</p>
        <p>ARE MARRIED.</p>
        <p>St. George's, Hanover Square, has been for many generations the home of Hymen. Situated in the very centre of what was un- til a very few years ago, the richest part of London, its parishioners were drawn from among some of the wealthiest families in the land. The portico is the most beautiful in London With its six 'Corinthian columns. St. George's is one of the fifty new churches that were built in the reign of Queen Anne. There •is a fine painting of the Last Supper as altar piece, attributed to Sir James Thornhill. The chancel and choir etalls are in oak, the effect being of great beauty when, at a marriage, the elaborate floral de- corations of the present day are seen against this background. Especially is this so in the case of a military or naval wedding, when soldiers or sailors line the ais:e, forming an effective frame for the white array of the bride and the pale tints of the bridesmaids' dresses.</p>
        <p>The choir of St. George's is a very fine one and with Dr. Jolly at the organ the fully choral wedding- services are of great beauty, the voices of the boys and men being not only carefully chosen but as carefully trained. It has been remarked of this church that the marriage service is conspicuous for dignity, and solemnity, qualities that are occasionally absent from smart' weddings. INTERESTING SIGNATURES.</p>
        <p>Re- gister of St. George's Among the interesting entries in the Re- cultivated Americans in great numbers. It records how one Marianne Evans was mar- -1 ried to Mr. Walter Cross; the bride was grey-haired woman, the a was no • less a person ibsridoengerotehma: attracts a young r man in the prime. of life. lclarianne Evans than George Eliot, the celebrated authoress. During the last 'fifteen years the church of St Paul's,' KnightslXidgp, has become a rival to St. George's, Hanover Square, as a fav- ourite place for fasitionable weddings. Among its parishioners are the Grosvenors and many other familiesJof great wealth and distinction. The register contains signa- tures of supreme 1 interest. The clear and symmetrical autograph of the late William Ewart Gladstone, a beautiful piece of ges- ture, is shown with pride by the vestry clerk. He shows also the plain "George' signed by the late Duke of Cambridge, on the occa- sion of the marriage of his physician, Dr. Manley Sims: No fewer than three royal princesses signed the register after the mar- riage of the Hon. Simn Baring, Maid of Honour to Queen 'Victoria, to Sir James Reid hi 1899. The church was licensed for marriages in pB4-3, and amongst the first wit- nesses who signed the register ,was the great Duke of Wellington.</p>
        <p>A ROYAL PECULIAR. The Chanel Royal, St. jam„, Palace, is one of the most interesting of London churches. It is supposed to have formed part of the original Palace, built by Henry VIII. on the site of what was once a Hospital for Lepers, in the fields that are fIOW the Mall and the Park, but were then a long way from any houses. It is a small chapel, the length being but 62 feet and a peculiar- ity is that the roof is copper, not lead. The ceiling in a Renaissance design 'of blue and gold is attributed ,to Holbein. Here and there appears the united cypher of Henry and Anne Boleyn enwreathed by a true- lovers' knot. The date, 1540, records the time of this fond affection which was ended so abruptly. There are five galleries in this small chapel, the Royal Closet, the Peeresses', the Household, the Strangers', and the Organ. When the King and Queen attend weddings they sometimes sit in the Royal Closet, and sometimes have chairs in front of the chan- cel. The pews are all wainscoted with oak and the seats are also oak. This deep back- ground is very effective for a wedding and the ornamental scarlet and gold of the chor- isters affords a brilliancy of colour to be found in no other church. This chapel is a Royal Peculiar, belonging to the Court, and srieclal permission has to be obtained from the Ring for we,ldings to be celebrated there. It is oppned to the public in certain condi- tions. Early service on Sundays used always to be held here for the Royal Family until after the reconstruction in 1005. THE GUARDS' CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>----- - It is difficult to imagine a prettier sight than that afforded by a military wedding at the Guards' Chapel at the Wellington Bar- racks built in 1840 on the model of a Grecian temple. Its proportions were good but the interior arrangement and fittings left much to be desired with requisites for divine ser- vice. In 1877 the Brigade of Guards, hav- ing obtained the necessary permission, began the work which has resulted in making the interior of an exquisite beauty, not only in form but in colour, and in arrangement suit- able; for the purpose of divine worship. All the designs were by the late George E. Street, the celebrated B.A. The marbles used in the decorations are in shades of rose- red, grey, green and b:a,ck, this last is chief- ly used for flooring. The effect of pale tones with hidden electric lights, is quite lovely and gives the walls the effect of being suf- fused .with soft sunset tints. In the old days when the Spanish Embassy had its quarters in Portman Square, the chapel in lv situated for the. members of the Legation. Spanish Place was very convenient- The extetior of the church remains as it was then. The architecture is Early English. The Presbytery is on the site of the old stables. It is a most interesting building. with spacious room wainscoted in oak and the arms of Spain in the stained glass win- dows. In the refectory are some interesting paintings and in the sacristy there is a very fine old ivory crucifix, a marvel of fine carv- ing. Beyond conception beautiful is, the ex- pression of the crucified Saviour, the eyes leaking -upwards. This crUeifix was execut- ed in the Netherlands during the reign Of King Char:es V. of, Spain. Rubies let into the ivory represent,the drops of blood from the,hands, the feet, and the side. The interior of ' the church is of great beauty, much of which is due to the fine vaulted roof with its decorated arches, the ' whole kept light in Colour. The fine marbles and mosaics of the altar are admirable andvery the wide spaces of the chancel allow them to be seen to singular advantage. High on the left is the royal pew where the King and QQueenSnail' 6:1, when they attend the service here. -Above this pew is the personal standard of the monarchs of Spain, presented by !King Mfonso.. Marriages in this chapel have all the sur- roundings that lend picturesqueness to such occasions and the so:etan nature of the Sacra- m-nt is not for a moment forgotten. At diplomatic weddings the scene is very brilliant owing to the /cumber of magnificent uniforms worn /by Ambassadors and members of the L,gritio6s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0295" n="art0295">
        <head>WHERE m.p.!s ARE. MARRIED.</head>
        <p>WHERE m.p.!s ARE. MARRIED.</p>
        <p>St. Margaret's, Westminster, though handsome enough in the Gothic style, com- pares very much to its own detriment with Westminsten. Abbey and is placed so •as to hide a considerable portion of this beautiful old building on the eastern side. Though b ners built originally by the parishio, it has been kept up at the expense of Parlament for nearly two hundred years and is now cons&amp; erk‘d a national foundation for the use of flit llouse of Commons. Consequently, many nuimbers are married in this church and in such cases the congregation consist largely of members. their wives .and daughters. The church is not very- well adapted for a wed- ding, the central aisle being rather narrow, the entire Nyidth of the church being but 65 feet. The chancel however, is handsome and enclosed by beautifully wrought iron work.</p>
        <p>Among interesting marriages celebrated at this church were those of the poet, Milton, whose banns are in the register; Pepys, the famous diarist, Edmund Waller and Thomas Campbell. The Church of the Assumption, in War- wick Street, off Recent Street, once attached to the Portuguese Legation, has had a curi- ous history. At one time it was known as the Shilling Opera, because owing to the number of eminent operatic stars who formed the choir, seats were charged is. each and a very fashionable congregation was attracted by the music. The church is small in size and very plain of exterior. Thirty-five years ago the rector was Monsignor Talbot, seventh son of the second Viscount Innestrz</p>
      </div>
