      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0297" n="art0297">
        <head>Photograph by Birtles. MR. DUNN AND HIS BRIDE.</head>
        <p>Photograph by Birtles. MR. DUNN AND HIS BRIDE.</p>
        <p>Miss Ethel Brocklehurst, only daughter of ,Mr. and Mrs. S. Brocklehurst, Moss House, Stockton Heath, and ,niece of the Mayor, was , married to Mr. Arthur Mills Dunn, son of the late Mr. Alfred H. and Mrs. Dunn, of Stockton Heath, at Bold-street Wesleyan, Church on , Thursday. The officiating clergyman was the ' Rev. Joseph Howard (uncle of the bridegroom). Mr. Frank Hickman presided at the organ, and played appropriate wedding music. ' The bride, who was given away by Tier father, wore a dress of soft satin and brocaded crepe de chine, trimmed with old lace collar and cuffs. She wore a veil of old lace and carried a bouquet lof white roses and lilies of the valley. Her I travelling dress was of blue suiting, with bro- ceded silk vest, and trimmed with amber but- tons. The bridesmaids were Miss Mabel Isaac (cousin of the bridegroom), and Miss Gladys Peacock, of Fairbourne (cousin of the bride). I Miss Isaac's dress was of white voile, trimmed with lace, and she wore a cream Leghorn hat. Miss Peacock wore a dress of soft white satin and a Limerick lace hat, flowered ninon, pink ' ribbons and chiffon roses. The best man was Mr. S. Brocklehurst (brother of the bride). The wedding breakfast was partaken of at the Winmarleigh Cafe, and the Mayor proposed the toast of the bride, whilst Mr. J. W. Ilayward I proposed that of the bridegroom. Each were suitably honoured.</p>
        <p>After the departure of the newly-wedded couple for the honeymoon, which is being spent in North Wales, the company enjoyed a happy musical evening, whilst dancing was also indulged in.</p>
        <p>LIST OF THE PRESENTS. The following were the presents:— Bridegroom to bride, cheque. Bride to bridegroom, reading chair. Bride's father, cheque. Bride's mother, household linen. Bridegroom's mother, piano. Mr. S. T. Brocklehurst (brother of bride). cheque. Mr. S. B. Brocklehurst (brother of bride), carpet sweeper. Mr. A. Howard Dunn (brother of bridegroom), large engravip,g:</p>
        <p>Afityor -(Councillor P. Peacock), silver ser- viette rings. The Mayoress (Mrs. Peacock), hem-stitched linen sheets. Mr. and Mrs. T. Brough, eiderdown quilt. , Miss Brough, pen painted cushion. Miss Annie Brough, cut-glass salts and silver spoons. Master Walter Brough, silver sugar tongs. Mr. and Mrs. W. Broadhurst, pillow slips. Mrs. Baker, half-dozen afternoon spoons.</p>
        <p>[lna one or nine firotners, everyone v: whom was over six feet in height. One of them became the eighteenth Earl of Shrews- bury. Three of them entered the English Church, Dr. Talbot was the first convert re- ceived by Cardinal Manning and this was the first Roman church ever enteted by Car- dinal Newman.</p>
        <p>One hundred years ago Father Nassau, the spiritual director of Mrs. Fitsherbert, was rector and married her in this church to George IV. Some distinguished Catholic couples have been married here, and it is the place of worship attended by the Duke of Norfolk when residing in his house in St. James' Square.</p>
        <p>Mrs. R. J. Cook (Penarth), music cabinet. Miss Dolly Caine, silver jam dish. Mr. and Mrs. Crabtree (Failsworth), Chester- field back. Mr. and Mrs. Frederick J. Dunn (Dublin), hot water kettle. Miss H. Dunn (Canterbury), picture. Mr. and Mrs. E. England, fruit bowl. Mr. and Mrs. G. Galin, bed spread. The Misses Phyllis and Madge Gillin, cut-glass sugar basin. Mr. and Mrs. J. W. Hayward, silver egg stand. Mr. and Mrs. William Isaac and family, case of fish eaters and silver cake basket. Miss Hilda Jones, cut-glass celery glass. Mr. George Jack, velvet pile table cover. Mr. and Mrs. Kitchen (I3acup), table cloth. Mr. and Mrs. Luxton (Oldham), water-colours. The Misses Mary and Edith Lyon • (Crank), table centre and silver cake knife. Mr. and Mrs. J. Langley, silver toast rack. Mr. and Mrs R. Jackson Moss, silver flower stand.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. T. Morgan (Crewe), silver jam spoons,</p>
        <p>Mr. L. Morgan and Miss Ellis (Crewe), silver- mounted butter dish. Mr. and Mrs. J. Myles (Leeds), bed spread. Mr. and Mrs. C. Neild (Crewe), silver toast rack. Mr. and Mrs. Arthur Peacock (Fairbourne), silver cake basket. Messrs. A. and G. Peacock, clock.</p>
        <p>Master Reg. Peacock, silver-mounted sugar sifter. Mr. and Mrs. J. Seed, skin door mats. Mrs. Taylor (bridegroom's aunt), grandfather clock, &amp;c. Miss E. Taylor, linen table cloth. Miss E. Tyson, oak. tray. Mr. and Mrs. G. Taylor (Blackburn), photo album.</p>
        <p>The Misses L. and B. Taylor (Blackburn), silver flower vase.</p>
        <p>' Mr. and Mrs. P. Taylor, half-dozen custard !glasses. _</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. R. Thompson (New Brighton), silver egg stand. Mr. and Mrs. J. Whitehouse, photogravure. Mr. G. Whitehouse, picture. Mr. and Mrs. H. Whitehouse (Darwen), photo frame. Messrs. and the Misses Wilkinson, silver fruit and flower stand. Miss Elsie Whitfield, glass sugar basin and cream jug. The Stockton Heath Wesleyan Sunday School Teachers and Offi'cials, case of silver tea knives. Members of the Bride's Sunday School Class, clock. Members of Bold-street' Wesleyan Choir, sil- ver cruet. And various presents from the Wallasey Staff of the Prudential Assurance Company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0298" n="art0298">
        <head>• WORKERS' EDUCATION. Last Night's Meeting of the Warring-</head>
        <p>• WORKERS' EDUCATION. Last Night's Meeting of the Warring-</p>
        <p>ton W.E.A-Branch.</p>
        <p>"To arouse in the workers an interest for hiiher education and to direct their attention to the facilities already provided," is only one of the splendid objects of the Workers' Educa- tional Association. The Warrington and Dis- trict branch held its first annual meeting in the Town Hall last night, 34r. J. Moore Murray pre- siding. . _ _</p>
        <p>- In his remarks the Chairman said that if the association only did something in regard to women's work it would' do a great deal to earn the sympathy and support of people in the town; If there was one thing requiring most serious attention it was the welfare of the children of the town. Whilst he saw an immense change, daily in the condition of the children of the , town there was still room for greater change,' and if the association did something to bring about that change it would merit the thanks of the inhabitants of the town.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0299" n="art0299">
        <head>FINANCE.</head>
        <p>FINANCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. H. Sherburn presented the balance sheet, which showed a balance of £9 lOs. He explained that they had to pay ..he out- standing debt of £55 for the teaching xv ork of the association. They were expecting a grant of .210 from the Education Committee, one of ..t3O from the Government, and A:10 from Gilchrist Trustees. _ _</p>
        <p>The report and balance sheet were +nloptel. The officials were re-elected en bloc, with the addition of Mr. R. Williams' name to the vice- presidents. The president is Canon Mor!ey Stevenson. The Chairman intimated that they were going to start adult classes in three centres of the town for women,- in cooking, dressmaking, •ind millinery.</p>
        <p>An applicant for a -Wine lience at Burnley gave as the principal reason that since the pass- ing of the Insurance Act there had been a greater demand' for medicated wines, doctors telling their patients that tbv should get them when they were run down ix health. The chem- ist said that he was losing custom through not being able to supplir medicated wines, people getting other things from the places where they could get the wines. The application, which was opposed by the temperance party, was granted.</p>
        <p>One of the Most picturesque functions in War- rington is the May Queen Festival, held under the auspices of the Warrington Band of Hope Union, and the 12th ceremony of crowning the Queen will take place in the Parr Hall on Thursday next, and the ceremony will be re- peated on Saturday.</p>
        <p>Miss Eva Tanner is the Queen-elect, and we are sure the selection is a popular one. There will be the Dickens Pageant and crowning cere- mony, and the principal Characters, along with the Queen-elect, will be Bumble, the Beadle, A. llamblett; Flora, May Ellis; Fairies, Jessie Davies, E. Warhurst and E. Roscoe. Dances and action songs; as well as other items of en- tertainment, will be contributed.</p>
        <p>On Thursday the Mayor-will preside, and on Saturday Mrs. James Dodds (the president of the union) will occupy the chair. There will be a choir and character procession of 400 voices, and the regal ceremony all through promises to be as attractive, if not more so, than ever.</p>
      </div>
